window.TEACH=window.TEACH||{};
Object.assign(TEACH,{
0:[
{h:"Three books in one",
s:"Revelation is a letter, a prophecy and an apocalypse all at once, and each label changes how you should read it.",
t:["It calls itself an “apocalypse” in its very first word (Revelation 1:1). The Greek word apokalypsis simply means an unveiling. Apocalyptic writing was a style Jewish readers already knew from Daniel and Ezekiel: dramatic visions full of beasts, numbers and cosmic battles, designed to pull back the curtain on what is really going on behind history.",
"It also calls itself a “prophecy” (1:3). In the Bible, prophecy is mainly God speaking to a people in their situation, calling them to faithfulness and giving them hope. Predicting the future is part of it, but only a part.",
"And it is a letter. Revelation 1:4 opens like Paul’s letters: “John to the seven churches that are in Asia.” Chapters 2–3 are seven short letters to real congregations with real struggles. That means the book was first of all understandable to the people who received it, in their own time and place."],
k:"Read Revelation as an unveiling of Jesus, sent as a letter to real churches. It is not a secret code for headlines."},
{h:"A blessing for the reader",
s:"Before a single beast appears, Revelation promises happiness to anyone who reads it and takes it seriously.",
t:["“Blessed is the one who reads aloud the words of this prophecy, and blessed are those who hear, and who keep what is written in it, for the time is near” (Revelation 1:3). Most Christians in the first century could not own a copy. One person read it aloud to the gathered church, and the whole group listened together.",
"Notice the three verbs: read, hear, keep. “Keep” means to obey and hold on to, not just to understand. Revelation does not expect you to crack every puzzle. It expects you to be shaped by it and to stay faithful to Jesus.",
"This is the first of seven “blessed” sayings scattered through the book (1:3; 14:13; 16:15; 19:9; 20:6; 22:7; 22:14). The book is bracketed by blessing, which tells you the tone it wants to leave you with: hope, not fear."],
k:"The goal of reading Revelation is not to be a prediction expert. It is to read, hear and keep, and to be blessed doing it."},
{h:"Four ways to read the book",
s:"Faithful Christians have read Revelation in four main ways. Knowing them will help you make sense of everything you hear about it.",
t:["Preterist (Latin for “past”): most of the book describes events of the first century, such as the pressure from Rome and the fall of Jerusalem in AD 70. It spoke to its first readers about their own world.",
"Historicist: the visions unfold across church history, from the early church through the Middle Ages and on to the end. The seals, trumpets and beasts are matched to successive eras.",
"Futurist: from about chapter 4 onward, the book mostly describes events still to come just before Christ returns. This is the view behind many popular “end times” books and films.",
"Idealist (or symbolic): the visions are not tied to particular dates. They show the repeating, timeless struggle between Christ and evil that every generation of Christians lives in. Many readers combine elements of these views. This app will explain each fairly as you meet the texts, so you can judge for yourself."],
k:"The four views are preterist (past), historicist (history), futurist (future) and idealist (timeless). Most readers borrow from more than one."}
],
1:[
{h:"John on Patmos",
s:"The man who wrote Revelation was an exiled church leader in the middle of a persecution, and he says so himself.",
t:["John calls himself “your brother and partner in the tribulation and the kingdom and the patient endurance that are in Jesus” (Revelation 1:9). He is not writing from a comfortable study. He was on Patmos, a small, rocky island in the Aegean Sea about 60 miles (100 km) from Ephesus, “on account of the word of God and the testimony of Jesus.” Rome sometimes used islands as places of banishment.",
"Early church writers such as Justin Martyr and Irenaeus identified him as John the apostle, Jesus’ close friend. Some scholars suggest a different John, such as “John the elder,” but the tradition behind the apostle is strong and early. The book itself only says “John.”",
"What matters for reading it is that John shares his readers’ suffering. He is not an outsider lecturing them. He is a fellow prisoner of hope. Compare Acts 4:1–20, where Peter and John are threatened for speaking about Jesus and answer that they cannot stop."],
k:"Revelation was written by a persecuted believer to persecuted believers. Keep their situation in mind and the book makes more sense."},
{h:"Caesar versus Christ",
s:"Revelation was written in a world where the emperor was treated as divine, so “Jesus is Lord” was a dangerous sentence.",
t:["In the Roman world, emperors were honored with temples, statues and sacrifices. Citizens were expected to show loyalty by joining in this worship. For Christians, who said that Jesus alone is Lord, this created a constant crisis: refuse and risk trouble, or go along and betray Christ.",
"Nero (emperor 54–68) blamed Christians for the great fire of Rome in AD 64 and killed many of them. Domitian (emperor 81–96) liked to be addressed as “lord and god.” Most scholars date Revelation to around AD 95, near the end of Domitian’s reign, though some argue for the late 60s under Nero.",
"The date matters because it changes what the book is responding to. A date in the 60s places it just before the fall of Jerusalem in AD 70. A date in the 90s places it in a time of growing pressure to worship the emperor. How hard Domitian actually persecuted Christians is debated, but the pressure to compromise was real in either case."],
k:"Whichever date you choose, Revelation is about who deserves worship: Caesar or the Lamb."},
{h:"The Lord’s Day and the seven cities",
s:"John got his vision on a Sunday and sent it to seven cities along a real road.",
t:["“I was in the Spirit on the Lord’s day” (Revelation 1:10). Most understand this as Sunday, the day Christians gathered to celebrate Jesus’ resurrection. A few take it to mean the future “Day of the Lord,” which you will meet on Day 5. Either way, John’s vision happens in the middle of worship.",
"The seven churches (Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea) are listed in an order that makes a loop on a map. A messenger carrying a scroll could have walked this circuit, starting at Ephesus, the nearest port to Patmos, and ending at Laodicea.",
"Seven is the number of completeness, so these real congregations also stand for the whole church. Each letter ends with “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches” (2:7), plural. Every church was meant to overhear every letter."],
k:"The seven letters are about seven real churches and, at the same time, about every church."}
],
2:[
{h:"Rule 1: Let the text explain",
s:"The first place to look for what a symbol means is the book itself, because John often tells you.",
t:["Revelation 1:20 is the model: “As for the mystery of the seven stars that you saw in my right hand, and the seven golden lampstands, the seven stars are the angels of the seven churches, and the seven lampstands are the seven churches.” Jesus himself hands us the decoder.",
"Other cases work the same way. The great dragon is “that ancient serpent, who is called the devil and Satan” (12:9). The incense in the golden bowls is “the prayers of the saints” (5:8). The fine linen of the bride is “the righteous deeds of the saints” (19:8).",
"Before you reach for outside theories, check whether the verse, the paragraph or the chapter has already given you an explanation. Where it hasn’t, the next two rules help."],
k:"Always ask first: does the book tell me what this symbol means?"},
{h:"Rule 2: Find the Old Testament source",
s:"Almost every image in Revelation was borrowed from an earlier part of the Bible, and the source tells you what it means.",
t:["John’s first readers knew the Old Testament well. So when Jesus appears with hair like white wool and a voice like many waters (Revelation 1:14–15), they would think of Daniel 7 and Ezekiel 43. When locusts swarm out of the abyss (9:3), they would think of Joel 1–2. When Babylon falls (chapter 18), they would think of Isaiah and Jeremiah.",
"A good habit: when you meet a strange image, ask “where have I seen this before?” and look at the cross-references in your Bible. The question is not “what does this look like?” but “what did this stand for the last time God used it?”",
"Day 4 goes much deeper on this. For now, remember that Revelation is more like a mosaic of Old Testament pieces than a brand-new invention."],
k:"Revelation recycles Old Testament pictures. Learn the source and you usually learn the meaning."},
{h:"Rule 3: The meaning, not the picture",
s:"Apocalyptic images are meant to be understood, not photographed. A literal reading usually produces a monster instead of a message.",
t:["In Revelation 5:5–6, John hears that “the Lion of the tribe of Judah” has conquered, and then he turns and sees “a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain, with seven horns and with seven eyes.” Nobody thinks Jesus is a seven-horned sheep. Horns picture power and eyes picture knowledge, and the verse tells us the eyes are “the seven spirits of God sent out into all the earth.”",
"Notice also the surprise: what John hears (a Lion) and what he sees (a Lamb) are two pictures of the same person. The Lion conquers by being the slain Lamb. Revelation often does this, so keep your eyes open for twists.",
"Similarly, a sword coming from a mouth (1:16) doesn’t mean a weapon in his teeth. It pictures the power of his word. Ask what the image communicates, then check that against the rest of Scripture."],
k:"Read symbols for what they mean. A lamb with seven eyes is a picture of Jesus’ power and knowledge, not an animal."},
{h:"Number basics",
s:"Numbers in Revelation carry meaning. A handful of them keep returning, and you can learn them now.",
t:["Seven stands for completeness or fullness: seven churches, seven seals, seven trumpets, seven bowls. Twelve stands for God’s people (twelve tribes of Israel, twelve apostles). Four stands for the created world (four corners of the earth, four winds).",
"Three and a half is half of seven, and it comes from Daniel’s “a time, times, and half a time” (Daniel 7:25). In Revelation it appears as 42 months and 1,260 days (11:2–3; 12:6). It speaks of a limited period when evil seems to have the upper hand, but only for a time.",
"Ten suggests a full, human-sized completeness, such as the ten horns. A thousand suggests a very large number, as in Psalm 50:10, where God owns the cattle on a thousand hills. The number 666 (13:18) is called “the number of man,” and it is built from sixes, one short of the perfect seven, which many take to mean “human, falling short.” Many scholars think numbers are sometimes literal and sometimes symbolic, so we will flag which when we meet them."],
k:"Seven is complete, twelve is God’s people, four is the earth, and three and a half is a limited time of trouble."}
],
3:[
{h:"The Old Testament in Revelation’s DNA",
s:"Revelation quotes almost no one directly, yet nearly every verse echoes something in the Old Testament.",
t:["Scholars count hundreds of allusions in Revelation to the Old Testament. John rarely says “as it is written.” Instead, he weaves words, scenes and phrases from earlier books into his visions the way a musician reuses a melody.",
"The biggest sources are Daniel, Ezekiel, Isaiah and Zechariah. The plagues of the trumpets and bowls echo Exodus. The tree of life and the serpent come from Genesis 2–3. The rod of iron comes from Psalm 2 (Revelation 2:27; 12:5; 19:15). The lampstands and olive trees come from Zechariah 4.",
"This is not decoration. By reusing those images, John tells his readers that the story God began long ago is reaching its climax in Jesus."],
k:"Revelation is the Old Testament’s story reaching its end. Knowing the Old Testament makes the book far easier."},
{h:"One throne, many books",
s:"Put Daniel, Ezekiel and Revelation next to each other and you can watch the same throne room appear three times.",
t:["Daniel 7:9 describes thrones, a fiery throne with wheels, and a river of fire. Ezekiel 1 describes living creatures with eyes, wheels within wheels, a crystal expanse, and a throne with the likeness of a rainbow-like brightness all around it (Ezekiel 1:26–28).",
"Revelation 4 gathers these together: a throne in heaven, a rainbow around it (4:3), a sea of glass like crystal (4:6), four living creatures covered with eyes (4:6–8). Isaiah 6 adds the cry “Holy, holy, holy,” which the creatures in Revelation 4:8 also sing.",
"John isn’t copying. He adapts the images, and there are differences. Ezekiel’s creatures each have four faces, but Revelation’s each have one. This shows that the point is the shared message: the one God is on the throne, whether you stand in Babylon with Daniel, by a river with Ezekiel, or on Patmos with John."],
k:"The same throne appears in Daniel, Ezekiel, Isaiah and Revelation. God has not changed, and he is in charge."},
{h:"Why the Old Testament is the key",
s:"Without the Old Testament, Revelation sounds like wild science fiction. With it, the book reads like a conversation with Scripture.",
t:["Many popular interpretations of Revelation treat it as a puzzle to be solved with newspapers. The book’s own method points somewhere else: to the Old Testament. When Revelation talks about Babylon, an exodus, a new Jerusalem or a Lamb, it is picking up threads the Old Testament has been laying for a thousand years.",
"John also reads the Old Testament in light of Jesus. Pictures that once pointed to Israel’s kings, priests and temple now find their meaning in him. This is why a Lion can turn out to be a Lamb.",
"A practical tip for this app: whenever a lesson says “see Daniel” or “compare Isaiah,” tap the reference and read the passage. Spending two minutes with the source will teach you more than ten minutes of speculation."],
k:"Use the Old Testament as your dictionary for Revelation. Tap the references as you go."}
],
4:[
{h:"Already and not yet",
s:"The New Testament says God’s kingdom has begun but is not finished. Holding both ideas together unlocks how the whole Bible thinks about time.",
t:["Jewish writers divided history into two stretches: “this age,” full of sin and death, and “the age to come,” when God sets everything right. The surprise of the gospel is that Jesus’ resurrection means the age to come has already started inside this age.",
"Paul explains in 1 Corinthians 15:20–28: Christ has risen as the “firstfruits,” the first part of the harvest. The rest of the harvest follows “at his coming.” Then comes “the end,” when Christ hands the kingdom to the Father after destroying every enemy, with death the last one to go.",
"A helpful picture is a rugby match decided in the 70th minute, but the final whistle hasn’t blown yet. The result is settled; the game is still being played. Isaiah 2:1–4 shows the finished picture, with nations streaming to God’s mountain and swords beaten into plowshares. We have a down payment of that now and the full thing later."],
k:"The kingdom has already begun in Jesus and will be completed when he returns. Revelation lives in the space between."},
{h:"The Day of the Lord",
s:"Through the prophets, the Day of the Lord is when God personally steps in to judge evil and rescue his people. It is one event with two sides.",
t:["Joel 2:28–32 describes it: the sun turning dark, the moon to blood, and yet, “everyone who calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved.” The same day is judgment for those who oppose God and deliverance for those who trust him.",
"The prophets also warned that the day is no guarantee of safety for people who only claim God’s name. Amos 5:18–20 tells Israel that for those who live unjustly, the day will be darkness, not light.",
"This matters because Revelation’s judgments and rescues are Day-of-the-Lord language. When you see plagues and a great harvest side by side later in the book, you are watching one day from two angles: the rescue of the faithful and the removal of what ruins creation."],
k:"The Day of the Lord means judgment on evil and rescue for God’s people at the same time."},
{h:"The last days have begun",
s:"The Bible’s “last days” do not start in the future. They began with Jesus.",
t:["On the day of Pentecost, Peter quotes Joel’s promise of the Spirit and says, in effect, “this is that” (Acts 2:16–17). He tells the crowd that the “last days” were already happening.",
"Hebrews 1:2 says the same: “in these last days he has spoken to us by his Son.” The writer of Hebrews is not describing some distant time. He means the era that started when Jesus came.",
"This reshapes how we read Revelation. If the “last days” stretch from Jesus’ resurrection to his return, then the book is speaking to every generation of believers living in them, as well as pointing to the climax at the end."],
k:"We are already in the “last days.” They started at Jesus’ resurrection and finish when he returns."}
],
5:[
{h:"Three questions",
s:"Matthew 24 starts because the disciples ask Jesus something big, and the answer depends on what they were really asking.",
t:["Jesus has just told them the magnificent temple will be completely torn down (Matthew 24:1–2). Stunned, they ask privately: “Tell us, when will these things be, and what will be the sign of your coming and of the close of the age?” (24:3).",
"Depending on how you read it, that is either two questions (when will the temple fall, and what about your return?) or three. Luke’s version (21:7) only asks about the temple’s destruction, which is one reason many readers think Jesus is answering in two layers.",
"Keep this in mind as you read: some of what Jesus says may be about the near event (the temple’s fall) and some about the end of the age. Where one stops and the other starts is exactly what readers disagree about."],
k:"The disciples asked about the temple, Jesus’ coming and the end of the age. Sorting which answer goes with which question is the whole challenge of Matthew 24."},
{h:"Birth pains, not the end",
s:"Jesus lists wars, famines and earthquakes, then immediately says they do not mean the end has arrived.",
t:["“You will hear of wars and rumors of wars. See that you are not alarmed, for this must take place, but the end is not yet” (Matthew 24:6). Then he adds that nation will rise against nation, and there will be famines and earthquakes, “but all these are but the beginning of the birth pains” (24:7–8).",
"Birth pains are an excellent picture. They are real and painful, they get stronger and closer together, but they are not the birth. They point toward something being born.",
"The practical point: Jesus is lowering the alarm, not raising it. Every generation has seen wars and disasters, and some have wrongly decided the end was here. Jesus warns about that mistake, speaks of persecution and false prophets (24:9–13), and says the end comes only after the gospel has reached all nations (24:14)."],
k:"Wars, famines and earthquakes are birth pains. They do not announce the end by themselves."},
{h:"The abomination of desolation and AD 70",
s:"Jesus points back to Daniel and says people in Judea will see something horrific, and they should run.",
t:["“When you see the abomination of desolation spoken of by the prophet Daniel, standing in the holy place... then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains” (Matthew 24:15–16). Daniel used this phrase for Antiochus’s desecration of the temple in 167 BC (Daniel 11:31; 12:11), which you saw on Day 10.",
"Luke’s version makes it clearer: “When you see Jerusalem surrounded by armies, then know that its desolation has come near” (Luke 21:20). In AD 66–70, Jewish rebels fought Rome. The Roman general Titus besieged Jerusalem, and in AD 70 the city and its temple were destroyed. Early Christian writers report that believers heeded Jesus’ warning and fled.",
"Many think this is what Jesus meant. Others see AD 70 as a preview of a larger future event. Both agree that Jesus used Daniel’s language to warn that the temple’s fall was coming, and it did."],
k:"Jesus’ warning about the abomination of desolation was literally life-saving for his followers in AD 70, and echoes Daniel."},
{h:"“This generation will not pass away”",
s:"Matthew 24:34 is one of the most debated verses in the Bible. Here are the four main readings.",
t:["The verse says: “Truly, I say to you, this generation will not pass away until all these things take place” (Matthew 24:34). The next line says “heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away.”",
"View 1: “generation” means the people alive when Jesus spoke, and “these things” are the events of AD 70. View 2: “generation” means the Jewish people or the human race, who will not disappear before it all happens. View 3: it means the generation that sees the signs begin, in the future. View 4: it means “this kind of people,” an evil, unbelieving generation that continues until the end.",
"You do not have to pick one today. What the four views share is that Jesus’ words are reliable, and the point of the passage is readiness, not calculation."],
k:"There are four readings of “this generation.” All of them agree that Jesus’ word will stand."}
],
6:[
{h:"Luke names the armies",
s:"Luke tells the same story for Gentile readers and makes explicit what Matthew leaves in Daniel’s code.",
t:["Where Matthew and Mark say “the abomination of desolation,” Luke says “when you see Jerusalem surrounded by armies, then know that its desolation has come near” (Luke 21:20). He also adds that people will “fall by the edge of the sword and be led captive among all nations, and Jerusalem will be trampled underfoot by the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled” (21:24).",
"After that, Luke describes signs in sun, moon and stars and “the Son of Man coming in a cloud with power and great glory” (21:25–27), echoing Daniel 7:13. Then comes one of the most encouraging lines: “Straighten up and raise your heads, because your redemption is drawing near” (21:28).",
"Notice how Luke distinguishes the fall of Jerusalem from the coming of the Son of Man. This is one of the main reasons many readers see a near event (AD 70) and a far event (Jesus’ return) in the discourse."],
k:"Luke explicitly connects Jesus’ warning to armies around Jerusalem, then looks ahead to the Son of Man coming on the clouds."},
{h:"No one knows the day or hour",
s:"Jesus says plainly that nobody can know the date of his return, which should end every date-setting scheme.",
t:["“But concerning that day and hour no one knows, not even the angels of heaven, nor the Son, but the Father only” (Matthew 24:36). Even Jesus, in his human life, did not claim to know. So if anyone offers you a date, you have Jesus’ own word against them.",
"He compares it with the days of Noah (24:37–39). People were eating, drinking and marrying, living normal life, and then the flood came without warning. The point is not that the world is especially wicked but that it is unprepared. The one taken and the other left (24:40–41) pictures a sudden separation, and whether those taken are rescued or swept away in judgment is read both ways.",
"His instruction is simple: “Therefore, stay awake” (24:42). People throughout history have named dates for the end, and every one of them has been wrong."],
k:"No one knows the day or hour. Jesus’ command is to be ready, not to guess."},
{h:"Parables of readiness",
s:"Jesus follows the warning with four stories about what readiness actually looks like.",
t:["The faithful servant (Matthew 24:45–51) keeps doing his job while the master is away, and the wicked servant abuses his fellow servants because he assumes the master is delayed. Readiness means faithful work, not speculation.",
"The ten virgins (25:1–13) all fall asleep, but only five brought extra oil. Being ready means being prepared for the long wait. The talents (25:14–30) say that each person is given something and is expected to use it for the master. The sheep and the goats (25:31–46) show that the final judgment is bound up with how people treated “the least of these,” Jesus’ brothers and sisters.",
"Together they tell you that Jesus’ return is both certain and uncertain in timing, and the right response is steady obedience, preparation and love. These themes carry straight into Revelation, such as Jesus’ promise “Behold, I am coming soon... to repay each one for what he has done” (Revelation 22:12)."],
k:"Be ready by being faithful: do your work, stay prepared, and care for others."}
]
});
Object.assign(TEACH,{
7:[
{h:"The statue and the stone",
s:"A pagan king dreams of a glittering statue made of four metals, and God uses it to show how human empires end.",
t:["In Daniel 2, King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon dreams of a statue with a head of gold, chest and arms of silver, belly and thighs of bronze, legs of iron, and feet of iron mixed with clay (Daniel 2:31–33). Daniel says the golden head is Babylon itself (2:38). Three more kingdoms follow, each weaker in value but harder in strength. Traditionally they are read as Medo-Persia, Greece and Rome, though some scholars count Media and Persia separately.",
"Then a stone “cut out by no human hand” strikes the feet and the whole statue crumbles like chaff, while the stone becomes “a great mountain and filled the whole earth” (2:34–35). Daniel explains that God will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed (2:44).",
"The message is simple. Human power looks magnificent and permanent, but it is brittle. God’s kingdom starts small and wins. Revelation picks up the same hope."],
k:"Empires look like gold and iron, but God’s kingdom is the stone that fills the earth."},
{h:"Heaven’s view: the four beasts",
s:"Daniel 7 shows the same history as Daniel 2, but from God’s point of view, and it looks very different.",
t:["Where Nebuchadnezzar saw a beautiful statue, Daniel sees four savage beasts climbing out of a stormy sea: a lion with eagle’s wings, a bear, a leopard with four wings and four heads, and a terrifying fourth beast with iron teeth and ten horns (Daniel 7:2–8). The sea often pictures chaos and the restless nations. The beasts are explained as kings and kingdoms (7:17, 23).",
"This tells you something about the whole Bible’s perspective. Political power may look glorious from the human side. From heaven’s side it often looks like a beast devouring people.",
"Keep these beasts in mind. In Revelation 13, John sees a beast rise from the sea with features of a leopard, a bear and a lion (13:2), which is Daniel’s list put together into one."],
k:"Daniel 2 shows empires as glorious. Daniel 7 shows them as beasts. Revelation 13 draws on that picture."},
{h:"The Ancient of Days and the Son of Man",
s:"The center of Daniel 7 is a courtroom scene in heaven, with a figure who will become one of Jesus’ favorite titles for himself.",
t:["“As I looked, thrones were placed, and the Ancient of Days took his seat; his clothing was white as snow, and the hair of his head like pure wool” (Daniel 7:9). The court sits, books are opened, and the fourth beast is destroyed (7:10–11).",
"Then: “with the clouds of heaven there came one like a son of man... and to him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve him” (7:13–14). “Son of man” just means a human figure, in contrast to the beasts. He comes on the clouds, which in the Old Testament is usually God’s mode of travel.",
"Jesus called himself “Son of Man” more than any other title, and before the high priest he quoted this exact passage (Mark 14:62). The priest tore his robes because he understood perfectly well what Jesus was claiming."],
k:"The Son of Man in Daniel 7 receives an everlasting kingdom from God. Jesus says that is him."},
{h:"The little horn and “time, times, and half a time”",
s:"Daniel 7 describes a final persecutor who speaks arrogantly and wears down God’s people for a limited period.",
t:["Among the fourth beast’s ten horns, a small one rises, with eyes like a man’s and a mouth speaking great things (Daniel 7:8). It makes war on the saints (7:21), speaks against the Most High and tries to change times and laws. God’s people are given into his hand “for a time, times, and half a time” (7:25), which is three and a half years.",
"Who is it? Readers have said Antiochus IV (see Day 10), a Roman emperor, or a future antichrist figure. Many see Antiochus as a first example that points to later and greater ones. The text itself does not give a name.",
"What matters for Revelation is the pattern: an arrogant ruler, persecution of the saints, a limited time, and God’s court ruling against him. Revelation reuses this as 42 months and 1,260 days (Revelation 11:2–3; 12:6, 14; 13:5)."],
k:"The little horn is a persecutor whose time is limited. Revelation keeps this timeline and these themes."},
{h:"The saints receive the kingdom",
s:"Both Daniel visions end in the same place, and it is not with the empires winning.",
t:["Daniel 2 ends with the stone filling the earth. Daniel 7 ends with God’s people on top: “the saints of the Most High shall receive the kingdom and possess the kingdom forever” (Daniel 7:18), and again, “the kingdom and the dominion... shall be given to the people of the saints of the Most High” (7:27).",
"Notice the surprise. The kingdom goes not to a super-king but to the persecuted people who belong to the Son of Man. They do not only survive. They reign with him.",
"Revelation says the same thing: Jesus has made believers “a kingdom and priests to our God, and they shall reign on the earth” (Revelation 5:10), and they “came to life and reigned with Christ” (20:4). If you remember one line from Daniel for this study, remember this: evil empires rise and fall, but God’s people inherit the kingdom."],
k:"Daniel’s hope, and Revelation’s, is that God’s suffering people will receive and share the everlasting kingdom."}
],
8:[
{h:"Daniel prays and Gabriel answers",
s:"The seventy weeks begin with a man who reads Scripture, realizes God’s promise is due, and responds with prayer.",
t:["Daniel has been reading Jeremiah, who said that Jerusalem would lie desolate for seventy years (Daniel 9:2; Jeremiah 25:11–12; 29:10). The seventy years are almost up. His response is not curiosity but confession: “we have sinned and done wrong” (9:5). He includes himself in the guilt even though the Bible records no failing for him.",
"His prayer rests on God’s character, not merit: “we do not present our pleas before you because of our righteousness, but because of your great mercy” (9:18). While he is still praying, Gabriel flies in at the time of the evening offering with an answer (9:21–23). Gabriel is also the angel who later brings news to Zechariah and Mary (Luke 1).",
"A habit to learn from this: Daniel let prophecy drive him to his knees rather than to a timetable. The seventy weeks that follow are God’s answer to a prayer."],
k:"Prophecy was meant to lead Daniel to prayer. Read the seventy weeks in the same spirit."},
{h:"Seventy “sevens”",
s:"Gabriel explains that the seventy years are only the first layer. A much longer period of seventy sevens is ahead.",
t:["The Hebrew word used here means “sevens.” Often it is understood as sevens of years, since Israel had Sabbath years every seventh year and the exile had made up for seventy missed Sabbath years (Leviticus 26:34–35; 2 Chronicles 36:21). Seventy sevens of years is 490 years.",
"The period is split into three sections: seven sevens (49 years), then sixty-two sevens (434 years), then one final seven (Daniel 9:25–27). The period is “decreed about your people and your holy city,” so it concerns Israel and Jerusalem.",
"Some interpreters treat the numbers as exact calendar years, and others read them as symbolic, with seventy sevens meaning a complete, God-determined period. Both agree that God holds the calendar."],
k:"Seventy sevens is usually read as 490 years, split into 7, 62 and 1 sevens, or as a symbolic complete period."},
{h:"Six goals for the period",
s:"Gabriel lists six things God will accomplish by the end of the seventy sevens. Three remove evil and three bring good.",
t:["Daniel 9:24: “to finish the transgression, to put an end to sin, and to atone for iniquity, to bring in everlasting righteousness, to seal both vision and prophet, and to anoint a most holy place.” Notice the pattern: three about dealing with sin, three about bringing in God’s righteous order.",
"Most Christians read the first three as accomplished through Jesus’ death (see Hebrews 9:26). Others see all six as finally completed only when Christ returns, and some see a now-and-not-yet fulfillment.",
"Whatever your reading, the passage promises that God has a plan to deal with sin fully, restore righteousness, and bring the temple vision to completion. Revelation ends exactly there: sin gone, righteousness at home, God dwelling with his people."],
k:"The six goals describe a complete solution to sin and the restoring of God’s order. Revelation 21–22 shows it finished."},
{h:"The anointed one cut off",
s:"The most mysterious part: an anointed one is killed, a city and sanctuary are destroyed, and someone makes things desolate.",
t:["“After the sixty-two sevens, an anointed one shall be cut off and shall have nothing. And the people of the prince who is to come shall destroy the city and the sanctuary” (Daniel 9:26). “Anointed one” is the word “Messiah.” Many Christians see Jesus here, put to death, with the Roman armies destroying Jerusalem in AD 70.",
"Then verse 27: “he shall make a strong covenant with many for one seven, and for half of the seven he shall put an end to sacrifice and offering. And on the wing of abominations shall come one who makes desolate.” Who is “he”? Some say the Messiah, whose death ended the need for sacrifice. Others say a future ruler who breaks a covenant. Others say Antiochus IV.",
"The Hebrew is compact and can be read more than one way, which is why thoughtful readers disagree. The safest summary is: a Messiah is killed, Jerusalem falls, and desolation follows."],
k:"A Messiah is cut off, the city and temple fall, and desolation follows. Who “he” is in verse 27 is debated."},
{h:"Where do the sevens start?",
s:"The 490 years can only be counted from a starting point, and the Bible gives more than one candidate.",
t:["Daniel 9:25 says the count begins “from the going out of the word to restore and build Jerusalem.” Candidates are Cyrus’s decree in 538 BC (Ezra 1), Artaxerxes’ decree to Ezra in 458/457 BC (Ezra 7:11–26), and the king’s permission to Nehemiah in about 445 BC (Nehemiah 2).",
"Counting 483 years (7 plus 62 sevens) from 457 BC lands around AD 27, near the time Jesus began his ministry. Counting from 445 BC, with a different year-length, others reach AD 32–33. Starting from Cyrus gives a different result, and critical scholars end the count in the 160s BC at Antiochus.",
"That the math shifts depending on the starting point is exactly why this passage has produced so many charts. It is better to hold the big picture firmly (God’s plan, a Messiah, a destroyed city, final victory) and the dates loosely."],
k:"Different starting decrees give different timelines. Hold the big picture firmly and the exact dates loosely."}
],
9:[
{h:"The ram and the goat",
s:"One of Daniel’s visions comes with its own explanation, which makes it a useful test case for how prophecy can be specific.",
t:["Daniel 8 shows a two-horned ram pushing in every direction, then a goat with one great horn racing across the earth without touching the ground and shattering the ram (8:3–7). The angel Gabriel explains: “The ram that you saw with the two horns are the kings of Media and Persia. And the goat is the king of Greece” (8:20–21).",
"The great horn is Greece’s first king, usually understood as Alexander the Great. It breaks at its height and is replaced by four horns, matching the four kingdoms into which his empire split after his death (8:8, 22).",
"A little horn then grows out of one of these (8:9) and attacks the “host of heaven,” the daily offering and the sanctuary. This is the figure who will be named on the next point. Daniel 8 shows that biblical prophecy can be remarkably concrete when God wants it to be."],
k:"Daniel 8 tells you who the ram and goat are: Persia and Greece. Prophecy can be precise."},
{h:"Antiochus IV and the abomination",
s:"A real Greek king desecrated the Jerusalem temple in 167 BC, and that event became the pattern for later “abominations.”",
t:["Antiochus IV, called Epiphanes, ruled the Seleucid empire from 175 to 164 BC. He tried to force Jews to abandon their faith: banning circumcision, Sabbath and Torah, executing those who refused, and in December 167 BC setting up an altar to Zeus on the temple altar (1 Maccabees 1:41–64).",
"This is the “transgression that makes desolate” in Daniel 8:13, and is usually linked to the “abomination” in 9:27, 11:31 and 12:11. A Jewish revolt led by the Maccabees recaptured the temple in 164 BC and rededicated it. The Jewish festival of Hanukkah remembers that.",
"Antiochus matters for Revelation because Jesus points back to Daniel’s abomination when talking about the future (Matthew 24:15). Many interpreters see Antiochus as the first pattern of the arrogant persecutor, with later and greater ones to follow."],
k:"Antiochus IV desecrated the temple in 167 BC and became the template for later attackers of God’s people."},
{h:"Michael and the unseen conflict",
s:"Daniel 10 offers a rare glimpse behind the scenes: earthly politics happen in the middle of an unseen spiritual struggle.",
t:["Daniel has been mourning and praying for three weeks. An angel appears and says, “from the first day... your words have been heard,” but “the prince of the kingdom of Persia withstood me twenty-one days, but Michael, one of the chief princes, came to help me” (Daniel 10:12–13).",
"Michael is called “your prince” (10:21) and “the great prince who has charge of your people” (12:1). In Revelation 12:7, Michael and his angels fight the dragon.",
"The Bible gives very few details, and it warns against curiosity for its own sake. The main lesson is simple: God’s people are not alone, the real conflict is bigger than what we see, and prayer matters. Daniel’s prayer was heard on day one, even though the answer took three weeks."],
k:"Behind earthly events there is a spiritual conflict, and God’s people are not left to face it alone."},
{h:"Resurrection in the Old Testament",
s:"Daniel 12:2 is the clearest Old Testament promise that dead people will rise.",
t:["“And many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt” (Daniel 12:2). Dying is called “sleeping in the dust,” and waking up is bodily. Earlier hints appear in Isaiah 26:19 and Job 19:25–26.",
"The next verse says, “those who are wise shall shine like the brightness of the sky above” (12:3). In the Old Testament, hope for resurrection grew mainly in times of persecution, when death seemed to win. If God’s faithful ones are killed, God must raise them.",
"Jesus picks this up in John 5:28–29, and Revelation 20:11–15 shows the dead standing before God. This is the background for why martyrs in Revelation are not defeated: death is not the end of the story."],
k:"Daniel 12:2 promises bodily resurrection. That is why faithful suffering is never wasted."},
{h:"Three and a half years in Revelation",
s:"Daniel’s “time, times, and half a time” becomes one of Revelation’s most repeated time markers, under three different names.",
t:["Daniel 12:7 says the events will last “a time, times, and half a time,” which is one year, two years and half a year, or three and a half years. Revelation uses the same period in three forms: 42 months (11:2; 13:5), 1,260 days (11:3; 12:6), and “a time, and times, and half a time” (12:14). 42 months of 30 days equal 1,260 days, so these are the same period.",
"Some take it literally. Others treat it as a symbolic number: half of seven, an incomplete and limited time. In history, Antiochus’s persecution lasted about three years, and Elijah’s drought lasted three and a half (Luke 4:25; James 5:17).",
"What every reading agrees on is that the period is bounded. Evil gets a limited window, God sets the limit, and the story turns after it."],
k:"42 months, 1,260 days, and “a time, times, and half a time” are one period: bounded and brief."}
],
10:[
{h:"One figure, two Daniel pictures",
s:"John’s first view of the risen Jesus merges two figures from Daniel 7 that readers had assumed were separate.",
t:["“Among the lampstands was one like a son of man... his head and his hair were white like white wool, like snow” (Revelation 1:13–14). The first part is the “one like a son of man” from Daniel 7:13. The white hair belongs to the Ancient of Days in Daniel 7:9. John gives both descriptions to one person.",
"That is a staggering claim. A first-century Jew reading this would see that Jesus is not merely a human figure approaching God’s throne. He shares God’s own identity and appearance.",
"John, who had once leaned on Jesus at the supper, falls at his feet “as though dead” (1:17). He knows exactly who this is, and yet is overwhelmed by his glory."],
k:"The Son of Man and the Ancient of Days appear as one person: the risen Jesus."},
{h:"What each feature says",
s:"Every detail of the vision is chosen, and most come from the Old Testament.",
t:["White hair: eternity and wisdom (Daniel 7:9). Eyes like a flame of fire: he sees through everything, and later he says, “I am he who searches mind and heart” (Revelation 2:23). Feet like burnished bronze: strength and judgment, also from Daniel 10:6. Voice like many waters: majesty (Ezekiel 43:2). A sharp two-edged sword from his mouth: the power of his word to judge and to cut (Isaiah 11:4; Hebrews 4:12). Face like the sun at full strength: glory, as at the transfiguration (Matthew 17:2).",
"In his right hand are seven stars, the churches’ angels, which shows he holds them. He walks among the lampstands (Revelation 2:1), so he is present with his churches, not far away.",
"This is the Jesus the seven churches need before they hear a word of praise or rebuke: all-seeing, all-powerful, and close."],
k:"The features of the vision all point to his glory, his knowledge and his authority, and he is among his churches."},
{h:"The First and the Last",
s:"This title is the Old Testament’s way of saying only one God exists, and Revelation applies it to Jesus.",
t:["In Isaiah, God says, “I am the first and I am the last; besides me there is no god” (Isaiah 44:6; compare 48:12). In Revelation 1:17, Jesus says, “Fear not, I am the first and the last.” Revelation 1:8 gives “Alpha and Omega” to “the Lord God,” and 22:13 gives it to Jesus.",
"A Jewish believer like John, who would never worship a creature, still gives Jesus this title without hesitating. Revelation later shows the Lamb receiving the same worship as the one on the throne (5:13).",
"For persecuted believers, this means Jesus is not one more power in a crowded universe. He bookends all of history, and nothing falls outside his reach."],
k:"“First and Last” is a title for the one true God, and Jesus carries it."},
{h:"The keys of Death and Hades",
s:"The one who stands before John has already been where every person is afraid to go, and came back with the keys.",
t:["“I died, and behold I am alive forevermore, and I have the keys of Death and Hades” (Revelation 1:18). Keys mean authority: the power to open and shut (Isaiah 22:22; Matthew 16:19). Hades is the realm of the dead.",
"Caesar claimed divine honor, but Caesar died. Jesus is the only ruler who passed through death and walked back out. His resurrection is his credential.",
"For Christians threatened with execution, this changes what the worst case means. Death is no longer a locked door that only closes. Jesus holds the key, and Revelation 20:13–14 shows Death and Hades themselves thrown into the lake of fire."],
k:"Jesus holds the keys to death. Believers can face the worst the empire can do."},
{h:"“Fear not” and the explanation",
s:"The first thing Jesus says to a terrified John is comfort, and his last words in the vision give a decoder.",
t:["Jesus puts his right hand on John and says, “Fear not” (Revelation 1:17). He identifies himself, then tells John to write what he has seen, what is now, and what is to take place after this (1:19). Some readers use this as an outline for the book.",
"Then comes the explanation: the seven stars are “the angels of the seven churches” and the lampstands are the seven churches (1:20). The Greek word for angel, angelos, can mean “messenger,” so these may be heavenly guardians or the churches’ human messengers. Either way, Jesus is the one who holds them.",
"Notice the order: comfort first, commission second. Before the book gives any warning or judgment, Jesus makes sure his people know he is with them."],
k:"Revelation begins with Jesus’ comfort. He is holding his churches and walking among them."}
],
11:[
{h:"The shape of every letter",
s:"The seven letters follow a pattern, and noticing where a letter breaks the pattern tells you something.",
t:["Most letters have these parts: Jesus’ title (taken from chapter 1), “I know your works,” praise, correction, a call to repent, “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches,” and a promise “to the one who conquers.”",
"Smyrna and Philadelphia receive no correction. Laodicea receives no praise. The other four mix both. Every letter ends by addressing “the churches,” plural, so each church was meant to hear what was said to the others.",
"“Conquer” in Revelation is not about perfection. Later we are told that believers “conquered him by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony” (12:11). It means staying loyal to Jesus under pressure."],
k:"Each letter follows a pattern, and every church is meant to listen to all seven."},
{h:"Ephesus: right belief, lost love",
s:"Ephesus did almost everything right except the thing Jesus cares about most.",
t:["Ephesus was a major port and home of the vast Temple of Artemis (Acts 19:23–41). Jesus commends the church’s hard work, endurance, testing of false apostles, and hatred of the works of the Nicolaitans, a group that seems to have encouraged compromise with idolatry (Revelation 2:2–6).",
"But: “you have abandoned the love you had at first” (2:4). The love in question is love for Christ and for one another. You can win the argument and lose the relationship.",
"The remedy has three steps, all verbs: remember where you have fallen, repent, and do the works you did at first (2:5). The warning is serious: otherwise Jesus will remove their lampstand. A church can be right about everything and still stop shining."],
k:"Truth without love is not enough. Remember, repent and return to the first love."},
{h:"Smyrna: poor, yet rich",
s:"Smyrna is the first of two churches Jesus does not criticize, and he does not promise them an easy time.",
t:["Smyrna (modern Izmir) was a proud, wealthy city with strong loyalty to Rome. The church was poor and slandered, and Jesus warns it is about to suffer, with some thrown into prison (Revelation 2:8–10). His words, “I know your poverty (but you are rich),” turn the city’s values upside down.",
"“Ten days” of testing is a short, limited period, as in Daniel 1:12–15. The call is, “Be faithful unto death, and I will give you the crown of life.” This is not a wreath for winning a race. It is life itself.",
"Jesus introduces himself to Smyrna as “the first and the last, who died and came to life.” That is exactly right for people facing death. Bishop Polycarp of Smyrna was executed around AD 155, reportedly saying he had served Christ 86 years and would not now deny him."],
k:"Smyrna’s riches were invisible. Jesus promises no easy road, but the crown of life."},
{h:"The “synagogue of Satan”",
s:"This phrase has been twisted into something Jesus never meant. Here is what it actually means.",
t:["Jesus says, “I know the slander of those who say they are Jews and are not, but are a synagogue of Satan” (Revelation 2:9). The conflict was local. Some in Smyrna’s Jewish community apparently slandered and informed against Christians, which could expose them to Roman authorities.",
"Jesus, John and nearly all first believers were Jewish. Paul says elsewhere that real belonging to God’s people is about the heart (Romans 2:28–29; 9:6). Jesus is rebuking particular slanderers, not a people.",
"Using this verse to condemn Jews as a group is a tragic misuse that has caused real harm. The right reading keeps it to its original setting: a specific local dispute."],
k:"This verse concerns a local conflict. It is not a verdict on the Jewish people."},
{h:"Promises from Eden to the end",
s:"Each letter ends with a promise, and these two connect to the beginning and end of the Bible.",
t:["To Ephesus: “I will grant to eat of the tree of life, which is in the paradise of God” (Revelation 2:7). That tree was lost in Genesis 3 and returns in Revelation 22:2, 14. The way back to Eden is reopened.",
"To Smyrna: “The one who conquers will not be hurt by the second death” (2:11). Revelation later defines this as the lake of fire, final separation from God (20:14–15; 21:8). A believer might die the first death physically, but never the second.",
"Notice how the promises answer the situation. Ephesus needs life and love restored. Smyrna, facing death, receives a promise beyond death."],
k:"The promises point from Eden’s tree of life to a death that cannot touch believers."}
],
12:[
{h:"Pergamum: where Satan’s throne is",
s:"Pergamum faced the hardest pressure of any of the seven, and Jesus knows exactly where they live.",
t:["Jesus says to Pergamum, “I know where you dwell, where Satan’s throne is” (Revelation 2:13). Pergamum had a huge altar to Zeus on its hill, temples to Athena and Asclepius, and one of the first temples in Asia dedicated to the emperor. Scholars debate which one Jesus means. All suggest intense pressure to worship something other than Christ.",
"The church held fast even when “Antipas my faithful witness” was killed there. Jesus introduces himself as the one with the sharp two-edged sword (2:12), and warns that unless they repent he will come and fight with it (2:16).",
"The lesson is that faithfulness in a hostile place is noticed by Jesus personally, by name."],
k:"Faithful even where evil is loudest. Jesus knows where you live."},
{h:"The teaching of Balaam",
s:"The danger in Pergamum was not only persecution but blending in, and Jesus uses a story from Numbers to explain.",
t:["Balaam, in Numbers 22–24, was hired to curse Israel but could only bless it. Later, Numbers 31:16 says he advised the Moabites to lure Israel into worshiping Baal of Peor, with feasting and sexual immorality (Numbers 25). Jesus says some in Pergamum hold this teaching: eating food sacrificed to idols and practicing sexual immorality (Revelation 2:14).",
"In a city of trade guilds and temple banquets, joining in was the easy way to keep jobs and friends. Paul deals with the same issue in 1 Corinthians 8–10. The Nicolaitans (Revelation 2:15) look like another version of the same compromise.",
"Jesus’ response: repent. Otherwise he will “war against them with the sword of my mouth” (2:16). He cares more about faithfulness than about comfort."],
k:"Compromise with idolatry is as dangerous as open persecution. Jesus calls for repentance."},
{h:"Thyatira: tolerating Jezebel",
s:"Thyatira was a church doing many things well, but it tolerated something it should not have.",
t:["Thyatira was a modest city famous for trade guilds. Lydia, the seller of purple, came from there (Acts 16:14). Guild membership often meant feasts to the guild’s patron god, so faithful Christians faced an economic as well as a religious problem.",
"Jesus praises their love, faith, service and patient endurance, and says their latest works exceed the first (Revelation 2:19). But they tolerate “that woman Jezebel,” who calls herself a prophet and teaches people to commit sexual immorality and eat food sacrificed to idols (2:20). The name recalls Ahab’s queen, who promoted Baal worship (1 Kings 16:31; 21).",
"She has had time to repent and refuses (2:21). The judgment is serious, so that “all the churches will know that I am he who searches mind and heart” (2:23). Tolerating harmful teaching is sometimes treated as kindness, but Jesus counts it as a failure of love."],
k:"A church can love and serve well and still fail by tolerating teaching that leads people away from Christ."},
{h:"“Son of God” and the emperor",
s:"The title Jesus uses for himself in Thyatira would have sounded like a direct challenge to Rome.",
t:["“The words of the Son of God, who has eyes like a flame of fire, and whose feet are like burnished bronze” (Revelation 2:18). It is the only time in the book Jesus is called “Son of God.” The image of feet like bronze would be felt in a city of bronze-workers.",
"Emperors used divine titles (Augustus was styled “son of the divine Julius”). Thyatira also had a local cult of Apollo, called a son of Zeus. For Christians, the real Son of God is Jesus.",
"The promise to the conqueror is authority over the nations, “as I myself have received authority from my Father,” quoting Psalm 2:8–9. The weak church will share in the Son’s rule."],
k:"Jesus is the true Son of God, and his people will share his authority."},
{h:"Hidden manna and a white stone",
s:"These promises are rich in imagery. Here is what they likely mean.",
t:["Hidden manna (Revelation 2:17) recalls the bread God gave Israel in the wilderness (Exodus 16), a jar of which was kept in the ark (Hebrews 9:4). It is the opposite of idol-food: God’s own provision, held safe for his people.",
"The white stone with a new name is unclear. People in the ancient world used white stones to vote for acquittal, to give as tickets to banquets, or to honor winners. The point is the same: acceptance, belonging, and a secret identity only the receiver knows.",
"To Thyatira, Jesus promises the “morning star” (2:28), which he later names as himself (22:16). The conqueror receives the Lord, not just a prize."],
k:"The promises assure the faithful of provision, acceptance and the presence of Christ himself."}
],
13:[
{h:"Sardis: the city that fell asleep",
s:"Jesus tells Sardis to wake up, and the city’s own history made the warning hit hard.",
t:["Sardis sat on a hilltop citadel that seemed impossible to capture. Yet it fell to Cyrus around 546 BC and to Antiochus III in 214 BC, both times because the defenders left a way up the cliff unguarded. “Wake up” (Revelation 3:2) would remind everyone of that.",
"It was also a wealthy city that had been rebuilt after a major earthquake in AD 17. Archaeologists have found a large synagogue there, showing a substantial Jewish community.",
"Jesus introduces himself as the one who has the seven spirits of God and the seven stars (3:1). He has the fullness of the Spirit and holds the churches, and he knows they have gone to sleep."],
k:"Sardis’s own history warned what happens when you stop watching. Jesus says: wake up."},
{h:"A name but no life",
s:"Sardis looked healthy and was dying, and nothing from outside was attacking it.",
t:["“You have the reputation of being alive, but you are dead” (Revelation 3:1). Unlike other churches, Sardis has no heresy or persecution mentioned. It seems comfortable and respected. The problem is that its works are “not complete” (3:2).",
"The cure is five commands: wake up, strengthen what remains, remember what you received, keep it, and repent (3:2–3). A few in Sardis “have not soiled their garments” and will walk with Jesus in white (3:4).",
"The overcomer is clothed in white garments and will not have his name blotted out of the book of life (3:5; Exodus 32:32–33). In Revelation, white garments mean victory and purity (7:14)."],
k:"A good reputation can hide spiritual death. Jesus calls for waking up and renewal."},
{h:"Coming like a thief",
s:"Jesus uses a thief to warn Sardis, and this image recurs across the New Testament.",
t:["“If you will not wake up, I will come like a thief, and you will not know at what hour I will come against you” (Revelation 3:3). Jesus used the same picture in Matthew 24:43–44, which you studied on Day 7. Paul, Peter and John use it too (1 Thessalonians 5:2–4; 2 Peter 3:10; Revelation 16:15).",
"For Sardis, it was a pointed reminder: their city had fallen to a night-time surprise attack before. If you are asleep, the thief wins.",
"The coming here may be a coming in judgment on the church, as in 2:5, or the final return. Both would take the unprepared by surprise. The only safe response is staying awake."],
k:"The thief image is a call to stay alert, not a code for predicting a date."},
{h:"Philadelphia: little power, open door",
s:"Philadelphia is the other church with no rebuke, and Jesus’ words to it are all encouragement.",
t:["Philadelphia was a small city in an earthquake zone. The church had “little power” (Revelation 3:8), whether that means small numbers, low status or limited strength. Yet it had kept Jesus’ word and had not denied his name.",
"Jesus holds “the key of David, who opens and no one will shut” (3:7; Isaiah 22:22). He alone controls who enters his kingdom. Those who opposed the church, called a “synagogue of Satan” (3:9), will come and bow at their feet, reversing Isaiah’s promises (Isaiah 49:23; 60:14), in which the nations bow to Israel.",
"Jesus also says he will keep them “from the hour of trial that is coming on the whole world” (3:10). Whether that means protection through trials or removal before them is a famous debate, and we will return to it."],
k:"Small and weak is not a problem for the church Jesus is pleased with. He holds the key."},
{h:"The open door and the pillar",
s:"Two images in Philadelphia’s letter say that Jesus gives opportunity and permanence.",
t:["The “open door” (Revelation 3:8) is read two ways. Elsewhere in the New Testament, an open door means an opportunity for sharing the gospel (1 Corinthians 16:9; Colossians 4:3). It can also point to entry into the kingdom, since Jesus holds the key.",
"The promise to the overcomer is: “I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God. Never shall he go out of it” (3:12). For a city that was regularly shaken by earthquakes and whose people often fled outdoors, that is a promise of permanence.",
"Three names are written on the believer: God’s name, the name of the city of God (the new Jerusalem), and Jesus’ new name. This is full belonging."],
k:"Jesus opens doors for his faithful people and gives them a permanent place."}
],
14:[
{h:"Laodicea: neither hot nor cold",
s:"The famous “lukewarm” verse becomes clearer when you know Laodicea’s water.",
t:["Laodicea’s title for Jesus is “the Amen, the faithful and true witness” (Revelation 3:14). The city was a rich center of banking, black wool and eye medicine, but it had no good water of its own and piped it in from a distance. Nearby Hierapolis had hot, medicinal springs, and Colossae had cold, pure water. By the time water reached Laodicea it was lukewarm.",
"The common sermon reads “hot” as passionate and “cold” as unbelieving. But both hot and cold water were useful: hot heals, cold refreshes. Lukewarm does neither and makes you want to spit it out (3:16). Jesus’ complaint is that their works have no effect.",
"Whichever way you read it, the message is that comfort without usefulness is distasteful to Jesus."],
k:"Lukewarm means ineffective. Hot water heals, cold refreshes, and lukewarm does neither."},
{h:"Rich, yet wretched",
s:"Laodicea thought it had everything. Jesus says it has nothing, and then offers to supply exactly what it lacks.",
t:["“You say, ‘I am rich, I have prospered, and I need nothing,’ not realizing that you are wretched, pitiable, poor, blind, and naked” (Revelation 3:17). After an earthquake in about AD 60, Laodicea famously refused help from Rome and rebuilt at its own expense. Self-sufficiency was a point of pride.",
"Jesus offers three things from his own supply: gold refined by fire, white garments to cover shame, and salve for the eyes (3:18). These match what the city was famous for: banking, black wool, eye medicine. They are given freely, since he says “buy” in the same way Isaiah 55:1 says to buy without money.",
"The deeper message: we do not need to be rescued from poverty only. We need to be rescued from thinking we are fine."],
k:"Self-sufficiency is the Laodicean trap. Jesus offers true riches, clothing and sight."},
{h:"The rebuke is love",
s:"Jesus’ strongest words to Laodicea come with an explanation: they come from love.",
t:["“Those whom I love, I reprove and discipline, so be zealous and repent” (Revelation 3:19). It quotes Proverbs 3:11–12, also used in Hebrews 12:5–11, where discipline shows that God treats us as sons and daughters.",
"This helps with how to read all seven letters. Even the harsh parts are meant to restore. Jesus has no interest in destroying churches. He wants to wake them up.",
"A good test for hard words in Scripture is: what is this meant to do? Here it is meant to lead to repentance and a renewed relationship."],
k:"Jesus’ discipline is a sign of his love and aims at restoration."},
{h:"Standing at the door",
s:"Possibly the most famous verse in the letters is usually read in a way slightly different from its original setting.",
t:["“Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, I will come in to him and eat with him, and he with me” (Revelation 3:20). It is often used in evangelism, and the invitation to individuals is real.",
"But look at where it appears: in a letter to a church. The picture is of a congregation that bears Christ’s name while Christ himself is outside. He is knocking at the door of his own church.",
"The promise is a shared meal, a sign of close friendship (Luke 12:36–37), and then a seat with him on his throne, since he too conquered and sat down with his Father on his throne (3:21)."],
k:"Jesus is knocking, even at the door of a church. He offers friendship and a place beside him."},
{h:"Recap: the seven in one view",
s:"Step back and look at all seven letters together. Four patterns stand out.",
t:["Three kinds of danger appear across the letters: outside pressure (Smyrna, Philadelphia, Pergamum), false teaching inside (Ephesus, Pergamum, Thyatira), and comfortable complacency (Sardis, Laodicea). Some contrasts are useful: Ephesus has doctrine without love, while Thyatira has love without doctrine. Smyrna is poor but rich, and Laodicea is rich but poor.",
"The seven promises line up with the end of the book. The tree of life returns in 22:2, the second death is defined in 21:8, the new name and the city are in chapters 21–22, and the reign with Christ is in 22:5. Chapters 2–3 are previews of what chapters 21–22 deliver.",
"Use the table below to compare the letters. Jesus is the same in all of them: he knows, he warns, he encourages, and he promises. That is the lens to carry into the visions."],
k:"The seven letters show what Jesus cares about: faithfulness, love, truth and endurance, with promises that Revelation 21–22 fulfills."}
]
});
Object.assign(TEACH,{
15:[
{h:"“Come up here”",
s:"At chapter 4 the camera moves from earth to heaven, and John is invited to see what is really in charge.",
t:["Chapters 1–3 showed Jesus walking among the lampstands, the churches on earth. Now “a door standing open in heaven” appears, and the same trumpet-like voice from 1:10 says, “Come up here, and I will show you what must take place after this” (Revelation 4:1). John is immediately “in the Spirit” and looking at a throne.",
"The order matters. Before John sees any beast, plague or battle, he is shown who sits on the throne. Everything that follows in the book happens under that throne’s authority.",
"For the seven churches, who had just heard about prison and martyrdom, this is the first and biggest comfort: heaven is not empty, and it is not confused."],
k:"Before anything frightening happens, John sees God on the throne. Everything else is read in that light."},
{h:"A throne described by light",
s:"John carefully avoids describing God’s face and uses only light and color.",
t:["“And he who sat there had the appearance of jasper and carnelian, and around the throne was a rainbow that had the appearance of an emerald” (Revelation 4:3). Jasper shines like crystal, carnelian is deep red, and the rainbow is green. There is no human shape, just brilliance.",
"This follows Ezekiel 1:26–28, where the prophet also sees a throne with a form that glows like fire and a rainbow around it, and carefully says it was “the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD.” The reverence is deliberate: God cannot be pictured or fully described.",
"A rainbow also recalls God’s promise after the flood (Genesis 9:13–16). The throne is a place of terrifying majesty, and also of covenant faithfulness."],
k:"God’s throne shines with glory too bright to describe, and the rainbow reminds us he keeps his promises."},
{h:"The twenty-four elders",
s:"Twenty-four elders sit on thrones around God’s throne, and nobody is completely sure who they are.",
t:["They wear white robes and gold crowns (Revelation 4:4), and they fall down to worship, throwing their crowns before the throne (4:10). Several suggestions exist. They could be a group of angels with priestly or ruling roles, the redeemed people of God (twelve tribes plus twelve apostles, the old and new people of God together), or representatives of all God’s people. Twenty-four was also the number of priestly divisions in Israel (1 Chronicles 24).",
"What the picture says is clear even if the identity is not. They rule under God, they are dressed in victory, and they give everything back in worship. Their crowns are not for show.",
"If they represent God’s people, it is another encouragement: those who seem powerless on earth are seated in heaven."],
k:"The elders show worship and rule together. Whoever they are, they cast their crowns before God."},
{h:"Four living creatures",
s:"Four strange, many-eyed creatures lead heaven’s worship, and each part of their description has meaning.",
t:["One is like a lion, one like an ox, one like a man, and one like an eagle, and they are “full of eyes” and have six wings (Revelation 4:6–8). They never stop saying, “Holy, holy, holy, is the Lord God Almighty, who was and is and is to come.”",
"They pull together Ezekiel 1 (four creatures with eyes, the lion, ox, man and eagle faces) and Isaiah 6 (six wings and “Holy, holy, holy”). One idea is that they represent the best of creation: the wild, the domestic, humanity and the birds. Eyes mean they miss nothing.",
"Their only job is worship. Even the highest creatures in the universe spend their existence pointing to God, not to themselves."],
k:"The four living creatures stand for creation praising its Maker without stopping."},
{h:"The calm sea of glass",
s:"In front of the throne lies a sea like crystal, and in Bible imagery that is a powerful detail.",
t:["“Before the throne there was as it were a sea of glass, like crystal” (Revelation 4:6). In the Old Testament, the sea often pictures chaos, danger and the nations’ restless rage. Daniel 7’s beasts came out of it (Daniel 7:2–3).",
"Here the sea is still, clear and solid, spread out before God like a floor. The chaos that scares everyone else is tamed in his presence. Ezekiel 1:22 also describes a shining expanse beneath the throne.",
"Later the beast will rise from the sea (13:1), and at the end John says that “the sea was no more” (21:1). Those pictures start here with the one who rules the waters."],
k:"What looks like chaos to us is a calm glass floor in front of God’s throne."},
{h:"“You created all things”",
s:"The first great hymn of Revelation is about creation, and it explains why the throne has authority.",
t:["The elders sing: “Worthy are you, our Lord and God, to receive glory and honor and power, for you created all things, and by your will they existed and were created” (Revelation 4:11). The reason God is worthy of worship is that everything exists because of him.",
"This also confronts Rome’s claims. Emperors were called “lord and god,” but they did not create the universe and could not keep it going. Only the Creator holds that place.",
"Chapter 4 prepares for chapter 5, where a second hymn will praise the Lamb for redemption. Creation and redemption together are the book’s foundation."],
k:"God is worthy because he made everything. Every other claim to rule is borrowed."}
],
16:[
{h:"A sealed scroll",
s:"God holds a scroll no one can open, and it stands for the plan that will carry history to its goal.",
t:["The scroll is in God’s right hand, written on both sides and sealed with seven seals (Revelation 5:1). Ezekiel also saw a scroll written on both sides (Ezekiel 2:9–10), and Isaiah spoke of a sealed vision (Isaiah 29:11). Ancient wills and legal documents were sealed, and a seal meant the contents were secure until the right person opened it.",
"Most readers see the scroll as God’s plan for history, his purpose for judging evil and bringing in his kingdom, or as the title deed to the earth. Either way it is the key to what happens next.",
"A strong angel asks, “Who is worthy to open the scroll?” and “no one in heaven or on earth or under the earth” can (5:2–3). The problem is real: if no one can open it, the plan stops."],
k:"The sealed scroll is God’s plan for history, and it cannot move forward until someone worthy appears."},
{h:"John weeps, and then a Lamb appears",
s:"One of the most dramatic turning points in the Bible happens in two verses.",
t:["John weeps loudly because no one is worthy. An elder says, “Weep no more; behold, the Lion of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has conquered, so that he can open the scroll” (Revelation 5:5). Both titles are royal: the Lion comes from Jacob’s blessing of Judah (Genesis 49:9) and the Root from Isaiah 11:1, 10.",
"John turns to look for the Lion and sees something else: “a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain” (5:6). The Lamb recalls Isaiah 53:7, the Passover lamb, and John the Baptist’s words, “Behold, the Lamb of God” (John 1:29). He bears the marks of death, yet he stands.",
"Jesus’ victory is not a victory in spite of the cross. It is a victory through it."],
k:"The Lion who conquers is the slain Lamb who now stands alive."},
{h:"Hear, then see",
s:"John often hears one thing and then sees another. Learning to notice this pattern unlocks the book.",
t:["In Revelation 5, John hears about a Lion and sees a Lamb. They are not two beings but two descriptions of the same person. What he hears is the title (conqueror, king), and what he sees reveals how it happens (sacrifice).",
"You will find the same pattern again in chapter 7, where John hears about an army of 144,000 and sees an uncountable multitude. Many readers think these also show one group, described in two ways.",
"So when a picture in Revelation seems to contradict the words around it, pause. The second picture usually interprets the first."],
k:"Hearing a Lion and seeing a Lamb is a clue: Revelation often explains one image through another."},
{h:"Seven horns, seven eyes",
s:"The Lamb’s strange appearance tells us who he is and what he has.",
t:["The Lamb has “seven horns and seven eyes, which are the seven spirits of God sent out into all the earth” (Revelation 5:6). A horn is a symbol of strength, and seven means completeness: perfect power. Eyes mean knowledge and watchfulness.",
"The eyes come from Zechariah 4:10, which says the “eyes of the LORD... range through the whole earth.” Revelation connects this to the Spirit of God, who is sent out into every place.",
"The picture is about the Lamb’s ability, not his zoology. He has all the power needed to carry out the plan, and nothing in the world escapes his sight."],
k:"Seven horns mean complete power and seven eyes mean complete knowledge. The Lamb lacks neither."},
{h:"Worship in widening rings",
s:"Heaven’s praise starts near the throne and spreads outward until it fills the whole universe.",
t:["First the four creatures and twenty-four elders fall down with harps and golden bowls of incense, “which are the prayers of the saints” (Revelation 5:8). Then they sing. Then John hears “many angels, numbering myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands” (5:11). Finally, “every creature in heaven and on earth and under the earth and in the sea” joins in (5:13).",
"Notice who receives the praise: “To him who sits on the throne and to the Lamb be blessing and honor and glory and might forever and ever.” The Lamb is worshiped together with God on the throne, sharing the same praise.",
"The scene builds to a single moment when the whole creation agrees about who is worthy. It is the opposite of the world John’s readers lived in."],
k:"All creation is eventually drawn into praising the Lamb along with the one on the throne."},
{h:"The new song",
s:"The song praises the Lamb for buying a worldwide people for God.",
t:["“Worthy are you to take the scroll and to open its seals, for you were slain, and by your blood you ransomed people for God from every tribe and language and people and nation, and you have made them a kingdom and priests to our God, and they shall reign on the earth” (Revelation 5:9–10).",
"That fulfills God’s promise to Abraham that all nations would be blessed through him (Genesis 12:3). The people are drawn from everywhere, not a single ethnic group. They are called a “kingdom and priests,” using the title God gave Israel at Sinai (Exodus 19:6).",
"They will reign, which brings us back to Daniel 7:27. The song gives Revelation’s whole message in miniature: Jesus died, people from every nation are bought, and they will reign with him."],
k:"The Lamb’s death has bought a people from every nation, and they will reign with him."}
],
17:[
{h:"The Lamb breaks the seals",
s:"The four horsemen are not loose in the world. They are called out by heaven, one at a time.",
t:["Each time the Lamb breaks one of the first four seals, one of the four living creatures says “Come!” and a horse and rider appear (Revelation 6:1–8). The one who opens the seal is the Lamb who was just declared worthy in chapter 5.",
"This is crucial. The seals do not start because evil takes over history. They begin because Jesus opens the scroll. Even the worst things the riders do are under his authority.",
"Keep this in mind whenever Revelation describes terrifying events. The Lamb is in control of the scroll."],
k:"Every seal is opened by the Lamb. Nothing in this chapter is out of his control."},
{h:"Four horses, four riders",
s:"Each horse has a color and a job, and together they sketch the pattern of a broken world.",
t:["White: a rider with a bow and a crown “conquering, and to conquer” (6:2). Red: a great sword, taking peace from the earth so people kill each other (6:4). Black: a pair of scales, with a quart of wheat or three quarts of barley for a denarius, a full day’s wage, while oil and wine are untouched (6:5–6). That is enough food for one person but not a family, and luxuries remain for the rich. Pale green: Death, with Hades following (6:8).",
"The white rider is debated. Some say Christ, since a white horse is used for him in 19:11. Others say conquest, false messiahs, or an invading empire, such as the bow-wielding Parthians, and note he matches the first of Jesus’ warnings in Matthew 24:4–5.",
"The other three are not controversial: war, famine and death."],
k:"The riders bring conquest, war, famine and death. The first rider’s identity is debated."},
{h:"Zechariah’s horses",
s:"The horsemen were not John’s invention. He borrowed them from Zechariah.",
t:["In Zechariah 1:8–11, riders on red, sorrel and white horses patrol the earth for the LORD. In Zechariah 6:1–8, four chariots with red, black, white and dappled horses go out to the four winds as God’s agents.",
"In Zechariah they are scouts and messengers who carry out God’s decrees. John uses the same picture to show that the forces moving through history are, in the end, on a leash held in heaven.",
"This is Rule 2 from Day 3 in action: find the Old Testament source, and the strange image becomes familiar."],
k:"The four horsemen come from Zechariah, where the horses carry out God’s decrees."},
{h:"The pattern of Jesus’ outline",
s:"The first four seals walk through the same sequence Jesus gave on the Mount of Olives.",
t:["In Matthew 24:4–8, Jesus lists false christs, wars and rumors of wars, famines, and then earthquakes as “the beginning of the birth pains.” Luke 21:11 adds plagues. The first four seals line up in the same order: conquest, war, famine, death.",
"This is why many readers see the seals as covering the same stretch of time as the Olivet Discourse. They describe the troubles that mark the whole era between Jesus’ first and second coming, not a single moment.",
"Others read the seals as events at the end of the age. Both agree that Jesus warned these things would happen and that they are not the end by themselves."],
k:"The seals echo Jesus’ own outline: false messiahs, wars, famines and death."},
{h:"Look for the limits",
s:"The most overlooked detail in this passage is how many limits it contains.",
t:["Count the passive verbs. The first rider “was given a crown” (Revelation 6:2). The second “was permitted to take peace from the earth” and “was given a great sword” (6:4). Death and Hades “were given authority over a fourth of the earth” (6:8). Someone else is doing the giving.",
"The famine has a voice that sets the price and spares the oil and wine (6:6). Death reaches “a fourth,” not all. The terrible things in this chapter have fences around them.",
"The point is not to minimize the suffering but to assure believers that evil is on a leash. This repeats in later cycles, where the plagues expand from a fourth to a third to all."],
k:"Each rider is given limited authority. Evil is on a leash."}
],
18:[
{h:"Souls under the altar",
s:"The fifth seal moves from the world’s troubles to God’s people who died for him.",
t:["“I saw under the altar the souls of those who had been slain for the word of God and for the witness they had borne” (Revelation 6:9). In the temple, the blood of the sacrifices was poured out at the base of the altar (Leviticus 4:7), and Leviticus 17:11 says the life is in the blood.",
"The picture says these believers’ lives were offered as sacrifices. They are not forgotten bodies on a roadside. They are at God’s altar, close to him, alive and aware.",
"Notice the contrast with the earth. The people killed on earth appear to have lost. In heaven, they are at the center of the temple."],
k:"Martyrs are pictured at God’s altar, honored as sacrifices and close to him."},
{h:"Killed for faithfulness",
s:"The cause of their death is stated clearly, and it is not about politics or accident.",
t:["They were slain “for the word of God and for the witness they had borne” (Revelation 6:9). They died because they would not stop following Jesus and telling the truth about him.",
"This ties to 1:9, where John himself is on Patmos for the same reason, and to the letter to Smyrna, “Be faithful unto death” (2:10). It is also the only reason the Lamb himself was slain.",
"Revelation never treats suffering as a sign of God’s absence. It shows it as the cost of witness, which is exactly what Jesus predicted in Matthew 24:9."],
k:"The martyrs died for their loyalty to Jesus, not because God lost control."},
{h:"“How long, O Lord?”",
s:"The martyrs ask the oldest question in the Bible, and Scripture lets them ask it.",
t:["“O Sovereign Lord, holy and true, how long before you will judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?” (Revelation 6:10). The same question fills the Psalms (Psalm 13:1–2; 79:5), Habakkuk (1:2) and Zechariah (1:12). Abel’s blood cried out from the ground in Genesis 4:10.",
"This is not a petty desire for revenge. It is a plea for God to do what is right. Romans 12:19 tells believers to leave vengeance to God, and that is what they are doing: handing the matter to him.",
"The fact that this prayer is in Scripture is a gift. God is not offended by honest questions from suffering people."],
k:"“How long?” is an honest prayer found throughout Scripture. God hears it."},
{h:"A white robe and a little longer",
s:"God’s answer to the martyrs isn’t a date. It is comfort and a promise.",
t:["“Then they were each given a white robe and told to rest a little longer, until the number of their fellow servants and their brothers should be complete, who were to be killed as they themselves had been” (Revelation 6:11). White robes mean victory and purity (7:14).",
"The answer has three parts: honor now (a robe for each one), patience for a short time, and a promise that the number is known to God. He is not late. He is counting.",
"It also means the story is bigger than the martyrs themselves. More will join them, and that is part of God’s plan, not a defeat of it."],
k:"God’s answer is honor now, a little more waiting, and a promise that he has the count."},
{h:"Who are these martyrs?",
s:"The identity depends on how you read the book, but the comfort is the same in every reading.",
t:["Preterist readers see first-century martyrs, such as those under Nero. Historicist readers see martyrs through the whole age of the church. Futurist readers often see those killed in a final period of tribulation. Idealist readers see all believers who have died for their faith.",
"Whichever view you hold, the picture says that God sees, hears and remembers every person who has suffered for Jesus. Their deaths are not unnoticed.",
"This is also meant to shape how we live now. If our faith costs us something, it will not be wasted."],
k:"All readings agree: God hears and remembers those who suffer for Jesus."}
],
19:[
{h:"The cosmic list",
s:"The sixth seal contains a long list of cosmic upheavals, and each has a long history in the Bible.",
t:["“There was a great earthquake, and the sun became black as sackcloth, the full moon became like blood, and the stars of the sky fell to the earth as the fig tree sheds its unripe figs... The sky vanished like a scroll that is being rolled up, and every mountain and island was removed from its place” (Revelation 6:12–14).",
"The stars falling like unripe figs, shaken by a wind, is a picture of utter collapse. The sky being rolled up like a scroll suggests the whole created order being put away. Everything stable is shaken.",
"Some readers take this as literal events at the end of the world. Others take it as the kind of shaking prophets used for judgment events within history. Either way, the picture is of everything people trust being removed."],
k:"The sixth seal describes the shaking of everything people think is stable."},
{h:"Old Testament cosmic language",
s:"Prophets described judgments on nations in language that sounds like the end of the universe.",
t:["Isaiah 13:10 uses sun, moon and star darkening for the fall of Babylon in 539 BC, and Isaiah 34:4 uses the heavens rolling up for Edom. Joel 2:10 and 2:30–31 use it for a locust-like invasion and the Day of the Lord. Amos 8:9 and Nahum 1:5 use similar language for judgments on nations.",
"On the Day of Pentecost Peter says Joel’s prophecy was beginning to be fulfilled (Acts 2:19–21), though the sun did not literally go dark that day.",
"This is why many readers say the language can describe either a historical collapse of powers or the final end. The pattern is the same: when God judges, everything seems to shake."],
k:"Prophets used cosmic language for God’s judgments, even on specific nations."},
{h:"Everyone hides",
s:"The reaction to the sixth seal shows how far the judgment reaches and what people fear most.",
t:["“Then the kings of the earth and the great ones and the generals and the rich and the powerful, and everyone, slave and free, hid themselves in the caves and among the rocks of the mountains, calling to the mountains and rocks, ‘Fall on us and hide us from the face of him who is seated on the throne, and from the wrath of the Lamb’” (Revelation 6:15–16).",
"The words come from Hosea 10:8. Jesus used the same line on the way to the cross (Luke 23:30). The list covers every class, from kings to slaves, so no social position protects anyone.",
"The phrase “wrath of the Lamb” is surprising and deliberate. The Lamb who died is the same person who judges. His mercy and his justice come from the same person."],
k:"No rank or wealth protects from God’s judgment. The Lamb is also the judge."},
{h:"“Who can stand?”",
s:"Chapter 6 ends with an open question, and chapter 7 immediately answers it.",
t:["“For the great day of their wrath has come, and who can stand?” (Revelation 6:17). It is a real question: if everyone is hiding, who survives? The prophets asked something similar in Joel 2:11 and Nahum 1:6.",
"The question is not a rhetorical way to end the chapter. It is a cliffhanger, and chapter 7 is its answer. The next chapter shows those who can stand: God’s people, sealed and robed in white.",
"So the pattern is typical for Revelation: judgment is followed by a picture of those sheltered through it. Fear is never the last word."],
k:"“Who can stand?” is answered immediately in Revelation 7 by the sealed people of God."}
],
20:[
{h:"The four winds held back",
s:"Before more judgment, heaven pauses to protect God’s people.",
t:["John sees four angels at the four corners of the earth holding back the four winds so no wind blows on the earth, sea or trees (Revelation 7:1). Another angel with the seal of the living God calls out: “Do not harm the earth or the sea or the trees, until we have sealed the servants of our God on their foreheads” (7:3).",
"The seal on the forehead recalls Ezekiel 9:4, where a mark is placed on those who grieve over sin in Jerusalem so they are spared. Later in Revelation, the followers of the beast will have a mark, and God’s people have his name on their foreheads (13:16; 14:1; 22:4).",
"The point: God marks those who belong to him before the storm. Belonging, not strength, is what protects."],
k:"God seals his servants before judgment comes. The seal marks who belongs to him."},
{h:"The 144,000",
s:"The number and the list of tribes are among the most discussed details in Revelation.",
t:["“And I heard the number of the sealed, 144,000, sealed from every tribe of the sons of Israel” (Revelation 7:4). It is 12,000 from each of twelve tribes. The number is 12 × 12 × 1,000, so it is built from the number for God’s people multiplied by itself and then by a large number.",
"The list is unusual. Judah is first, not Reuben, the firstborn. Dan and Ephraim are missing, and Manasseh and Joseph appear instead. Levi, who normally has no land, is included. Dan may be left out because of its history of idolatry (Judges 18).",
"Three main readings exist: ethnic Israel, specifically Jewish believers or the Jewish people in the end times; the church, seen as the true Israel (Romans 2:28–29; Galatians 6:16); or a symbolic number for the complete people of God. We will see how the second picture helps."],
k:"The 144,000 are the sealed people of God. Readers disagree whether this means Israel, the church, or both."},
{h:"The great multitude",
s:"Then John looks and sees something he could not count.",
t:["“After this I looked, and behold, a great multitude that no one could number, from every nation, from all tribes and peoples and languages, standing before the throne and before the Lamb, clothed in white robes, with palm branches in their hands” (Revelation 7:9).",
"The language echoes God’s promise to Abraham of descendants like the stars (Genesis 15:5) and the new song of 5:9. Palm branches recall the Feast of Tabernacles and the crowd welcoming Jesus (John 12:13), both times of celebration.",
"They cry out, “Salvation belongs to our God who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb!” (7:10). Their whole identity is gratitude."],
k:"The great multitude is a worldwide, uncountable people celebrating salvation before the throne."},
{h:"“Out of the great tribulation”",
s:"One of the elders explains exactly who this crowd is.",
t:["“These are the ones coming out of the great tribulation. They have washed their robes and made them white in the blood of the Lamb” (Revelation 7:14). “Great tribulation” recalls Jesus’ words in Matthew 24:21 and Daniel 12:1.",
"The tense is “coming out,” which suggests an ongoing stream. Some see a final period of distress and others see the whole age of the church. In either case the crowd consists of those who stayed faithful through suffering.",
"The image of robes washed white in blood is deliberately paradoxical. Blood normally stains. Here it cleans, because it is the blood of the Lamb."],
k:"The multitude has come through great tribulation, cleansed by the Lamb’s blood."},
{h:"Same group, two pictures",
s:"The hear-and-see pattern appears again, and it helps interpret the chapter.",
t:["John hears an exact count: 144,000, organized by tribe, like an army ready to march (Revelation 7:4–8). Then he looks and sees a crowd too large to number, drawn from every nation (7:9). As with the Lion and the Lamb in chapter 5, the two descriptions may be about the same people.",
"One reading says the first is the church as it is counted and sealed by God, and the second is the same people seen as they appear in heaven. Another reading keeps them as two groups, with Israel and the nations. Both readings are held by serious Bible students.",
"What matters is that both are safe. Whether counted or uncountable, they belong to God."],
k:"The 144,000 and the multitude may be one people seen twice, or two groups. Both are safe in God’s hands."},
{h:"Tears wiped away",
s:"The chapter ends with some of the most tender promises in the Bible.",
t:["“They are before the throne of God, and serve him day and night in his temple... They shall hunger no more, neither thirst anymore; the sun shall not strike them, nor any scorching heat. For the Lamb in the midst of the throne will be their shepherd, and he will guide them to springs of living water, and God will wipe away every tear from their eyes” (Revelation 7:15–17).",
"The language is borrowed from Isaiah 49:10 and 25:8. The Lamb is the shepherd, which is another surprise: the sacrifice becomes the one who cares for the flock.",
"This promise returns almost word for word in Revelation 21:4, at the very end. What is promised here as a comfort will be delivered fully there."],
k:"The Lamb will shepherd his people and wipe away every tear. The same promise returns in Revelation 21."}
],
21:[
{h:"Silence in heaven",
s:"When the seventh seal opens, the loud worship of heaven stops.",
t:["“When the Lamb opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven for about half an hour” (Revelation 8:1). After chapters 4–7 full of singing and shouting, this is shocking.",
"In the Old Testament, silence is the right response when God is about to act. “The LORD is in his holy temple; let all the earth keep silence before him” (Habakkuk 2:20), and “Be silent before the Lord GOD! For the day of the LORD is near” (Zephaniah 1:7).",
"The silence is a held breath. It also means the scroll is now fully open, since no more seals remain."],
k:"The silence is awe before God acts."},
{h:"Seals open into trumpets",
s:"The seventh seal does not end the story. It opens into seven trumpets.",
t:["Seven angels receive seven trumpets (Revelation 8:2). The structure is like Russian dolls: the seventh seal contains the seven trumpets, and later the seventh trumpet will contain the seven bowls (11:15–19; 15:1).",
"This matters for how you read the book. Some think the visions run one after another in time. Others think they overlap, with each series looking at the same era from a different angle and growing more intense. We’ll see more evidence on Day 23.",
"Either way, the pattern shows that the book is carefully designed, not random."],
k:"The seventh seal opens into the trumpets, so the series are nested inside each other."},
{h:"Prayers and incense",
s:"The next scene shows what happens to the prayers of God’s people.",
t:["“And another angel came and stood at the altar with a golden censer, and he was given much incense to offer with the prayers of all the saints on the golden altar before the throne, and the smoke of the incense, with the prayers of the saints, rose before God from the hand of the angel” (Revelation 8:3–4).",
"This likely includes the cry of the martyrs, “How long?” (6:10). It is the answer to chapter 6’s question: their prayers are collected, not lost.",
"Incense was part of daily temple worship (Exodus 30:1–8), and Psalm 141:2 compares prayer to incense. The picture is that prayers rise like sweet smoke to God."],
k:"The prayers of God’s people are carried before him like incense. None are lost."},
{h:"Prayers go up, answers come down",
s:"The same censer that carried prayer upward is now thrown down to earth.",
t:["“Then the angel took the censer and filled it with fire from the altar and threw it on the earth, and there were peals of thunder, rumblings, flashes of lightning, and an earthquake” (Revelation 8:5). The same fire that was on the altar with the prayers now falls.",
"The result is a direct link: the trumpets that follow are God’s response to the prayers of his people. They asked how long, and heaven is acting.",
"The thunder, lightning and earthquake pattern appears four times in the book, getting bigger each time (4:5; 8:5; 11:19; 16:18). It signals that God is stepping in."],
k:"The fire from the prayer altar falls to earth. The trumpets are heaven’s response to its people’s prayers."}
],
22:[
{h:"Jesus’ outline in the seals",
s:"Place Revelation 6 beside Matthew 24 and you can see the seals following Jesus’ pattern.",
t:["Jesus gave a sequence in Matthew 24:4–31: false christs, wars and rumors of wars, famines, earthquakes as “the beginning of birth pains,” persecution of believers, cosmic signs, and then the Son of Man coming on the clouds. The seals follow in order: conquest, war, famine, death, martyrs, cosmic upheaval.",
"The table below puts them side by side. The match is too close to be accidental.",
"John seems to have taken Jesus’ own teaching and turned it into a vision. It is a good reminder that Revelation is built on Jesus’ words, not separate from them."],
k:"The seals and Matthew 24 follow the same sequence. Revelation builds on Jesus’ own teaching."},
{h:"What the seals add",
s:"Revelation doesn’t simply copy Jesus’ outline. It adds something he didn’t emphasize.",
t:["Matthew 24 describes events from the point of view of people living through them. Revelation 6 shows the view from heaven: the Lamb opens each seal, and each rider is “given” his authority.",
"This is a different kind of comfort. The disciples are told not to be alarmed because these things must happen. John’s readers are shown why: because the Lamb is in charge of the scroll.",
"If you ever read the seals as only frightening, go back and look for who is opening them."],
k:"Revelation adds the heavenly view: the Lamb holds every seal."},
{h:"A puzzle: the sixth seal sounds like the end",
s:"The sixth seal seems to describe the end of the world, yet the book keeps going.",
t:["Revelation 6:12–17 has the sky rolled up, mountains moved and everyone hiding from the wrath of the Lamb. That sounds like the final day. But in chapter 8 the seventh seal opens and trumpets begin, and later bowls follow.",
"Some think this means the book is going back over the same ground. This is called recapitulation: the seals, trumpets and bowls each describe the whole period between the cross and the return, each time with greater intensity. Others think the visions are sequential, and the sixth seal is simply the start of the final period.",
"We will keep watching for clues. For now, notice the question and don’t force an answer."],
k:"The sixth seal sounds final, yet the book continues. That puzzle is the starting point for recapitulation."},
{h:"Self-check before Week 5",
s:"A short review makes the next section easier.",
t:["Try answering from memory: what did each of the first six seals reveal? (Conquest, war, famine, death, martyrs, cosmic shaking.) Who opens them? (The Lamb.) What question does the sixth seal end with? (“Who can stand?”) Where is the answer? (Revelation 7.)",
"Then think about the two pictures in chapter 7. One is a count and one is a crowd. What might that tell you about how Revelation works?",
"If you can answer those, you have the framework for the trumpets. They follow the seals in the same way the seals followed Jesus’ outline, with new details and higher stakes."],
k:"Six seals, one Lamb, one question, and an answer in chapter 7. That is the framework for what comes next."}
],
23:[
{h:"Four trumpets, four plagues",
s:"The first four trumpets shake the natural world, and each one echoes a plague on Egypt.",
t:["First trumpet: hail and fire mixed with blood burn up a third of the earth, trees and grass (Revelation 8:7), like the seventh plague in Exodus 9:23–25. Second: a great burning mountain is thrown into the sea, and a third of the sea becomes blood and a third of the sea creatures die (8:8–9), like the Nile turning to blood in Exodus 7. Third: a star called Wormwood falls on the rivers, making a third of the water bitter (8:10–11). Fourth: a third of the sun, moon and stars are struck and darkened (8:12), like the plague of darkness in Exodus 10:21–23.",
"Wormwood is a bitter plant that Jeremiah used as a picture of the result of idolatry (Jeremiah 9:15).",
"The Exodus background tells you who the plagues are aimed at. In Egypt they targeted a proud empire that enslaved God’s people, and God’s people were spared."],
k:"The first four trumpets echo the plagues on Egypt, as God confronts the world’s proud powers."},
{h:"Why only a third?",
s:"The fraction matters. It tells you what these judgments are meant to do.",
t:["The seals touched “a fourth of the earth” (Revelation 6:8). The trumpets hit “a third” (8:7–12). The bowls, as you will see in chapter 16, are not limited. The pattern is a growing intensity with a limited reach, and each stage is a warning.",
"This fits Revelation’s later complaint: after the plagues, people still “did not repent” (9:20–21). The judgments are not only punishment. They are loud calls to turn around.",
"A third is a reminder that God is patient. 2 Peter 3:9 says he is “not wishing that any should perish, but that all should reach repentance.”"],
k:"A third means warning, not the end. The trumpets call people to repent while there is still time."},
{h:"The eagle’s three woes",
s:"After four trumpets, a voice warns that the worst is still ahead.",
t:["“Then I looked, and I heard an eagle crying with a loud voice as it flew directly overhead, ‘Woe, woe, woe to those who dwell on the earth, at the blasts of the other trumpets that the three angels are about to blow!’” (Revelation 8:13). Some ancient manuscripts say “angel” here, and that is the reading used in the KJV in the built-in Bible reader.",
"“Those who dwell on the earth” is a phrase that appears often in Revelation for people who have made the world their home and put their trust in it. It is not a general description of all humans but of those who oppose God.",
"The three woes will be trumpets 5, 6 and 7. A pause in the action builds tension before the next section."],
k:"The eagle announces three woes for the last three trumpets. The worst judgments are still ahead."}
],
24:[
{h:"The key to the abyss",
s:"The fifth trumpet opens a shaft to the abyss, and the word “given” appears again.",
t:["“And I saw a star fallen from heaven to earth, and he was given the key to the shaft of the bottomless pit. He opened the shaft of the bottomless pit, and from the shaft rose smoke like the smoke of a great furnace, and the sun and the air were darkened” (Revelation 9:1–2). The star is a personal being, probably a fallen angel (compare Isaiah 14:12; Luke 10:18).",
"The abyss is the realm of evil spirits (Luke 8:31) and will come up again in Revelation 11:7, 17:8 and 20:1–3. Notice that the star is “given” the key. Someone else holds the real authority.",
"Out of the smoke come locusts. The eighth plague on Egypt was locusts (Exodus 10), and Joel 1–2 used a locust swarm as a picture of an invading army."],
k:"The abyss is opened with a key that is given. Even demonic forces act only by permission."},
{h:"Exact limits",
s:"The locusts are terrifying, but the passage insists on strict limits.",
t:["They are told not to harm grass, plants or trees, “but only those people who do not have the seal of God on their foreheads” (Revelation 9:4). They may torment but not kill, and only for five months (9:5), about the length of the locust season.",
"God’s people are protected by the seal from chapter 7. The sealed servants are not removed from the world, but they are marked as belonging to God.",
"The torment is meant to be so severe that people seek death and cannot find it (9:6). That is a terrible picture, but it is also bounded and aimed at those who refuse to repent."],
k:"The locusts can torment but not kill, only for five months, and cannot touch those sealed by God."},
{h:"The war-horse swarm and Abaddon",
s:"The locusts look like a cavalry army, and their leader has a name.",
t:["They look like horses prepared for battle, with crowns like gold, faces like human faces, hair like women’s hair, teeth like lions’ teeth, breastplates of iron, and wings roaring like chariots (Revelation 9:7–9). Joel 2:4–5 describes an invading army in the same way.",
"“They have as king over them the angel of the bottomless pit. His name in Hebrew is Abaddon, and in Greek he is called Apollyon” (9:11). Both mean “Destroyer.” Abaddon appears in the Old Testament for the place of destruction (Job 26:6; Proverbs 15:11).",
"Interpreters differ on whether these are demonic forces, a human army, or a symbol of the torment evil brings. They agree that the leader of destruction serves a limited purpose and ends at “the first woe is past” (9:12)."],
k:"The locust army is led by Abaddon, “Destroyer.” His reach is limited and the first woe passes."}
],
25:[
{h:"A voice from the golden altar",
s:"The sixth trumpet starts with a voice from the altar of prayer.",
t:["“Then the sixth angel blew his trumpet, and I heard a voice from the four horns of the golden altar before God, saying to the sixth angel who had the trumpet, ‘Release the four angels who are bound at the great river Euphrates’” (Revelation 9:13–14). The horns were the corners of the incense altar (Exodus 30:2).",
"It is the same altar where the prayers of the saints were offered in 8:3–5. The next judgment is connected directly to those prayers.",
"The four angels have been held for a precise time: “the hour, the day, the month, and the year” (9:15). God’s timetable is exact, even when we cannot see it."],
k:"The sixth trumpet’s command comes from the prayer altar. God’s timing is exact."},
{h:"The Euphrates army",
s:"A giant army crosses the Euphrates, the traditional river of invasion.",
t:["The Euphrates was Israel’s ideal northern boundary (Genesis 15:18) and the direction from which Assyria and Babylon invaded (Isaiah 8:7; Jeremiah 46). In John’s day, the Parthian empire beyond the Euphrates was the one frontier Rome feared.",
"The army’s number is “twice ten thousand times ten thousand” (Revelation 9:16), which is 200 million. Some take that literally. Others note that the phrase “myriads of myriads” is used for overwhelming numbers elsewhere (Daniel 7:10; Revelation 5:11).",
"The horses have heads like lions and breathe fire, smoke and sulfur, and a third of mankind is killed (9:17–18). Whether the army is spiritual, human, or both, the point is overwhelming force held back until God lets it go."],
k:"An overwhelming army crosses the Euphrates at God’s appointed time."},
{h:"They still did not repent",
s:"The most important sentence in the chapter comes last.",
t:["“The rest of mankind, who were not killed by these plagues, did not repent of the works of their hands nor give up worshiping demons and idols of gold and silver and bronze and stone and wood, which cannot see or hear or walk, nor did they repent of their murders or their sorceries or their sexual immorality or their thefts” (Revelation 9:20–21).",
"Idols that “cannot see or hear or walk” echo Psalm 115:4–7 and Daniel 5:23. The sins listed are a mix of false worship and harm to others.",
"This is like Pharaoh in Exodus 7–11, who was hardened by each plague. The plagues were meant to turn people to God, and the sad truth is that people can see judgment and still refuse. That is why God’s patience matters so much."],
k:"The purpose of the trumpets is repentance, yet most people still refuse. That makes God’s patience more striking."}
],
26:[
{h:"Another mighty angel",
s:"After the sixth trumpet comes a pause, like the interlude in chapter 7.",
t:["“Then I saw another mighty angel coming down from heaven, wrapped in a cloud, with a rainbow over his head, and his face was like the sun, and his legs like pillars of fire. He had a little scroll open in his hand. And he set his right foot on the sea, and his left foot on the land” (Revelation 10:1–2). Some think this is Christ because of the cloud, rainbow, sun-like face and fiery legs. Others think a very powerful angel.",
"The little scroll is open, unlike the sealed scroll of chapter 5. One foot on the sea and one on the land claims all creation.",
"He calls out with a voice like a lion’s roar. The scene prepares for the final trumpet by reminding us who owns the world."],
k:"A mighty angel with an open scroll claims land and sea for God."},
{h:"Sealed thunders and “no more delay”",
s:"John is told not to write down everything he hears, and the angel makes a solemn oath.",
t:["Seven thunders speak, and John is about to write, but a voice says, “Seal up what the seven thunders have said, and do not write it down” (Revelation 10:4). Some things are not revealed. Deuteronomy 29:29 says, “The secret things belong to the LORD our God.”",
"The angel raises his hand and swears “by him who lives forever and ever, who created heaven and what is in it” that “there will be no more delay, but in the days of the trumpet call to be sounded by the seventh angel, the mystery of God would be fulfilled, just as he announced to his servants the prophets” (10:5–7). “No more delay” can also be read “no more time.”",
"The promise is that the long wait is almost over."],
k:"Some things stay secret, but the angel swears that God’s plan is about to be completed."},
{h:"Sweet, then bitter",
s:"John is told to eat the little scroll, and it affects him in two ways.",
t:["“So I went to the angel and told him to give me the little scroll. And he said to me, ‘Take it and eat it. It will make your stomach bitter, but in your mouth it will be sweet as honey’” (Revelation 10:9). Ezekiel had a similar experience in Ezekiel 2:8–3:3, and Jeremiah said God’s words were his joy (Jeremiah 15:16).",
"The word of God is sweet to receive because it is true and good. It is bitter to deliver because it contains judgment and the cost of witness.",
"John is then told, “You must again prophesy about many peoples and nations and languages and kings” (10:11). This leads straight into chapter 11, where the church’s witness becomes the focus."],
k:"God’s word is sweet to receive and bitter to deliver. John must keep prophesying."}
],
27:[
{h:"Measuring the temple",
s:"John is given a measuring rod, and what he measures tells us what God protects.",
t:["“Rise and measure the temple of God and the altar and those who worship there, but do not measure the court outside the temple; leave that out, for it is given over to the nations, and they will trample the holy city for forty-two months” (Revelation 11:1–2). Measuring is used in Ezekiel 40–48 and Zechariah 2 to mark protection and restoration.",
"What is the temple? Futurist readers often see a rebuilt temple in Jerusalem. Others see the church, since the New Testament calls believers God’s temple (1 Corinthians 3:16; Ephesians 2:21). Some see Jerusalem’s temple before AD 70.",
"The forty-two months equal the 1,260 days of verse 3 and “a time, times, and half a time” (12:14), the limited time of trouble from Daniel."],
k:"Measuring marks what God protects. The outer court is trampled for a limited 42 months."},
{h:"Two witnesses",
s:"Two witnesses prophesy for 1,260 days and look like figures from the Old Testament.",
t:["“I will grant authority to my two witnesses, and they will prophesy for 1,260 days, clothed in sackcloth” (Revelation 11:3). They are “the two olive trees and the two lampstands that stand before the Lord of the earth” (11:4), a picture from Zechariah 4, where the Spirit supplies light “not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit.”",
"They act like Elijah, calling down fire and shutting the sky so there is no rain (1 Kings 17; 2 Kings 1), and like Moses, turning water to blood and striking the earth with plagues. Two witnesses were also required for valid testimony in Deuteronomy 19:15.",
"Readers differ: two literal prophets at the end, the church as a witnessing community, or Moses and Elijah as the Law and the Prophets. The key is that witness is powerful because God’s Spirit is behind it."],
k:"The two witnesses prophesy with the Spirit’s power. Their identity is debated, but their role is clear."},
{h:"Killed, raised, and taken up",
s:"The witnesses’ story follows a pattern that should sound familiar.",
t:["When they have finished their testimony, “the beast that rises from the bottomless pit will make war on them and conquer them and kill them” (Revelation 11:7). This is the first time “the beast” appears. Their bodies lie in the street of the great city, “which is spiritually called Sodom and Egypt, where their Lord was crucified” (11:8). The world celebrates and exchanges gifts.",
"After three and a half days, the breath of life from God enters them (compare Ezekiel 37:5, 10), they stand, and a voice says, “Come up here!” They go up in a cloud (11:11–12). A great earthquake follows, and “the rest were terrified and gave glory to the God of heaven” (11:13).",
"The pattern is the pattern of Jesus: faithful witness, death in the city, vindication. It is Revelation’s promise to every witness who dies faithfully."],
k:"The witnesses follow Jesus’ path: faithful witness, death, resurrection. God has the last word."}
]
});
Object.assign(TEACH,{
28:[
{h:"“Has become the kingdom of our Lord”",
s:"At the seventh trumpet, heaven announces that the victory is won, even though more of the story is still to come.",
t:["“Then the seventh angel blew his trumpet, and there were loud voices in heaven, saying, ‘The kingdom of the world has become the kingdom of our Lord and of his Christ, and he shall reign forever and ever’” (Revelation 11:15). The verb is in the past tense, as though it is already done.",
"This is how prophecy often speaks: so certain that it is described as finished. It echoes Psalm 2, where God gives his anointed king the nations, and Daniel 2:44, where God’s kingdom replaces every empire.",
"You might wonder why the book continues for eleven more chapters. The answer is that the rest of the book shows how the kingdom comes and what it costs, not whether it will."],
k:"The seventh trumpet announces that the kingdom is certain. The remaining chapters show how it arrives."},
{h:"Reward, judgment and destroyers",
s:"The elders’ song explains what the kingdom’s arrival means.",
t:["“The nations raged, but your wrath came, and the time for the dead to be judged, and for rewarding your servants, the prophets and saints, and those who fear your name, both small and great, and for destroying the destroyers of the earth” (Revelation 11:18).",
"Three things happen together: judgment of the dead, reward for those who belong to God, and an end to those who ruin the earth. The same moment brings good news and bad news, as in the Day of the Lord (Day 5).",
"A small detail: the elders say God “who is and who was” in 11:17, with no “who is to come” as in 1:4 and 4:8. Many scholars think it is because he has now arrived."],
k:"The kingdom brings judgment, reward and the end of those who destroy the earth, all at once."},
{h:"The ark in the open temple",
s:"The temple in heaven opens and the ark of the covenant appears.",
t:["“Then God’s temple in heaven was opened, and the ark of his covenant was seen within his temple. There were flashes of lightning, rumblings, peals of thunder, an earthquake, and heavy hail” (Revelation 11:19). The ark was the box that held the tablets of the law and symbolized God’s presence. It had been lost since Babylon destroyed Jerusalem, and Jeremiah 3:16–17 said people would no longer miss it because God himself would be present.",
"Here the ark appears in the heavenly temple. The point is that God is faithful to his covenant, which is exactly what the seven trumpets of judgment prove.",
"The lightning, thunder and earthquake mark the end of the trumpet series, as they did the seals (8:5). The “third woe” promised in 8:13 is described in 12:12."],
k:"The ark in heaven shows God keeps his covenant. The trumpets end with the same signs as the seals."}
],
29:[
{h:"The repeating shape",
s:"Looking back at chapters 1–11 reveals a repeating design that will help with the rest of the book.",
t:["The seals and trumpets share a structure: six, then a pause, then the seventh. The seals run from 6:1 to 8:1, and the pause is chapter 7 with the sealed multitude. The trumpets run from 8:2 to 11:19, and the pause is 10:1 to 11:13 with the little scroll and the two witnesses.",
"In each interlude, the focus shifts from the world’s troubles to God’s people: sealed, protected, commissioned to witness. This is how Revelation consistently comforts its readers.",
"If you remember one structural lesson from the first half, it is this: after trouble, the book stops to show how God keeps his people."],
k:"Each series has six, a pause for God’s people, and then the seventh. The pauses are the comfort."},
{h:"The same closing signs",
s:"Both series end with lightning, thunder and an earthquake at the throne, and the prophets used the same colors.",
t:["After the seventh seal comes the censer thrown down with thunder, lightning and an earthquake (Revelation 8:5). After the seventh trumpet comes the ark with lightning, thunder, earthquake and hail (11:19). The same signs mark God’s presence arriving in judgment.",
"Zephaniah 1:14–18 paints the Day of the Lord in similar colors: “a day of trumpet blast and battle cry,” “a day of wrath,” of “darkness and gloom.” John is using the prophets’ pattern.",
"This supports the view that the series are linked or overlapping. Each ends with the same arrival of God. Whether you read them as sequential or as repeated views of the same era, the destination is the same."],
k:"Both series end with the same signs of God’s arrival, in the colors of the Day of the Lord."},
{h:"Check yourself",
s:"A good review makes the second half easier. Try explaining these terms in your own words before looking at the answers.",
t:["Lampstands: the churches (1:20). The Lamb with seven eyes: perfect knowledge and the Spirit sent into all the earth (5:6). White robes: victory and purity, washed in the Lamb’s blood (7:14). “A third”: a limited warning, not the end (8:7–12). The abyss: the realm of evil spirits (9:1–2). 1,260 days: three and a half years, a limited period of trouble taken from Daniel (11:3).",
"Now write five sentences on what Revelation is about so far. A good summary might say: Jesus is alive and rules; he knows his churches; the Lamb opens the scroll; judgments are limited warnings aimed at repentance; and his people are sealed, protected and called to witness.",
"If you can do that, you are ready for chapter 12, where the story steps back to show the conflict behind it all."],
k:"If you can explain those six terms and write five sentences on the book’s message, you have the foundation for the second half."}
],
30:[
{h:"The woman",
s:"A glorious woman appears in heaven, and her description is full of Old Testament clues.",
t:["“A great sign appeared in heaven: a woman clothed with the sun, with the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars” (Revelation 12:1). The imagery comes from Joseph’s dream in Genesis 37:9, where the sun, moon and eleven stars bowed to him, and they stood for his family. Twelve stars point to the twelve tribes of Israel.",
"She is pregnant and cries out in labor (12:2), like Zion in Isaiah 66:7 and Micah 5:2–3. The child she bears is the Messiah, who will “rule all the nations with a rod of iron” (12:5; Psalm 2:9).",
"Her identity is layered. She is Israel, from whom the Messiah came, and also the whole people of God, since at the end of the chapter her other children are those who “hold to the testimony of Jesus” (12:17). Many Christians also see Mary in the picture, as the one who bore the Messiah. These readings overlap more than they compete."],
k:"The woman represents God’s people who bring forth the Messiah, from Israel to the church."},
{h:"The dragon",
s:"A fiery red dragon waits to destroy the child, and the book tells you exactly who he is.",
t:["“And behold, a great red dragon, with seven heads and ten horns, and on his heads seven diadems” (Revelation 12:3). Seven heads and diadems suggest a counterfeit of the Lamb’s seven horns and eyes. His tail sweeps a third of the stars to earth (12:4), echoing Daniel 8:10.",
"Verse 9 settles the identity: “that ancient serpent, who is called the devil and Satan, the deceiver of the whole world.” This goes back to Genesis 3, where the serpent deceived Eve, and God promised that the woman’s offspring would crush his head (Genesis 3:15).",
"His attempts to devour the child repeat through the Bible: Pharaoh ordering the Hebrew babies killed (Exodus 1) and Herod’s slaughter in Bethlehem (Matthew 2). Behind earthly tyrants is the same enemy."],
k:"The dragon is Satan, and every attempt to destroy the Messiah traces back to the serpent in Genesis 3."},
{h:"The child and the wilderness",
s:"The child is taken up to God, and the woman flees, in two verses that cover a lot of history.",
t:["“She gave birth to a male child, one who is to rule all the nations with a rod of iron, but her child was caught up to God and to his throne” (Revelation 12:5). Notice that the verse moves directly from birth to ascension. The cross and resurrection are not mentioned here, as the focus is on the dragon’s failure: he cannot stop the Messiah from reaching the throne.",
"The woman flees into the wilderness, “where she has a place prepared by God, in which she is to be nourished for 1,260 days” (12:6). The wilderness recalls Israel’s time after the exodus, when God provided for them (Exodus 19:4; Hosea 2:14).",
"The 1,260 days are the same three and a half years you have seen. God’s people are hunted but not abandoned, and for a limited time."],
k:"The dragon fails to stop the Messiah. His people are protected in the wilderness for a limited time."}
],
31:[
{h:"War in heaven",
s:"The conflict behind the earthly struggle breaks into the open, and the dragon loses.",
t:["“Now war arose in heaven, Michael and his angels fighting against the dragon. And the dragon and his angels fought back, but he was defeated, and there was no longer any place for them in heaven” (Revelation 12:7–8). Michael is the angel from Daniel 10 and 12.",
"The timing is debated. Some read this as a primeval event before creation, others as something that happened because of the cross and resurrection, others as a future event. Jesus said, “I saw Satan fall like lightning from heaven” (Luke 10:18) and, before the cross, “now will the ruler of this world be cast out” (John 12:31). Isaiah 14:12–15 pictures a proud king falling, which many have applied to Satan.",
"In every reading, Satan is not an equal of God. He is defeated by an angel."],
k:"Satan is cast down. He is not God’s equal, since an archangel can defeat him."},
{h:"The Accuser and the blood",
s:"Satan’s role is accusation, and Revelation says how believers defeat it.",
t:["“The accuser of our brothers has been thrown down, who accuses them day and night before our God” (Revelation 12:10). That is Satan’s role in Job 1–2, where he accuses Job, and in Zechariah 3:1–5, where he accuses Joshua the high priest, whose filthy clothes are replaced with clean ones.",
"The believers’ answer is in verse 11: “And they have conquered him by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony, for they loved not their lives even unto death.” Three things: the Lamb’s blood (his sacrifice answers every charge), their testimony, and willingness to die.",
"Compare Romans 8:33–34: “Who shall bring any charge against God’s elect? It is God who justifies.” The accusations have no ground left."],
k:"Believers overcome the Accuser by the Lamb’s blood, their testimony, and loyalty even in death."},
{h:"The dragon turns on the woman",
s:"The defeated dragon goes after the woman, and God protects her again.",
t:["Cast down to earth, the dragon is “filled with fury, because he knows that his time is short” (Revelation 12:12). He pursues the woman, but she is given “the two wings of the great eagle,” echoing Exodus 19:4, where God carried Israel on eagles’ wings, and she flies to the wilderness to be cared for for “a time, and times, and half a time” (12:14).",
"The serpent pours water like a flood from his mouth, but “the earth came to the help of the woman, and the earth opened its mouth and swallowed the river” (12:15–16), like the earth swallowing Egypt’s army (Exodus 15:12) and Korah (Numbers 16).",
"Finally he “went off to make war on the rest of her offspring, those who keep the commandments of God and hold to the testimony of Jesus” (12:17). That means the church, and sets up the next chapter."],
k:"The dragon’s rage is limited and his floods are swallowed. He turns to attack the church."}
],
32:[
{h:"A beast made of Daniel’s beasts",
s:"The dragon calls up an ally, and the description of it should remind you of Daniel 7.",
t:["“And I saw a beast rising out of the sea, with ten horns and seven heads, with ten diadems on its horns and blasphemous names on its heads. And the beast that I saw was like a leopard; its feet were like a bear’s, and its mouth was like a lion’s mouth” (Revelation 13:1–2). Daniel’s four beasts were a lion, bear, leopard and a terrible fourth (Daniel 7:3–7). Here they are combined in one.",
"The dragon gives it “his power and his throne and great authority.” That is a parody of the Father giving authority to the Son. The beast is a counterfeit Christ.",
"The sea suggests chaos and the nations. This is a political power that emerges from the turmoil of human history."],
k:"The beast from the sea combines Daniel’s four beasts: an imitation of Christ’s authority."},
{h:"The wounded head and “Who is like the beast?”",
s:"The beast imitates the Lamb in two ways, and both are parodies.",
t:["“One of its heads seemed to have a mortal wound, but its mortal wound was healed, and the whole earth marveled as they followed the beast. And they worshiped the dragon... and they worshiped the beast, saying, ‘Who is like the beast, and who can fight against it?’” (Revelation 13:3–4).",
"The healed wound imitates the Lamb that was slain but lives. The question mocks the Bible’s question, “Who is like you, O LORD?” (Exodus 15:11; Micah 7:18). Some readers connect the wounded head to a legend that Nero would return after his death, though that is debated.",
"The point is that evil copies what is true because it cannot create. It offers a fake resurrection and demands worship that belongs to God alone."],
k:"The beast copies the Lamb with a fake resurrection and takes worship that only God deserves."},
{h:"Forty-two months and a call to endure",
s:"The beast has real power, but for a fixed time, and the response is endurance.",
t:["He is given a mouth to speak blasphemies and authority for forty-two months, to make war on the saints and conquer them, with authority over every tribe, people, language and nation (Revelation 13:5–7). Once again the language is passive: it was “given.” The time is the three and a half years you have already met.",
"Verse 8 says everyone worships him “whose name has not been written before the foundation of the world in the book of life of the Lamb who was slain.” Verse 10 echoes Jeremiah 15:2 and ends: “Here is a call for the endurance and faith of the saints.”",
"The beast could be Rome, a future ruler, or a repeating pattern of tyranny. Whichever you take, the response is the same: be faithful to the Lamb."],
k:"The beast’s power is real but limited to 42 months. The call is to endure in faith."}
],
33:[
{h:"The imposter lamb",
s:"A second beast appears that looks harmless and acts as the first beast’s publicist.",
t:["“Then I saw another beast rising out of the earth. It had two horns like a lamb and it spoke like a dragon” (Revelation 13:11). It looks gentle but sounds like Satan. Later this beast is called the false prophet (16:13; 19:20).",
"It makes everyone worship the first beast, performs great signs including calling down fire from heaven (a counterfeit of Elijah, 1 Kings 18), and orders an image of the beast to be made, which is given breath to speak (13:12–15).",
"In John’s world that would remind readers of the imperial cult, with its priests, statues and loyalty tests. It also warns every age to watch for religion that serves political power."],
k:"The second beast is a false prophet who promotes the state’s claim to worship."},
{h:"The mark on hand and forehead",
s:"The mark of the beast is a counterfeit of God’s seal, and its main point is allegiance.",
t:["“Also it causes all, both small and great, both rich and poor, both free and slave, to be marked on the right hand or the forehead, so that no one can buy or sell unless he has the mark” (Revelation 13:16–17). In Deuteronomy 6:8 God’s people were told to bind his words on their hands and foreheads. God’s servants are sealed on their foreheads in 7:3 and have the Father’s name in 14:1.",
"The mark copies that. It shows who owns you. In John’s day, trade guilds and markets were tied up with idol worship (see the letters to Pergamum and Thyatira), so refusing could cost you your job.",
"Some readers think it is a literal future mark. Others see it as a symbol of allegiance, as the seal is. Both agree the contrast is between belonging to the Lamb and belonging to the beast."],
k:"The mark counterfeits God’s seal. The choice is whom you belong to."},
{h:"The number 666",
s:"The most famous number in the Bible comes with a request to think.",
t:["“This calls for wisdom: let the one who has understanding calculate the number of the beast, for it is the number of a man, and his number is 666” (Revelation 13:18). In Hebrew and Greek, letters double as numbers. Writing “Nero Caesar” in Hebrew letters adds up to 666, and spelling it without the final “n” gives 616, a variant found in some ancient manuscripts.",
"Other candidates have been suggested over the centuries, and none has been universally accepted. The text calls it “the number of a man,” and the repeated sixes fall short of seven, suggesting something that imitates completeness but never reaches it.",
"The safest reading is that 666 identifies a human power that claims divine honor and fails. Whatever else it means, it is not a cause for fear: Jesus has already beaten it."],
k:"666 is a human number that falls short of seven. Nero fits a popular reading, but the main point is that human power imitates God and fails."}
],
34:[
{h:"The man of lawlessness",
s:"Paul describes a figure who will appear before Jesus returns, and the description connects with Revelation.",
t:["Paul says that the Day of the Lord will not come “unless the rebellion comes first, and the man of lawlessness is revealed, the son of destruction, who opposes and exalts himself against everything called divine or worshiped, so that he takes his seat in the temple of God, proclaiming himself to be God” (2 Thessalonians 2:3–4).",
"Jesus will destroy him “with the breath of his mouth” (2:8), recalling Isaiah 11:4 and the sword from Jesus’ mouth in Revelation 19:15. His coming is “by the activity of Satan with all power and false signs and wonders,” and people perish because “they refused to love the truth” (2:9–10).",
"Where is “the temple of God”? Readers say a rebuilt temple, the church, or a symbol for claiming God’s place. This passage and Revelation 13 clearly describe the same kind of figure."],
k:"Paul’s man of lawlessness claims God’s place, uses false signs and is destroyed by Jesus’ word."},
{h:"Many antichrists already",
s:"John is the only Bible writer who uses the word “antichrist,” and he uses it differently than you might expect.",
t:["“Children, it is the last hour, and as you have heard that antichrist is coming, so now many antichrists have come” (1 John 2:18). He defines the spirit: “Who is the liar but he who denies that Jesus is the Christ? This is the antichrist” (2:22) and “every spirit that confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is from God” (4:2–3).",
"The Greek prefix “anti” can mean “against” or “in place of,” and an antichrist both opposes Christ and imitates him. For John, the pattern is already visible among false teachers.",
"This helps keep a balance. There may be a final, personal Antichrist, and the spirit of antichrist is also at work in every generation."],
k:"For John, antichrist is both a coming figure and a present spirit that denies who Jesus is."},
{h:"Types and the restrainer",
s:"Scripture shows the pattern of an arrogant ruler repeatedly, and Paul mentions something holding it back.",
t:["Pharaoh enslaved God’s people and defied him (Exodus 5). Nebuchadnezzar built a golden image and demanded worship (Daniel 3). Antiochus IV desecrated the temple in 167 BC, and Daniel 11:36–45 describes a king who exalts himself and speaks blasphemies. Each is a type, a pattern pointing to a greater fulfillment.",
"Paul says “you know what is restraining him now,” and that “the one who now restrains it will do so until he is out of the way” (2 Thessalonians 2:6–7). Who or what is the restrainer? Suggestions include the Roman government and law, the Holy Spirit, the church’s witness, the preaching of the gospel, or an angel.",
"Paul seems to expect his readers to know, and we don’t. It is better to say honestly that the identification is uncertain than to guess confidently."],
k:"Pharaoh, Nebuchadnezzar and Antiochus are types of the Antichrist. The restrainer’s identity is uncertain."}
],
35:[
{h:"The 144,000 on Mount Zion",
s:"The 144,000 appear again, this time with the Lamb on Zion, and their description confirms what the seal meant.",
t:["“Then I looked, and behold, on Mount Zion stood the Lamb, and with him 144,000 who had his name and his Father’s name written on their foreheads” (Revelation 14:1). Zion is where God’s king is installed in Psalm 2:6. The name on the forehead is the opposite of the beast’s mark in 13:16, and it is the same as the seal in 7:3.",
"They sing a new song that only they can learn, follow the Lamb wherever he goes, and are “firstfruits for God and the Lamb” (14:4). It says they are “virgins” who have not defiled themselves with women. Many take this as a picture of spiritual purity and loyalty to God as against idolatry (see Hosea; 2 Corinthians 11:2). Others read it more literally.",
"“No lie was found in their mouth, for they are blameless” (14:5). They are the people who stayed faithful to the Lamb rather than the beast."],
k:"The 144,000 stand with the Lamb on Zion, marked with his name, loyal and blameless."},
{h:"Three angels",
s:"Three angels fly with a message for the whole world.",
t:["The first has “an eternal gospel to proclaim to those who dwell on earth, to every nation and tribe and language and people,” saying, “Fear God and give him glory, for the hour of his judgment has come, and worship him who made heaven and earth, the sea and the springs of water” (Revelation 14:6–7). Even in judgment, there is gospel and an invitation.",
"The second announces, “Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great” (14:8), quoting Isaiah 21:9. It is announced in advance, and the details come in chapters 17–18.",
"The third warns that anyone who worships the beast will drink the wine of God’s wrath (14:9–11; Psalm 75:8; Isaiah 51:17). Then comes the call: “Here is a call for the endurance of the saints” (14:12)."],
k:"The three angels announce the gospel, Babylon’s fall, and a warning against the beast."},
{h:"“Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord”",
s:"In the middle of the warnings comes the second of the seven blessings.",
t:["“And I heard a voice from heaven saying, ‘Write this: Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from now on.’ ‘Blessed indeed,’ says the Spirit, ‘that they may rest from their labors, for their deeds follow them!’” (Revelation 14:13). This is the second of seven blessings in the book, after 1:3.",
"For Christians facing death for their faith, it reframes what dying means. It is not defeat. It is rest from labor, and nothing they have done for God is lost: their deeds go with them.",
"The Spirit himself adds the “Yes.” It is one of the few places in the book where he speaks."],
k:"Death in the Lord is blessed rest. Nothing done for God is wasted."},
{h:"Two harvests",
s:"The chapter ends with two harvest scenes, and readers disagree about what they mean.",
t:["First, “one like a son of man,” sitting on a white cloud with a golden crown and a sharp sickle, is told, “the harvest of the earth is ripe,” and the earth is reaped (Revelation 14:14–16). Then another angel with a sickle gathers the grapes and throws them into “the great winepress of the wrath of God,” and blood flows from the winepress as high as a horse’s bridle for about 1,600 stadia, roughly 180 miles (14:17–20).",
"The grape harvest is clearly judgment (Isaiah 63:1–6; Joel 3:13). The first harvest is debated. Some take the grain harvest as gathering God’s people, as in Matthew 13:30. Others say both are judgment, since Joel 3:13 also uses a sickle for a harvest of judgment.",
"What is certain: at the end, the Son of Man brings in what is ripe, and the story moves toward the final bowls."],
k:"The two harvests picture the end of history. The grapes are judgment, and the grain may be the gathering of God’s people or more judgment."}
],
36:[
{h:"The song of Moses and the Lamb",
s:"After the plagues begin, John sees those who beat the beast singing beside a sea.",
t:["“And I saw what appeared to be a sea of glass mingled with fire, and those who had conquered the beast and its image and the number of its name, standing beside the sea of glass with harps of God in their hands. And they sing the song of Moses, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb” (Revelation 15:2–3).",
"The song of Moses is from Exodus 15, sung after Israel crossed the Red Sea and was rescued from Pharaoh. The people sing after the rescue, not before. Their song praises God’s great and amazing deeds, his just and true ways, and says that all nations will come and worship (15:3–4).",
"The pairing is deliberate: Moses led an exodus, and the Lamb has led another. The church’s deliverance from the beast is an exodus too."],
k:"The conquerors sing the song of Moses and the Lamb: a new exodus, celebrated after the rescue."},
{h:"Smoke in the temple",
s:"Seven angels come out of the temple, and God’s glory fills it.",
t:["The seven angels receive seven bowls full of the wrath of God, and “the temple was filled with smoke from the glory of God and from his power, and no one could enter the temple until the seven plagues of the seven angels were finished” (Revelation 15:8).",
"This matches Exodus 40:34–35, when God’s glory filled the tabernacle so Moses could not enter, and 1 Kings 8:10–11 and Isaiah 6:4, when the temple filled with cloud or smoke.",
"The image means that God’s presence is so intense that no one can approach to intercede while the final judgment is carried out. It is a sign that the time for waiting is nearly over."],
k:"God’s glory fills the temple, and the time for these final plagues has come."},
{h:"Bowls 1–4",
s:"The bowls repeat the trumpet pattern, but now nothing is limited to a third.",
t:["The first bowl brings painful sores on those with the mark of the beast, like the boils in Exodus 9. The second turns the sea into blood and kills every living thing in it. The third turns rivers and springs to blood. The fourth lets the sun scorch people with fire (Revelation 16:2–9).",
"Compare the trumpets: a third of the sea became blood then, and now all of it. The warnings were serious, and now the judgment is complete.",
"The angel of the waters says, “Just are you, O Holy One... for they have shed the blood of saints and prophets, and you have given them blood to drink,” and the altar answers, “Yes, Lord God the Almighty, true and just are your judgments” (16:5–7). Heaven sees these plagues as justice."],
k:"The bowls are complete, not partial. Heaven says the judgments are true and just."},
{h:"They would not repent",
s:"The fourth bowl ends with the saddest sentence in the passage.",
t:["“They cursed the name of God who had power over these plagues. They did not repent and give him glory” (Revelation 16:9). They know where the plagues come from, and still they curse him.",
"This is like Pharaoh in Exodus, whose heart was hardened by each plague. It matches 9:20–21. Judgment on its own does not change a hard heart.",
"It also says something about the character of repentance. It is a gift, a turning, and requires humility. This is why the gospel of grace in the earlier chapters matters. God’s kindness, not just his power, is meant to lead people to repentance (Romans 2:4)."],
k:"Judgment alone does not soften a hardened heart. People can see God’s power and still refuse him."}
],
37:[
{h:"Darkness and the Euphrates",
s:"Bowls five and six hit the beast’s kingdom and prepare the way for the final battle.",
t:["The fifth bowl is poured on the throne of the beast, and “its kingdom was plunged into darkness” (Revelation 16:10). People gnaw their tongues in pain and curse God, but still do not repent (16:11). The darkness recalls the ninth plague on Egypt (Exodus 10:21–23).",
"The sixth bowl dries up the Euphrates “to prepare the way for the kings from the east” (16:12). A drying river recalls Cyrus, who in 539 BC diverted the Euphrates to enter Babylon (Isaiah 44:27–45:1).",
"Three unclean spirits like frogs come from the mouths of the dragon, the beast and the false prophet. They perform signs and gather the kings of the whole world “for the battle on the great day of God the Almighty” (16:13–14). Frogs were also an Egyptian plague."],
k:"The bowls bring darkness, a dried-up Euphrates and demonic spirits gathering the world’s kings."},
{h:"Armageddon",
s:"The name “Armageddon” is famous, and its meaning is more interesting than the movies suggest.",
t:["“And they assembled them at the place that in Hebrew is called Armageddon” (Revelation 16:16). The Hebrew is Har-Magedon, “Mount of Megiddo.” Megiddo was a fortress city overlooking the Jezreel Valley, a famous battleground: Barak and Deborah defeated Sisera nearby (Judges 4–5), and King Josiah was killed there (2 Kings 23:29; Zechariah 12:11).",
"No mountain called Megiddo exists, since it is a small hill (tell). Many readers take the name as a symbol of the final defeat of evil’s armies, with all the past battles gathered into one. Others expect a literal gathering in that region.",
"Notice what is missing: no battle is described in chapter 16. The armies assemble, but the fight is reported in 19:19–21."],
k:"Armageddon means “Mount of Megiddo,” a symbol of the place where evil gathers for its final defeat."},
{h:"“It is done”",
s:"The seventh bowl ends the plagues, and a voice from the throne announces completion.",
t:["The seventh angel pours his bowl into the air, and a loud voice from the throne says, “It is done!” (Revelation 16:17). There is lightning, thunder and the greatest earthquake ever, which splits the great city into three parts. “God remembered Babylon the great, to make her drain the cup of the fury of his wrath” (16:19). Islands and mountains vanish, and huge hailstones of about a hundred pounds fall (16:20–21). People curse God again.",
"On the cross, Jesus said, “It is finished” (John 19:30). The Greek words differ, but the sense is similar: the work is complete. The cross finished redemption, and the seventh bowl finishes judgment.",
"Babylon is mentioned but not described. Chapters 17–18 will explain who she is."],
k:"“It is done” marks the end of the plagues. Babylon is named, and the next chapters explain her."},
{h:"Gathered, but not yet fought",
s:"Chapter 16 gathers the armies, but nowhere does it describe a battle.",
t:["The spirits gather the kings of the whole world (Revelation 16:14), and the sixth bowl ends with them assembled at Har-Magedon (16:16). The seventh bowl then brings the earthquake and hail on Babylon. No fighting is described.",
"The actual clash is reported later in 19:19–21, where the beast and the kings gather to make war on the rider on the white horse and are defeated by the word from his mouth. There is also a final gathering of Gog and Magog in 20:7–9. Revelation often announces a conflict, pauses, and tells it later.",
"In the middle of the gathering, Jesus speaks: “Behold, I am coming like a thief! Blessed is the one who stays awake, keeping his garments on” (16:15). It is the third of the seven blessings. While the world’s kings assemble, Jesus tells his own people to stay awake and stay faithful."],
k:"The armies gather in chapter 16 but are defeated in chapter 19. In the middle, Jesus calls his people to stay awake."}
],
38:[
{h:"Two women",
s:"Revelation contrasts two women, and the contrast explains much of the book.",
t:["In chapter 12, a woman clothed with the sun gives birth to the Messiah and is protected by God. In chapter 17, a woman dressed in purple and scarlet sits on a beast. One is faithful, the other unfaithful. Later, a third picture completes the set: the Lamb’s bride, the new Jerusalem (19:7; 21:9).",
"The question for every reader is which woman they belong to. The pattern is a version of the Proverbs picture of two women calling to the simple: Lady Wisdom and the adulterous woman (Proverbs 9).",
"Revelation gives two cities as well, Babylon and Jerusalem, and the reader must choose which one to live in."],
k:"Two women, two cities: faithful or unfaithful. The book asks which one you belong to."},
{h:"The prostitute on the beast",
s:"One of the bowl angels shows John the judgment of the great prostitute.",
t:["“Come, I will show you the judgment of the great prostitute who is seated on many waters, with whom the kings of the earth have committed sexual immorality” (Revelation 17:1–2). She sits on a scarlet beast covered with blasphemous names, with seven heads and ten horns, the same beast as chapter 13. She is dressed in purple and scarlet, decked with gold and jewels, and holds a golden cup full of abominations (17:3–4).",
"On her forehead is written: “Babylon the great, mother of prostitutes and of earth’s abominations” (17:5). The word “mystery” in this verse means a hidden truth being revealed.",
"The woman rides the beast: a corrupt culture or system that depends on, and directs, political power."],
k:"The prostitute rides the beast: a seductive world system supported by political power."},
{h:"Drunk with the blood of the saints",
s:"She does two things at once: she seduces and she kills.",
t:["“And I saw the woman, drunk with the blood of the saints, the blood of the martyrs of Jesus. When I saw her, I marveled greatly” (Revelation 17:6). She seduces the kings with her luxury and kills those who refuse to bow.",
"Many empires work the same way: rewards for those who comply and violence for those who do not. That is why the picture of a prostitute fits. Like the beast, she tempts and threatens.",
"John is astonished. The angel then promises to explain the mystery of the woman and the beast (17:7), which we’ll look at tomorrow."],
k:"Babylon both seduces with luxury and kills those who refuse."},
{h:"Prophets and prostitutes",
s:"Calling a city a prostitute is not new. The prophets did it first.",
t:["Isaiah called Tyre a prostitute for selling herself to the nations for profit (Isaiah 23:15–17), Nahum called Nineveh a prostitute for her sorceries (Nahum 3:4), and Isaiah 1:21 and Ezekiel 16 and 23 used the same picture for Jerusalem herself when she turned from God.",
"The image is about faithfulness and loyalty. A city sells itself to power and profit, and gives its loyalty away. In this way Babylon in Revelation is not defined by geography but by behavior.",
"That is why the name “Babylon” can point both to the original Babylon, to Rome (Peter uses it that way in 1 Peter 5:13), and to any system that does the same."],
k:"The prophets used “prostitute” for cities that sold their loyalty for power and profit. Babylon is a pattern, not just a place."}
],
39:[
{h:"Seven hills, seven kings",
s:"The angel gives a clue that would have been obvious to John’s first readers.",
t:["“This calls for a mind with wisdom: the seven heads are seven mountains on which the woman is seated; they are also seven kings, five of whom have fallen, one is, the other has not yet come, and when he does come he must remain only a little while” (Revelation 17:9–10). Rome was famous as the city on seven hills, and Roman writers themselves said so.",
"The seven kings are harder. If they are Roman emperors, the count depends on where you start, and it has been used to date the book. If seven means completeness, they may stand for the whole sequence of oppressive powers. The beast is also called “an eighth” who “belongs to the seven” (17:11).",
"The first hearers would have recognized the hills at once. Beyond that, readers see Rome as the first example of a pattern that continues."],
k:"The seven hills point to Rome, and the seven kings are debated. Babylon is both a city and a pattern."},
{h:"Evil devours itself",
s:"The ten horns give power to the beast and then turn on the woman.",
t:["“And the ten horns that you saw are ten kings who have not yet received royal power, but they are to receive authority as kings for one hour, together with the beast. These are of one mind, and they hand over their power and authority to the beast” (Revelation 17:12–13). Then: “they will hate the prostitute. They will make her desolate and naked, and devour her flesh and burn her up with fire” (17:16).",
"Notice the irony. The system that fed on others is consumed by its own partners. Verse 17 says that “God has put it into their hearts to carry out his purpose.” Even evil, in its rebellion, ends up serving his plan.",
"The “waters” where the prostitute sits are “peoples and multitudes and nations and languages” (17:15). Her power depends on the people who support her, and it can collapse."],
k:"Babylon is destroyed by her own allies, and even that serves God’s purpose."},
{h:"The Lamb will conquer",
s:"In the middle of a frightening chapter, one verse settles the outcome.",
t:["“They will make war on the Lamb, and the Lamb will conquer them, for he is Lord of lords and King of kings, and those with him are called and chosen and faithful” (Revelation 17:14). The kings fight the Lamb, and the result is given in a single clause.",
"This is the pattern of the book: the powers are real, the war is real, and the outcome has never been in doubt. The title “Lord of lords and King of kings” is for the one who was slain.",
"The way Revelation is written is meant to remove fear. Before you read the details of Babylon’s fall in chapter 18, you already know who wins."],
k:"The Lamb conquers because he is Lord of lords and King of kings."},
{h:"Called, chosen and faithful",
s:"The same verse describes the people who stand with the Lamb.",
t:["“Those with him are called and chosen and faithful” (Revelation 17:14). Three words: God called them, God chose them, and they stayed faithful. The first two describe God’s action, and the third describes their response.",
"This is how Revelation holds together God’s sovereignty and human responsibility. Believers are not victorious because of their strength but because they belong to the one who conquers, and they are urged to keep faith.",
"It is a fitting summary for this section. When the world’s powers look overwhelming, the question is not whether you can defeat them but whether you will remain with the Lamb."],
k:"The Lamb’s people are called, chosen and faithful. They share his victory by staying with him."}
]
});
Object.assign(TEACH,{
40:[
{h:"“Fallen, fallen is Babylon”",
s:"Babylon’s fall was announced in chapter 14 and chapter 16. Now the details arrive.",
t:["A mighty angel lights up the earth with his glory and cries, “Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great! She has become a dwelling place for demons, a haunt for every unclean spirit, a haunt for every unclean bird” (Revelation 18:2). The words come from Isaiah 21:9, and the ruined-city picture from Isaiah 13:19–22, 34:11–15 and Jeremiah 50–51.",
"Babylon’s sin was that all the nations drank her wine of passion and that the merchants of the earth grew rich from her luxury (18:3). It is a picture of a culture that offers wealth and pleasure in exchange for loyalty.",
"Notice how total the ruin is: the place of glory becomes a place where only scavengers live. Whatever is built on pride and power does not last."],
k:"Babylon’s fall is described in the language of Isaiah and Jeremiah: a glorious city reduced to ruins."},
{h:"“Come out of her, my people”",
s:"In the middle of the judgment, a voice from heaven speaks to God’s own people.",
t:["“Come out of her, my people, lest you take part in her sins, lest you share in her plagues” (Revelation 18:4). It repeats Jeremiah 51:45 and Isaiah 48:20, where the exiles are told to flee Babylon.",
"What does coming out mean? Not leaving the world physically. Jesus prayed that his followers would stay in the world but be kept from the evil one (John 17:15), and Jeremiah told the exiles to seek the welfare of the city where they lived (Jeremiah 29:7). The call is to moral and spiritual separation: refuse her idolatry, her injustice and her greed.",
"The warning is practical. If a person shares in the system’s sins, they will share in its fall. God calls his people to be in the city but not of it."],
k:"“Come out” means refusing Babylon’s sins, not running away from the world."},
{h:"The cargo list",
s:"A long shopping list in the middle of a prophecy reveals what Babylon is really about.",
t:["“The merchants of the earth weep and mourn for her, since no one buys their cargo anymore, cargo of gold, silver, jewels, pearls, fine linen, purple cloth, silk, scarlet cloth, all kinds of scented wood, all kinds of articles of ivory... cinnamon, spice, incense, myrrh, frankincense, wine, oil, fine flour, wheat, cattle and sheep, horses and chariots, and slaves, that is, human souls” (Revelation 18:11–13). It follows Ezekiel 27’s list of Tyre’s trade.",
"Rome drew goods from across its empire, and its prosperity rested on slave labor. The list ends with the most horrifying item: “human souls.” The system that makes luxury treats people as merchandise.",
"It is a challenge to every age: where does our prosperity come from, and who pays for it?"],
k:"The last item on Babylon’s list is human beings. Luxury built on exploitation is part of what God judges."},
{h:"Who mourns and who rejoices",
s:"Three groups weep when Babylon falls, and heaven celebrates.",
t:["Kings mourn because they lost their partner in power. Merchants mourn because they lost their market. Sailors mourn because they lost their trade (Revelation 18:9–19). Each says the same thing: “in a single hour” it is gone.",
"Notice what they mourn: not her sin, only their loss. Their grief is selfish. They keep their distance, “standing far off in fear of her torment.”",
"But heaven answers: “Rejoice over her, O heaven, and you saints and apostles and prophets, for God has given judgment for you against her!” (18:20). A mighty angel throws a millstone into the sea, and her music, trade, light and weddings fall silent (18:21–23) because “in her was found the blood of prophets and of saints” (18:24)."],
k:"Those who profited weep for their loss, while heaven celebrates that justice has been done."}
],
41:[
{h:"The four Hallelujahs",
s:"The word “Hallelujah” appears in the New Testament only here, and it comes four times.",
t:["Hallelujah is Hebrew for “praise the LORD,” and it fills the Psalms. In the New Testament it appears only in Revelation 19:1, 3, 4 and 6. The great crowd in heaven shouts it after Babylon falls.",
"The reason for the praise is given: “his judgments are true and just; for he has judged the great prostitute who corrupted the earth with her immorality, and has avenged on her the blood of his servants” (19:2). The praise isn’t for cruelty but for the end of injustice.",
"The fourth Hallelujah announces: “For the Lord our God the Almighty reigns” (19:6). Everything in the book has been leading to this."],
k:"Heaven’s praise at Babylon’s fall shows that God’s judgments are true and just."},
{h:"Fine linen",
s:"The bride’s clothing is described, and its meaning is both a gift and a reflection of her life.",
t:["“It was granted her to clothe herself with fine linen, bright and pure”—for the fine linen is the righteous deeds of the saints (Revelation 19:8). The word “granted” means she is given it. Yet it is “the righteous deeds of the saints.”",
"Both are true. Paul says we are saved by grace as a gift, and we are created for good works that God prepared for us (Ephesians 2:8–10). The deeds are the evidence and the fruit of what he gives.",
"The contrast with Babylon is deliberate. She wore purple, scarlet, gold and jewels (17:4). The bride wears clean linen."],
k:"The bride’s clothing is a gift, and it shows up as righteous deeds."},
{h:"The marriage supper of the Lamb",
s:"The Bible’s story ends with a wedding.",
t:["“Let us rejoice and exult and give him glory, for the marriage of the Lamb has come, and his Bride has made herself ready” (Revelation 19:7). The prophets called Israel God’s bride (Isaiah 54:5–6; 62:5; Hosea 2:19–20), and Paul describes the church as Christ’s bride (Ephesians 5:25–32; 2 Corinthians 11:2). Jesus told parables about wedding feasts (Matthew 22:1–14; 25:1–13).",
"“Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb” (19:9) is the fourth of the seven blessings. It means that the end of history is a celebration, a shared meal with the one who loves his people.",
"The angel adds, “These are the true words of God.” Despite everything that came before, this is the book’s promise."],
k:"The goal of history is a wedding feast between Christ and his people."},
{h:"“Worship God”",
s:"John makes a mistake, and the angel’s correction is a lesson for every reader.",
t:["“Then I fell down at his feet to worship him, but he said to me, ‘You must not do that! I am a fellow servant with you and your brothers who hold to the testimony of Jesus. Worship God’” (Revelation 19:10). The same thing happens again in 22:8–9. Even John, overwhelmed by a glorious vision, is tempted to worship the messenger.",
"Angels, visions and even the book itself are not to be worshiped. They all point to God.",
"The last line is a guide for reading: “For the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.” All true prophecy is about Jesus. If a reading of Revelation is not centered on him, it has gone off track."],
k:"Only God is worshiped, and all genuine prophecy bears witness to Jesus."}
],
42:[
{h:"The Rider and his names",
s:"Heaven opens, and the one who appears has four different names.",
t:["“Then I saw heaven opened, and behold, a white horse! The one sitting on it is called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he judges and makes war” (Revelation 19:11). His eyes are like a flame of fire and he wears many diadems. He has a name written that no one knows but himself (19:12). He is called “The Word of God” (19:13), recalling John 1:1. On his robe and thigh is written “King of kings and Lord of lords” (19:16).",
"He is followed by armies in heaven on white horses, dressed in clean linen, and notice that they carry no weapons. The only weapon is “a sharp sword” that comes from his mouth, with which he strikes the nations and rules them with a rod of iron (19:15; Psalm 2:9).",
"Remember the first rider in chapter 6? This is the true Conqueror who is always faithful where beasts and imposters are not."],
k:"The Rider is Faithful and True, the Word of God, King of kings, and he conquers with his word."},
{h:"The robe dipped in blood",
s:"A detail that readers explain in two different ways.",
t:["“He is clothed in a robe dipped in blood” (Revelation 19:13). Isaiah 63:1–6 describes a warrior from Edom whose garments are stained from treading the winepress of his enemies. If John is echoing that, the blood is the blood of his foes.",
"But the robe is already bloody before the battle begins. For that reason many readers take it as his own blood, shed on the cross. He wins through sacrifice before he wins by judgment.",
"Both may be true. The one who judges is the one who died for the world. His victory has always been through the cross."],
k:"The bloody robe is either the blood of his enemies or his own blood from the cross. Both fit the one who conquers by sacrifice."},
{h:"The beast captured",
s:"The great battle is described in a single sentence, and the result is immediate.",
t:["The beast and the kings of the earth gather to make war on the Rider (Revelation 19:19). “And the beast was captured, and with it the false prophet... These two were thrown alive into the lake of fire that burns with sulfur” (19:20). The rest are killed by the sword that comes from his mouth (19:21).",
"No fight is described. The one with the sword of his word simply wins, as Paul said the Lord Jesus will destroy the lawless one “with the breath of his mouth” (2 Thessalonians 2:8).",
"The two beasts are the first to be thrown into the lake of fire, which comes up again in 20:10 and 20:14–15. The powers that terrorized God’s people are dealt with decisively."],
k:"The beast and false prophet are defeated by the Rider’s word, and no real contest takes place."},
{h:"Two suppers",
s:"Chapter 19 contains two meals, and everyone will be at one of them.",
t:["The first is the wedding supper of the Lamb (Revelation 19:9). The second is “the great supper of God,” where an angel calls the birds to eat the flesh of kings, commanders and warriors (19:17–18). The picture comes from Ezekiel 39:17–20, where birds feast after Gog is destroyed.",
"The contrast is stark. One is a table of joy, and the other is a scene of judgment. There is no third table.",
"The image is meant to wake the reader up. The invitation to the first meal is open (22:17), and the book is urging people to accept it."],
k:"Everyone is at the wedding supper of the Lamb or the great supper of God. The invitation to the first is still open."}
],
43:[
{h:"“The same way you saw him go”",
s:"The disciples are told by angels that Jesus will return, and the Greek word for it is worth knowing.",
t:["After Jesus ascends, two angels say, “This Jesus, who was taken up from you into heaven, will come in the same way as you saw him go into heaven” (Acts 1:11). The same Jesus, in the same bodily way, visibly. Revelation 1:7 echoes it: “every eye will see him.”",
"The word often used for his coming is parousia, which means “arrival” or “presence.” In the ancient world it was used for the formal visit of a king or emperor to a city.",
"Titus 2:11–14 calls it “our blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ.” For the New Testament writers it is the hope, not a topic to be afraid of."],
k:"Jesus will return personally and visibly. The New Testament calls it our blessed hope."},
{h:"The resurrection body",
s:"Paul explains what rising from the dead means, with a seed as his picture.",
t:["“So is it with the resurrection of the dead. What is sown is perishable; what is raised is imperishable... It is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body” (1 Corinthians 15:42–44). A seed and the plant are not identical, but they are connected. The body is continuous with the old one but transformed.",
"“Spiritual” here doesn’t mean made of nothing. It means animated by the Spirit. Jesus’ risen body could be touched and could eat (Luke 24:39–43), and yet it was no longer subject to death.",
"“We shall all be changed, in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet” (15:51–52). Paul ends with an instruction: stand firm, because your work in the Lord is not in vain (15:58)."],
k:"The resurrection body is the same person, transformed and no longer subject to death."},
{h:"Signs and suddenness",
s:"Jesus gives signs and also says he will come like a thief. Both are true for a reason.",
t:["On one side, Jesus listed signs in Matthew 24:4–14 and 24:29–30. On the other, he said the Son of Man comes at an hour you do not expect (24:44), and Paul says the day comes “like a thief in the night” (1 Thessalonians 5:2).",
"The signs keep believers awake and keep them from being alarmed. The suddenness prevents calculating a date. If either one were removed, people would either relax or start predicting.",
"Paul draws the practical conclusion: “you are not in darkness... So then let us not sleep, as others do, but let us keep awake and be sober” (5:4–6). He also introduces the passage as comfort for those grieving the dead (4:13–18)."],
k:"Signs call us to watch and suddenness prevents calculation. The result is staying awake."},
{h:"“Where is the promise of his coming?”",
s:"Peter predicted that people would mock the delay, and he gave an answer.",
t:["“Scoffers will come in the last days with scoffing, following their own sinful desires. They will say, ‘Where is the promise of his coming? For ever since the fathers fell asleep, all things are continuing as they were’” (2 Peter 3:3–4). Peter answers that God made the world and judged it with the flood, and the present world is being kept for judgment.",
"Then: “with the Lord one day is as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day” (3:8; Psalm 90:4). God does not measure time as we do. And: “The Lord is not slow to fulfill his promise... but is patient toward you, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should reach repentance” (3:9).",
"The delay is mercy. Peter ends with a question for us: “what sort of people ought you to be in lives of holiness and godliness?” (3:11)."],
k:"The delay of Jesus’ return is God’s patience, giving people time to repent."}
],
44:[
{h:"“Caught up”",
s:"Where does the word “rapture” come from, and what does the Bible actually say?",
t:["“Then we who are alive, who are left, will be caught up together with them in the clouds to meet the Lord in the air, and so we will always be with the Lord” (1 Thessalonians 4:17). “Caught up” translates the Greek harpazo, which the Latin Bible rendered as rapio, giving us “rapture.”",
"Paul writes to comfort grieving believers. The dead in Christ rise first, and then the living are gathered together with them. In 1 Corinthians 15:51–52, he says we will be changed “at the last trumpet.”",
"Everyone agrees on the facts: believers are united with Christ at his coming. The disagreement is over the timing and what happens next."],
k:"The “rapture” comes from the Latin for “caught up” in 1 Thessalonians 4:17. The comfort is that believers will be with the Lord forever."},
{h:"The main views",
s:"Christians who love Jesus and the Bible differ on when believers are caught up.",
t:["Pretribulation: believers are taken before a final period of tribulation, and Jesus returns visibly seven years later. Supporters point to texts like 1 Thessalonians 5:9 and Revelation 3:10. Midtribulation and pre-wrath views place it at points within that period. Posttribulation: believers are caught up at Christ’s visible return and meet him as he comes to earth, citing Matthew 24:29–31 and 2 Thessalonians 1:6–10.",
"Many Christians (amillennial and postmillennial traditions especially) see one return of Christ with no separate rapture, where the “meeting in the air” is the welcome of the returning king.",
"The strongest texts for each view, and the strongest objections to each, are in the panel below. Read them with an open mind. The pretribulation position became widely popular in the 1800s, and its supporters believe it is rooted earlier, while its critics see it as a later development."],
k:"Views differ on whether believers are caught up before, during or at the end of a final tribulation, or as part of one return."},
{h:"What everyone agrees on",
s:"Behind the disagreements lies a large area of agreement.",
t:["Every view says Christ will come back personally and visibly, the dead in Christ will rise, the living will be changed, and all believers will be with the Lord forever. Jesus said, “I will come again and will take you to myself, that where I am you may be also” (John 14:3). One of those passages Paul gave “to encourage one another” (1 Thessalonians 4:18).",
"All also agree that no one knows the day (Matthew 24:36–44). Jesus’ words about one taken and one left are about readiness.",
"Remember how much is shared. The things that unite are much bigger than the timing that divides."],
k:"All views agree on Christ’s return, the resurrection and being with the Lord forever."},
{h:"Conviction with humility",
s:"How should you hold a view on a topic where sincere believers differ?",
t:["Paul gave a principle in Romans 14: do not despise or quarrel over disputable matters, and each person should be fully convinced in his own mind (14:1–5). Prophecy is not exactly the same as the issues Paul described, but the principle applies.",
"Hold your view as firmly as the evidence supports, and no firmer. Be willing to say, “I might be wrong.” Treat people who disagree as fellow servants of Christ, not enemies.",
"The more important question is Titus 2:13: how do we live as people “waiting for our blessed hope”? Whatever your view, the aim is to be found faithful and ready."],
k:"Hold your view with conviction and humility. Sincere, careful readers land in different places."}
],
45:[
{h:"The angel with the key",
s:"Chapter 20 opens with an angel who has a chain and a key, and the dragon is stopped.",
t:["“Then I saw an angel coming down from heaven, having the key to the bottomless pit and a great chain in his hand. And he seized the dragon, that ancient serpent, who is the devil and Satan, and bound him for a thousand years, and threw him into the pit, and shut it and sealed it over him” (Revelation 20:1–3).",
"Notice the contrast with chapter 9, where a fallen star was “given” the key and opened the pit. Now the holder of the key is an angel from heaven, and a single angel can bind the great dragon. Satan is not an equal opponent.",
"All four of his names are used to make sure there is no doubt who is bound."],
k:"One angel binds Satan, and the key is in heaven’s hands."},
{h:"“That he might not deceive the nations”",
s:"What exactly is Satan prevented from doing?",
t:["He is bound “so that he might not deceive the nations any longer, until the thousand years were ended” (Revelation 20:3). The purpose is specific: his ability to deceive the nations is restrained. It does not say all his activity has stopped.",
"Elsewhere, Paul says that in past times God “overlooked” the times of ignorance of the nations, but now commands all people to repent (Acts 17:30). Jesus sent his disciples to all nations (Matthew 28:19). Many see the binding as the freedom for the gospel to reach the nations.",
"Peter says the devil still “prowls around like a roaring lion” (1 Peter 5:8), which some take as proof that binding has not yet happened, and others take as showing the binding is limited in what it prevents."],
k:"Satan is bound from deceiving the nations. How complete that is depends on how you read the passage."},
{h:"Is the binding under way?",
s:"Jesus used the picture of binding when he talked about his own ministry.",
t:["In Mark 3:27, he said no one can plunder a strong man’s house without first tying him up, after being accused of casting out demons by Satan. In Luke 10:17–20, when his disciples returned with joy that demons obeyed them, he said, “I saw Satan fall like lightning from heaven.” Colossians 2:15 says that on the cross Christ “disarmed the rulers and authorities.”",
"Those who read the thousand years as the present age say the binding began with the cross and resurrection. Those who see the thousand years as a future period say the binding is still to come and will be complete.",
"Either way, Scripture says Jesus has defeated Satan, and the dragon’s final fate is already certain."],
k:"Jesus spoke of binding the strong man. Readers differ on whether the binding of chapter 20 is already under way."},
{h:"“For a little while”",
s:"The binding comes with a time limit and an ending.",
t:["After this he “must be released for a little while” (Revelation 20:3). The word “must” shows that even Satan’s release is under God’s control. We will see in the next days what happens when he is released.",
"The thousand years may be literal or symbolic. A thousand is ten cubed, a number of completeness and great size. Psalm 90:4 and 2 Peter 3:8 remind us that a thousand years is a short time to God.",
"The release reveals something: after a long time without deception, many still follow him. It shows that the heart’s rebellion is not only caused by the devil’s influence."],
k:"Satan’s release is under God’s control, and what follows exposes the human heart."}
],
46:[
{h:"Thrones and judges",
s:"John sees thrones and people sitting on them.",
t:["“Then I saw thrones, and seated on them were those to whom the authority to judge was committed. Also I saw the souls of those who had been beheaded for the testimony of Jesus and for the word of God, and those who had not worshiped the beast or its image and had not received its mark on their foreheads or their hands” (Revelation 20:4). Daniel 7:22 and 27 say the saints receive judgment and the kingdom. Paul says believers will judge the world (1 Corinthians 6:2–3).",
"Two groups are named: those given authority to judge, and the martyrs who refused the beast. Some readers see these as the same group, with martyrs as the main example of all the faithful. Others see two separate groups.",
"The promise is: “if we endure, we will also reign with him” (2 Timothy 2:12; Romans 8:17)."],
k:"The faithful, especially those who died for Jesus, are given thrones and a share in his rule."},
{h:"“They came to life”",
s:"John says the faithful came to life and reigned with Christ for a thousand years, and then adds a puzzle.",
t:["“They came to life and reigned with Christ for a thousand years. The rest of the dead did not come to life until the thousand years were ended. This is the first resurrection” (Revelation 20:4–5).",
"The key question is what “came to life” means. The Greek verb is the same in both sentences. If it means bodily resurrection in the first sentence, the second does too. This is one reason premillennial readers say that the first resurrection is bodily. Others argue the meaning can shift, noting that Jesus spoke of an “hour that is coming, and is now here” when the dead hear his voice and live (John 5:24–29), a spiritual rising and a bodily one both.",
"Your reading of this verb largely shapes how you understand the millennium."],
k:"“Came to life” is the same verb twice. Whether it means bodily resurrection both times is the central question."},
{h:"Premillennial, amillennial and postmillennial",
s:"Three names are used for the main views of the thousand years.",
t:["Premillennial: Christ returns before the thousand years, believers are bodily raised, and Christ reigns on earth for a literal or long period before a final rebellion and the last judgment. Amillennial: the thousand years symbolize the present age. The first resurrection is spiritual new life in Christ or the souls of believers reigning with Christ in heaven, and Christ’s return ends history. Postmillennial: the gospel progressively succeeds and brings a long era of blessing, after which Christ returns.",
"Each view has Bible texts it relies on and objections it must answer. The panel on the page lays them out.",
"Notice that all three agree on the key points: Christ will return, the dead will be raised, there will be a final judgment and a new creation."],
k:"Pre-, a- and postmillennial views differ about timing and nature of the thousand years, not about Christ’s return."},
{h:"Blessed and holy",
s:"The passage ends with a promise that doesn’t depend on which view you take.",
t:["“Blessed and holy is the one who shares in the first resurrection! Over such the second death has no power, but they will be priests of God and of Christ, and they will reign with him for a thousand years” (Revelation 20:6). This is the fifth of the seven blessings.",
"The second death is defined in 20:14 as the lake of fire. Believers will not face it. They are also called priests, as in Exodus 19:6 and Revelation 1:6 and 5:10.",
"The point is that those who are loyal to Christ now will be with him then. Whatever the timing, belonging to Jesus is what counts."],
k:"Those who share in Christ’s resurrection are safe from the second death and serve as priests."}
],
47:[
{h:"One last rebellion",
s:"After a thousand years, Satan is released and attacks again.",
t:["“And when the thousand years are ended, Satan will be released from his prison and will come out to deceive the nations that are at the four corners of the earth, that is, Gog and Magog, to gather them for battle; their number is like the sand of the sea” (Revelation 20:7–8). “Four corners” means the whole earth, and “sand of the sea” is the Bible’s picture of a huge number (Genesis 22:17).",
"The rebellion shows that even after a long time of peace, hearts can still turn against God. The problem is not only outside influence but human sin.",
"Notice also that the enemy gathers “for battle” but nothing like a real battle follows. As in chapter 19, the defeat is quick."],
k:"The final rebellion comes from the whole earth, and it ends almost immediately."},
{h:"Gog and Magog",
s:"Revelation borrows names from Ezekiel, and it is worth knowing where they come from.",
t:["In Ezekiel 38–39, Gog of the land of Magog, chief prince of Meshech and Tubal, comes from the far north with a huge army to attack restored Israel. God destroys them with earthquake, plague, fire and hail. Magog is also in Genesis 10:2 as a son of Japheth.",
"Revelation takes the names and applies them to “the nations that are at the four corners of the earth.” That means it is not a single country. Attempts to match Gog to a particular modern nation are guesses that the text doesn’t support.",
"As with Babylon, John uses an Old Testament name as a pattern for the world’s hostility against God’s people."],
k:"Gog and Magog come from Ezekiel and stand for the nations of the whole world in rebellion."},
{h:"One battle or two?",
s:"Revelation has two battles that look very similar, and readers disagree about how to relate them.",
t:["In 19:19–21, the beast and the kings gather against the Rider and are destroyed. In 20:8–9, Satan gathers the nations against the saints and fire destroys them. Both draw on Ezekiel 38–39: the birds’ feast in 19:17–18 comes from Ezekiel 39:17–20, and the names Gog and Magog appear in 20:8.",
"Premillennial readers usually see two separate battles, one before and one after the millennium. Amillennial readers usually see one pattern told twice, since Revelation often repeats the same story from different angles.",
"You do not have to decide today. Notice the pattern: evil gathers, God acts, and evil is gone."],
k:"Revelation 19 and 20 describe similar battles. Some see two events and others see one told twice."},
{h:"Fire from heaven",
s:"The devil’s story ends here.",
t:["“And fire came down from heaven and consumed them, and the devil who had deceived them was thrown into the lake of fire and sulfur where the beast and the false prophet were, and they will be tormented day and night forever and ever” (Revelation 20:9–10). Fire from heaven recalls Sodom (Genesis 19), Elijah (2 Kings 1) and Ezekiel 38:22.",
"The devil joins the beast and the false prophet, who were thrown in earlier. The three who led the rebellion against God and his people meet the same end.",
"The deceiver’s career ends. Evil is not a permanent rival of God. It has a beginning in Genesis 3 and a final end here."],
k:"The devil is thrown into the lake of fire. Evil has a beginning and an end."}
],
48:[
{h:"Earth and sky flee",
s:"The final judgment begins with the whole creation giving way.",
t:["“Then I saw a great white throne and him who was seated on it. From his presence earth and sky fled away, and no place was found for them” (Revelation 20:11). White speaks of purity and justice. Nothing can stand before him.",
"Jesus said the Father has given all judgment to the Son (John 5:22), and Paul told the Athenians that God “has fixed a day on which he will judge the world in righteousness by a man whom he has appointed” (Acts 17:31). The judge is Jesus himself.",
"Peter says the heavens and earth will be dissolved with fire (2 Peter 3:10). This opens the way for the new heaven and earth in chapter 21."],
k:"The final judgment happens before the great white throne, and the judge is the Lamb."},
{h:"Two sets of books",
s:"People are judged by their deeds, and yet the book of life decides who is saved. How do these fit together?",
t:["“And books were opened. Then another book was opened, which is the book of life. And the dead were judged by what was written in the books, according to what they had done” (Revelation 20:12). Deeds are recorded and matter. Paul says all believers must appear before Christ’s judgment seat (2 Corinthians 5:10).",
"Yet the book of life is the deciding factor. Those whose names are written there are safe (3:5; 21:27; Daniel 12:1). Jesus said, “Whoever hears my word and believes him who sent me has eternal life. He does not come into judgment, but has passed from death to life” (John 5:24).",
"Works show where a person stands. They are the fruit of faith (Ephesians 2:8–10; James 2:17). Salvation is by grace, and real faith produces real life."],
k:"Deeds are judged, and the book of life records those who belong to the Lamb. Grace saves and works show it."},
{h:"Death itself dies",
s:"The last enemy is destroyed.",
t:["“And the sea gave up the dead who were in it, Death and Hades gave up the dead who were in them, and they were judged, each one of them, according to what they had done. Then Death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire. This is the second death, the lake of fire” (Revelation 20:13–14).",
"Death is pictured as a ruler, and Hades as its servant. Both are cast out. Paul said in 1 Corinthians 15:26, “The last enemy to be destroyed is death.”",
"For people facing martyrdom, this was the most important promise: death does not have the last word. It will itself be destroyed."],
k:"Death and Hades are thrown into the lake of fire. Death is the last enemy to be destroyed."},
{h:"Justice, not vengeance",
s:"The great white throne can be frightening, but it is also good news.",
t:["Every wrong, including every secret one, will be seen and answered (Ecclesiastes 12:14; Romans 2:16). People who were never believed, never rescued or never avenged will have their story told by the one who sees everything.",
"This is justice, not vengeance. God does not enjoy punishment (Ezekiel 33:11). The judge has also been the one who bore sin on the cross.",
"Go back to the question of Day 20: “Who can stand?” The answer is those whose names are in the Lamb’s book of life. That is the message of Revelation: you can stand, because of the one who stood in your place."],
k:"God’s judgment is justice for every wrong. Those in the Lamb’s book can stand."}
],
49:[
{h:"Being with Christ now",
s:"What happens between a believer’s death and the resurrection?",
t:["Paul says, “My desire is to depart and be with Christ, for that is far better” (Philippians 1:23). Jesus told the thief on the cross, “Today you will be with me in Paradise” (Luke 23:43). Paul also says that to be “away from the body” is to be “at home with the Lord” (2 Corinthians 5:8). In Revelation 6:9–11 the martyrs are at the altar and told to rest.",
"This is called the intermediate state. Most Christians hold that believers are consciously with Christ. A smaller group hold that the dead are unconscious until the resurrection, and that “sleep” in Scripture is literal.",
"Both agree that the intermediate state is not the end of the story, which leads to the next point."],
k:"After death, believers are with Christ. This is better than life now, but it is a waiting state."},
{h:"Resurrection is the goal",
s:"The Bible’s great hope is not floating souls, but renewed bodies in a renewed world.",
t:["Paul says we “wait eagerly for adoption as sons, the redemption of our bodies” (Romans 8:23) and that Christ “will transform our lowly body to be like his glorious body” (Philippians 3:20–21). In 2 Corinthians 5:2–4, he says we do not want to be “unclothed” but “further clothed.”",
"This is why Revelation ends with a new heaven and earth and not with souls in the sky. The intermediate state is real but incomplete. Think of it as waiting, not arriving.",
"If you ever hear that the goal is “going to heaven forever,” remember that the Bible’s last chapters describe heaven coming down to a renewed earth (Revelation 21:2)."],
k:"The goal is bodily resurrection in a new creation. The intermediate state is a waiting room."},
{h:"Sheol, Hades and Gehenna",
s:"Three different words are all translated “hell” in some versions, and each has a different meaning.",
t:["Sheol is the Hebrew word for the realm of the dead, the grave (Genesis 37:35; Psalm 16:10). Hades is the Greek equivalent (Acts 2:27, 31; Revelation 1:18; 20:13–14). Gehenna comes from the Hebrew for “Valley of Hinnom,” south of Jerusalem, where children were once sacrificed (2 Kings 23:10; Jeremiah 7:31–32) and later rubbish was burned. Jesus used it as an image of final judgment (Mark 9:43–48; Matthew 10:28).",
"Luke 16:19–31, the story of the rich man and Lazarus, places Lazarus at “Abraham’s side” and the rich man in torment in Hades. Whether to read it as a parable or a glimpse into the afterlife is debated. Its main lesson is that choices matter now and Scripture is enough to warn us.",
"Knowing which word is used helps you read each verse more accurately."],
k:"Sheol and Hades mean the realm of the dead. Gehenna is Jesus’ image of final judgment."},
{h:"Final destinies",
s:"This is a sober topic. Jesus spoke about it often, and Christians understand it in different ways.",
t:["Jesus said that the final division leads to “eternal punishment” or “eternal life” (Matthew 25:46). Revelation 20:14–15 and 21:8 describe the lake of fire as the second death. All Christians agree that Jesus spoke of real judgment, that God is both just and loving, and that he is not willing that any should perish (2 Peter 3:9).",
"Three views exist on its nature. The traditional view is conscious, unending punishment. Another holds that the lost are finally destroyed and cease to exist (“conditional immortality”), pointing to words like destruction in Matthew 10:28, Romans 6:23 and 2 Thessalonians 1:9. A small minority hope that all will finally be saved. The panel on the page lays out the arguments for each.",
"Whatever view you hold, the right response is to take the warnings seriously and to share the good news with compassion."],
k:"Jesus spoke of real judgment. Christians differ on its nature, but all say God is just and loving."}
],
50:[
{h:"No more sea",
s:"The first thing John notices about the new creation is something that is missing.",
t:["“Then I saw a new heaven and a new earth, for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away, and the sea was no more” (Revelation 21:1). The language comes from Isaiah 65:17 and 66:22.",
"In Scripture the sea often stands for chaos and threat (Daniel 7:2–3; Revelation 13:1), and it was the sea that separated John from his churches on Patmos. It was the home of the beast. “No more sea” means that evil, chaos and separation are gone.",
"It does not mean there will be no oceans. It means nothing dangerous or divisive remains."],
k:"“No more sea” means the end of chaos, evil and separation."},
{h:"Renewal, not replacement",
s:"“Behold, I am making all things new” can mean two different things, and one is better supported.",
t:["“And he who was seated on the throne said, ‘Behold, I am making all things new’” (Revelation 21:5). The Greek word for new here often means new in quality rather than new in time. The same thing is said of Jesus’ resurrection body: it is the old body made new.",
"Paul says “the creation itself will be set free from its bondage to corruption and obtain the freedom of the glory of the children of God” (Romans 8:21). Peter speaks of “new heavens and a new earth in which righteousness dwells” (2 Peter 3:13), and Acts 3:21 of “the time for restoring all the things.”",
"The pattern is that God does not throw away his creation. He redeems it, as he did Jesus’ body in the empty tomb."],
k:"God renews creation rather than discarding it, the way he raised Jesus’ body."},
{h:"God lives with his people",
s:"The promise at the center of the Bible is that God will live among his people.",
t:["“Behold, the dwelling place of God is with man. He will dwell with them, and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God. He will wipe away every tear from their eyes, and death shall be no more, neither shall there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain anymore, for the former things have passed away” (Revelation 21:3–4).",
"The language gathers a whole story: God walking in Eden (Genesis 3:8), the tabernacle (Leviticus 26:11–12), the covenant promise “I will be their God, and they shall be my people” (Ezekiel 37:27), and the Word who “dwelt among us” (John 1:14). The tears promise comes from Isaiah 25:8.",
"“It is done!” (21:6) answers the same words in 16:17. And the water of life is free, as in Isaiah 55:1."],
k:"The Bible’s great promise, God with his people, is finally fulfilled and every tear is wiped away."},
{h:"The overcomer and the outsiders",
s:"The section ends with a promise and a warning, and the warning is aimed at people inside the church.",
t:["“The one who conquers will have this heritage, and I will be his God and he will be my son” (Revelation 21:7). It takes a promise God made to David’s son (2 Samuel 7:14) and gives it to every believer.",
"Then: “But as for the cowardly, the faithless, the detestable, as for murderers, the sexually immoral, sorcerers, idolaters, and all liars, their portion will be in the lake that burns with fire and sulfur, which is the second death” (21:8). The first group named is the cowardly, those who abandon Christ under pressure, which was exactly the temptation facing the seven churches.",
"The list is a warning to keep going, not an excuse for despair. Jesus restored Peter after he denied him three times."],
k:"The overcomer becomes a child of God. The warnings are given so that believers will persevere."}
],
51:[
{h:"The bride is a city",
s:"John is shown the bride and sees a city. People and place are one picture.",
t:["One of the bowl angels says, “Come, I will show you the Bride, the wife of the Lamb,” and carries John to a high mountain where he sees “the holy city Jerusalem coming down out of heaven from God” (Revelation 21:9–10). The same angel and the same invitation appeared in 17:1, when he showed John the great prostitute.",
"Two women and two cities, side by side: Babylon and the new Jerusalem. One is a world that sells itself for power and profit. The other is a people who belong to the Lamb.",
"The city comes down from heaven. It is not a human achievement but a gift from God."],
k:"The bride and the city are one picture. The book contrasts Babylon with the new Jerusalem."},
{h:"A cube with twelve gates",
s:"The city’s shape and measurements are full of meaning.",
t:["The city is a perfect cube, about 12,000 stadia (roughly 1,400 miles or 2,200 km) in each direction (Revelation 21:16). The Most Holy Place in Solomon’s temple was also a cube (1 Kings 6:20). The whole city is the Most Holy Place, where God’s presence dwells.",
"It has twelve gates with the names of the twelve tribes of Israel (Ezekiel 48:30–35) and twelve foundations with the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb (21:12–14). Both the old and new covenant people are included.",
"The numbers are twelve and its multiples, the number for God’s people. The measurements are about completeness and access."],
k:"The cube echoes the Most Holy Place, and the gates and foundations unite Israel and the apostles."},
{h:"Jewels and gold",
s:"The city’s materials are meant to dazzle, and each links to something earlier in the Bible.",
t:["The wall is jasper, and the city is pure gold, “like clear glass” (Revelation 21:18). The foundations are decorated with twelve jewels (21:19–20), recalling Isaiah 54:11–12 and the twelve stones on the high priest’s breastplate (Exodus 28:17–20). Each gate is a single pearl, and the street is gold.",
"Contrast Babylon, whose wealth was gained by exploitation (chapter 18). The new city’s glory is God’s own. “The city has no need of sun or moon to shine on it, for the glory of God gives it light, and its lamp is the Lamb” (21:23).",
"The picture makes the whole city priestly. Everything is clean and bright, and everyone has access."],
k:"The city’s jewels echo the high priest’s breastplate, and its light is God’s own glory."},
{h:"No temple, open gates",
s:"The most surprising detail is what is not there.",
t:["“And I saw no temple in the city, for its temple is the Lord God the Almighty and the Lamb” (Revelation 21:22). The temple was the place where God and people met. Now there is no need for a building because God himself is present.",
"The gates are never shut (21:25; Isaiah 60:11), and “the kings of the earth will bring their glory into it” (21:24; Isaiah 60). The redeemed bring the best of the nations’ cultures and treasures, cleansed, into the city. “But nothing unclean will ever enter it... but only those who are written in the Lamb’s book of life” (21:27).",
"The invitation is wide open, and the book of life is the only requirement."],
k:"God and the Lamb are the temple. The gates are open to all whose names are in the Lamb’s book."}
],
52:[
{h:"The river of life",
s:"The Bible ends with a river that starts in the throne room.",
t:["“Then the angel showed me the river of the water of life, bright as crystal, flowing from the throne of God and of the Lamb through the middle of the street of the city” (Revelation 22:1–2). In Genesis 2:10, a river flowed out of Eden. Ezekiel 47:1–12 saw a river flowing from the temple, bringing life wherever it went, and Psalm 46:4 speaks of “a river whose streams make glad the city of God.”",
"Jesus said, “If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink” and John explains that he meant the Spirit (John 7:37–39).",
"The source is the throne itself. Life flows out from God and the Lamb to everyone."],
k:"The river of life flows from God’s throne through the whole city. God is the source of all life."},
{h:"The tree of life",
s:"Access to the tree, lost in Genesis 3, is open again.",
t:["“On either side of the river, the tree of life with its twelve kinds of fruit, yielding its fruit each month. The leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations” (Revelation 22:2). In Genesis 3:22–24, humans were driven from the tree and angels guarded the way. Now, “blessed are those who wash their robes, so that they may have the right to the tree of life” (22:14). It is the promise to Ephesus (2:7).",
"“No longer will there be anything accursed” (22:3), the reversal of the curse in Genesis 3:17.",
"The “healing of the nations” does not mean sickness exists but that all division and hostility between peoples is healed."],
k:"The tree of life is open to God’s people, the curse is gone, and the nations are healed."},
{h:"“They will see his face”",
s:"Everything in the Bible has been leading to this one sentence.",
t:["“They will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads” (Revelation 22:4). In Exodus 33:20, God told Moses, “you cannot see my face, for man shall not see me and live.” Jesus said the pure in heart will see God (Matthew 5:8), John says “we shall see him as he is” (1 John 3:2), and Paul says we will know “face to face” (1 Corinthians 13:12).",
"The name on their foreheads is the opposite of the beast’s mark. It says they belong to God, as in 3:12 and 14:1.",
"This is the highest hope of the Bible: not just a place without pain, but to know God directly."],
k:"God’s people will see his face and belong to him forever. This is the Bible’s ultimate hope."},
{h:"Reigning forever",
s:"The vision ends with God’s people serving and ruling together.",
t:["“And night will be no more. They will need no light of lamp or sun, for the Lord God will be their light, and they will reign forever and ever” (Revelation 22:5). Verse 3 says “his servants will worship him.” Serving and reigning go together.",
"This restores the human calling in Genesis 1:26–28, to rule over creation as God’s image-bearers. It fulfills Daniel 7:18, 27, and Revelation 5:10. Paul said, “if we endure, we will also reign with him” (2 Timothy 2:12).",
"Reigning in the new creation does not mean domination. It means caring for the world as God intended. The garden of Eden becomes a garden city, finished and secure."],
k:"God’s people serve and reign forever, restoring the human calling from Genesis 1."}
],
53:[
{h:"“I am coming soon”",
s:"Jesus says three times that he is coming soon, and the church has asked for 2,000 years what that means.",
t:["The phrase appears in Revelation 22:7, 12 and 20. The Greek word (tachy) can mean quickly, suddenly or soon. Readers take it three ways: imminent, meaning it could happen at any time; sudden, meaning that when it happens it will be quick; or certain, meaning it is sure to happen.",
"2 Peter 3:8–9 reminds us that God’s sense of time differs from ours. For John’s readers, it meant living in constant readiness. Notice also that in 22:10, John is told not to seal up the words of the book, “for the time is near.” In contrast, Daniel was told to seal his book (Daniel 12:4).",
"“Behold, I am coming soon, bringing my recompense with me, to repay each one for what he has done” (22:12). The statement is a motivation to live faithfully now."],
k:"“Soon” means ready now, whether it is imminent, sudden or certain. Jesus’ coming is the motivation for faithful living."},
{h:"The last blessings",
s:"Revelation’s seven blessings end with a call to take the book seriously.",
t:["“Blessed is the one who keeps the words of the prophecy of this book” (Revelation 22:7) is the sixth of the seven blessings, and 22:14 is the seventh: “Blessed are those who wash their robes, so that they may have the right to the tree of life and that they may enter the city by the gates.”",
"Compare the first in 1:3: read, hear and keep. The book opens and closes with the same instruction. The goal is obedience, not decoding.",
"The robes washed in the Lamb’s blood (7:14) are what give entry to the city. The book ends where it started: with those who belong to Jesus and live faithfully."],
k:"The book opens and closes with a blessing for those who keep its words and wash their robes."},
{h:"The open invitation",
s:"Revelation ends with a free offer to anyone who is thirsty.",
t:["“The Spirit and the Bride say, ‘Come.’ And let the one who hears say, ‘Come.’ And let the one who is thirsty come; let the one who desires take the water of life without price” (Revelation 22:17). The Spirit and the church are praying “Come” to Jesus, and also inviting the world to come.",
"“Without price” echoes Isaiah 55:1, “Come, everyone who thirsts, come to the waters... buy wine and milk without money and without price,” and John 7:37.",
"The final chapter of the Bible is an invitation, and everyone who reads it is invited. It is also a commission: those who hear are to say “Come.”"],
k:"The Bible ends with a free invitation to anyone who is thirsty."},
{h:"“Come, Lord Jesus”",
s:"The final warning and the final prayer.",
t:["“If anyone adds to them, God will add to him the plagues described in this book, and if anyone takes away from the words of the book of this prophecy, God will take away his share in the tree of life” (Revelation 22:18–19). Similar warnings guard Moses’ teaching in Deuteronomy 4:2 and 12:32. This is about deliberately distorting the message, not about sincere mistakes by readers.",
"Jesus says, “Surely I am coming soon,” and the book replies, “Amen. Come, Lord Jesus!” (22:20). This is the same prayer as the Aramaic “Maranatha” in 1 Corinthians 16:22, probably among the earliest Christian prayers.",
"The last words are, “The grace of the Lord Jesus be with all” (22:21). The Bible’s final word is grace."],
k:"The book ends with a warning to respect it, the prayer “Come, Lord Jesus,” and grace."}
],
54:[
{h:"“All Israel will be saved”",
s:"Romans 9–11 is the main passage on Israel’s future, and Christians read Romans 11:26 in three ways.",
t:["Paul first says “not all who are descended from Israel belong to Israel” (Romans 9:6–8). He insists God has not rejected his people (11:1–2). A “partial hardening has come upon Israel, until the fullness of the Gentiles has come in. And in this way all Israel will be saved” (11:25–26). He adds, “the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable” (11:29).",
"Three readings: a large-scale future turning of the Jewish people to Christ; all the elect, Jews and Gentiles together, as the “Israel of God” (Galatians 6:16); or the full number of Jews who believe throughout history. The phrase “in this way” (houtos) is about how, not just when.",
"All three agree that God keeps his promises and is merciful: “God has consigned all to disobedience, that he may have mercy on all” (11:32)."],
k:"Paul says God has not abandoned Israel. Readers differ on how “all Israel will be saved.”"},
{h:"Dry bones and a new heart",
s:"Ezekiel’s vision of restoration shapes how the New Testament talks about new life.",
t:["In Ezekiel 37:1–14, the prophet sees a valley full of dry bones, which stand for exiled Israel who say, “Our bones are dried up, and our hope is lost.” God’s breath enters them and they live. “I will put my Spirit within you, and you shall live” (37:14). Earlier, God promises, “I will give you a new heart, and a new spirit I will put within you” (36:26).",
"The New Testament echoes this. Jesus told Nicodemus, a teacher of Israel, that he must be born of “water and the Spirit” (John 3:5), the same promise. In Revelation 11:11, the breath of life enters the two witnesses.",
"The lesson is that restoration is God’s work, from start to finish. Dry bones cannot revive themselves."],
k:"Ezekiel’s dry bones show that God restores his people by his Spirit. It is his work from start to finish."},
{h:"The land promises",
s:"God promised Abraham’s descendants a land. Christians disagree about how that promise is fulfilled.",
t:["God promised Abraham the land of Canaan as an everlasting possession (Genesis 12:1; 15:18; 17:8). Some Christians hold that this still awaits fulfillment for the Jewish people, based on Romans 11. Others hold that it was fulfilled in Joshua’s time (Joshua 21:43–45) and now broadens in Christ to the whole new creation. They point to Romans 4:13, where Abraham is “heir of the world,” Hebrews 11:8–16, where Abraham looks for a better country, and Matthew 5:5, where the meek inherit the earth.",
"This app lays out the views without choosing for you. Notice also that what the Bible says about the land is a separate question from modern politics, and no reading justifies treating any group of people badly.",
"Every view says that God’s promises to Abraham are certain and that Jesus is the heir."],
k:"Christians read the land promises as still to come for Israel, or as fulfilled in Christ and expanded to the new creation."},
{h:"The olive tree",
s:"Paul gives a picture of how Jews and Gentiles belong together in God’s plan.",
t:["In Romans 11:17–24, Israel is a cultivated olive tree. Some natural branches were broken off because of unbelief, and Gentiles, a wild olive shoot, were grafted in among the rest and now share “the nourishing root.” Paul warns, “Do not be arrogant toward the branches... it is not you who support the root, but the root that supports you.”",
"Paul also says God is able to graft the natural branches in again if they do not continue in unbelief (11:23–24). The picture shows God’s kindness and severity.",
"Galatians 3:26–29 and Ephesians 2:11–22 say the same: in Christ there is one people, one new humanity, rooted in Israel’s story. There is no place for pride or contempt toward the Jewish people."],
k:"Gentile believers are grafted into Israel’s root. There is one people of God and no room for pride."}
],
55:[
{h:"Four temples",
s:"The Bible tells a story of temples, and knowing the timeline helps with Revelation.",
t:["The tabernacle was a tent (Exodus 25–40). Solomon built the first temple around 960 BC, and Babylon destroyed it in 586 BC. Zerubbabel’s second temple was finished around 516 BC (Ezra 6:15). Herod enlarged it hugely beginning around 20 BC, and Rome destroyed it in AD 70, as Jesus had predicted (Matthew 24:2).",
"There has been no Jewish temple since. Today the site in Jerusalem holds the Dome of the Rock and the al-Aqsa Mosque.",
"Different readings of Revelation 11:1–2 and 2 Thessalonians 2:4 depend on whether they think of a future physical temple, the church or the first-century temple. Keep the timeline in mind."],
k:"The temple story moves from the tabernacle, to Solomon, to Zerubbabel, to Herod, and to its destruction in AD 70."},
{h:"The new temple is a person and a people",
s:"The New Testament says Jesus fulfills the temple, and his people become God’s dwelling.",
t:["Jesus said, “Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up,” and John explains, “he was speaking about the temple of his body” (John 2:19–21). He also said that “something greater than the temple is here” (Matthew 12:6). When he died, the temple curtain was torn in two (Matthew 27:51).",
"Believers are now God’s temple: “Do you not know that you are God’s temple and that God’s Spirit dwells in you?” (1 Corinthians 3:16; Ephesians 2:19–22; 1 Peter 2:5). Hebrews 8–10 says the earthly temple was a shadow of the heavenly reality, and Christ’s sacrifice was once for all (Hebrews 10:10–14).",
"The temple’s purpose, God living with his people, is now carried out in Jesus and his people."],
k:"Jesus is the true temple, and his people are now God’s dwelling place."},
{h:"Why there is no temple in the new Jerusalem",
s:"John says he saw no temple in the city, which is surprising for a Jewish writer.",
t:["“I saw no temple in the city, for its temple is the Lord God the Almighty and the Lamb” (Revelation 21:22). A temple was needed when God’s presence had to be protected and approached through priests and sacrifices.",
"In the new Jerusalem, the whole city is shaped like the Most Holy Place (a perfect cube) and God’s presence fills it. Everyone has direct access. There is no distinction between sacred and ordinary because everything is sacred.",
"This is also the fulfillment of 7:15, where the multitude serves God “in his temple,” and of 3:12, where the believer becomes a pillar in the temple. The temple has become the whole place."],
k:"There is no temple because God’s presence fills the whole city."},
{h:"Ezekiel’s temple",
s:"The longest temple description in the Bible has been understood in at least three ways.",
t:["Ezekiel 40–48 describes a visionary temple in detail: a man with a measuring line (40:1–5), God’s glory returning (43:1–9), a river flowing out (47), and the city named “The LORD is there” (48:35). It describes priests, sacrifices and land division.",
"Three views: a literal temple to be built in the future, sometimes with memorial sacrifices; a plan for the returning exiles that was conditional on their obedience and never built in that exact form; or a symbolic vision of God dwelling with his people, fulfilled in Christ and the church and completed in the new creation. Revelation 21–22 echoes it: the high mountain, the river, and the city’s name.",
"The panel on the page lays out the views. Notice how often the New Testament uses Ezekiel’s images for something larger than a building."],
k:"Ezekiel’s temple is read as literal and future, as a conditional plan, or as a symbol of God’s presence fulfilled in Christ."}
]
});
Object.assign(TEACH,{
56:[
{h:"Core and secondary",
s:"It helps to separate what every Christian view of Revelation shares from what is up for discussion.",
t:["Every major view agrees that Christ will return bodily, the dead will be raised, there will be a final judgment, and creation will be made new. The great creeds of the church affirm those things without prescribing a detailed timeline.",
"The disagreements are about the timing and meaning of the tribulation, the thousand years, and the place of Israel. These are important, and also secondary to the core.",
"A useful habit is to ask of any claim: is this something the whole church holds, or is it one view among several? That question alone prevents a great deal of confusion."],
k:"Everyone agrees on Christ’s return, resurrection, judgment and new creation. The rest are secondary questions."},
{h:"Using the chart and the panel",
s:"The page gives you two comparison tools. Here is how to use them well.",
t:["The first sets the three millennial views side by side: premillennial, amillennial and postmillennial. The second sets the four ways to read the whole book: preterist, historicist, futurist and idealist.",
"Try this exercise with Revelation 20:1–6. For each view, ask three questions. When does it happen? What is the binding of Satan? What is the first resurrection? Writing the answers side by side shows you where the real differences lie.",
"A useful proverb applies: “The one who states his case first seems right, until the other comes and examines him” (Proverbs 18:17). Always read each view at its strongest."],
k:"Use the chart to compare how each view answers the same questions about the same text."},
{h:"Build your own chart",
s:"The aim is not to pick a team but to be honest about how sure you are.",
t:["Make three columns: what I am confident about, what I lean toward, and what I hold loosely. In the first, you might put: Jesus is alive and reigns, the Lamb wins, God will make all things new. In the second, a view of the millennium or tribulation you currently find more convincing. In the third, everything else.",
"Next to each item, write the verse that most influences you. That keeps your confidence tied to Scripture and not to a favorite teacher.",
"Come back to the chart in a year. If the middle and last columns have moved, that is growth, not failure."],
k:"List what you’re confident about, what you lean toward and what you hold loosely, with a verse for each."},
{h:"Three misreadings to avoid",
s:"Revelation has been misused in three common ways.",
t:["Date-setting: Jesus said, “It is not for you to know times or seasons that the Father has fixed by his own authority” (Acts 1:7; Matthew 24:36). Every predicted date has passed.",
"Newspaper-reading: matching verses to current headlines. Every generation has done it, with names and nations that later proved wrong. It makes Revelation useless to the people who first received it.",
"Fear-mongering: the book’s first words from Jesus are “Fear not” (1:17), and he says it again to Smyrna (2:10). If a reading of Revelation leaves you more anxious and less loving, it has probably missed the point."],
k:"Avoid date-setting, headline-matching and fear-mongering. Revelation is written to strengthen, not to scare."}
],
57:[
{h:"The structure at a glance",
s:"The whole book can be mapped in thirteen parts, and four phrases show its main divisions.",
t:["The parts are: prologue (1:1–8), letters (1:9–3:22), throne (4–5), seals (6:1–8:5), trumpets (8:6–11:19), conflict (12–14), bowls (15–16), Babylon (17–18), the return (19), the millennium (20:1–6), final rebellion and judgment (20:7–15), new creation (21:1–22:5) and epilogue (22:6–21).",
"Four times John says he was “in the Spirit” (1:10; 4:2; 17:3; 21:10), and these moments mark the book’s four main visions. The first is the risen Christ and the churches, the second heaven and earth’s judgments, the third Babylon, and the fourth the new Jerusalem.",
"The table below shows the full outline. Keep it in mind when you read any single passage, so you know where it sits."],
k:"Knowing where a passage sits on the map makes it much easier to understand."},
{h:"Parallels across the book",
s:"The same scenes and themes return, which is one of the most important clues for how the book works.",
t:["Martyrs appear at 6:9 and 20:4. The throne is central in chapters 4, 20 and 22. The lake of fire appears in 19–21. The cosmic finale of lightning, thunder and earthquake closes the seals, trumpets and bowls (8:5; 11:19; 16:18).",
"The fall of Babylon is announced in 14:8, enacted in 16:19 and described at length in 17–18. Judgment and rescue appear in pairs: for example, the sealed multitude after the sixth seal and the two witnesses after the sixth trumpet.",
"These repetitions suggest that John is circling the same story from different angles, each time with more detail. They are also what makes the idea of recapitulation plausible."],
k:"Repeated scenes, such as the martyrs, the throne and the cosmic finale, suggest that John returns to the same story from new angles."},
{h:"Recurring characters and numbers",
s:"Learning the cast and the numbers is like learning the alphabet of the book.",
t:["Characters: the Lamb, the dragon, the two beasts, the woman, and the bride. Two cities: Babylon and the new Jerusalem. Two women: the prostitute and the bride. Two groups: those marked with God’s seal and those with the beast’s mark.",
"Numbers: seven (completeness), twelve (God’s people), three and a half (a limited time, also 42 months and 1,260 days), a thousand (a great span), and 666 (the number of a man).",
"Once you recognize them, a passage that looked strange often turns out to be a familiar character in a familiar role."],
k:"The book reuses a small cast and a few numbers, and learning them unlocks much of it."},
{h:"A line, a spiral or a mix",
s:"Readers disagree about how the visions relate in time, and the choice shapes the rest of one’s interpretation.",
t:["A straight line: the visions follow one another in time, usually with chapters 6–19 describing a future period before Christ’s return. This is common among futurist readers.",
"A spiral: the book has several parallel sections (letters, seals, trumpets, bowls and so on) that each cover the whole time between the first and second coming, growing more intense each time. This is typical of idealist and many amillennial readers.",
"A mix: some parts recap earlier ones and others move forward. Many readers find that both patterns appear. The map helps you see which parts are clearly parallel and which seem sequential."],
k:"Revelation may be a line, a spiral or a mix. The map helps you test each idea."}
],
58:[
{h:"The book’s commands",
s:"Behind all the visions are a small number of instructions for the reader.",
t:["Worship: “Worship God” (Revelation 19:10; 4–5; 14:7). Endure: the call for the endurance of the saints (13:10; 14:12). Come out of Babylon (18:4). Overcome: each letter ends with a promise to the one who conquers (chapters 2–3). Watch: “Blessed is the one who stays awake” (16:15; 3:3). Repent (2:5, 16, 21; 3:3, 19). Testify: they conquered “by the word of their testimony” (12:11).",
"Each command comes with a promise. The book is not only telling people what to do; it is showing them who they are following and where it ends.",
"If you remember the seven verbs, you have the book’s practical shape."],
k:"Worship, endure, come out, overcome, watch, repent and testify. These are the book’s seven instructions."},
{h:"Comfort, not conspiracy",
s:"The first readers heard Revelation as a source of strength.",
t:["The church in Asia was facing pressure to compromise and fear of arrest. Peter wrote to similar believers, “Do not be surprised at the fiery trial when it comes upon you” (1 Peter 4:12). Hebrews 10:32–39 reminds readers of earlier suffering and urges endurance.",
"Revelation answers: the Lamb is on the throne, he holds the keys of death, the beast is limited, and the story ends with God wiping away every tear (21:4). Paul gives the same message in Romans 8:35–39: nothing can separate us from the love of Christ.",
"When you use Revelation to comfort someone, you are using it the way it was first used."],
k:"Revelation was written to comfort suffering believers, not to feed speculation."},
{h:"Common traps",
s:"Three traps catch many readers, and each is avoidable.",
t:["Fear: Jesus’ first words to John are “Fear not” (Revelation 1:17). A reading that makes you afraid is not following the book’s own tone.",
"Obsession with “who”: spending all one’s energy identifying the beast or the date. Jesus warned about those who say, “Look, here is the Christ!” (Matthew 24:23–26). Deuteronomy 29:29 reminds us that “the secret things belong to the LORD our God, but the things that are revealed belong to us and to our children, that we may do all the words of this law.”",
"Ignoring “how to live”: knowing every detail of the timeline and not changing how you live is exactly what the seven letters rebuke."],
k:"Avoid fear, endless speculation about “who,” and ignoring “how to live.”"},
{h:"A life aimed at the coming King",
s:"The New Testament explains what a life shaped by Jesus’ return looks like.",
t:["Titus 2:11–14: grace trains us “to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age, waiting for our blessed hope.” 2 Peter 3:11–14: “what sort of people ought you to be in lives of holiness and godliness.” 1 John 3:2–3: “Everyone who thus hopes in him purifies himself as he is pure.”",
"The letters in chapters 2–3 praise very practical things: love, service, faithfulness, endurance, keeping his word. A life shaped by Revelation is faithful in small things (Luke 16:10), generous, honest, worshipful and hopeful.",
"The best way to prepare for the end is to be faithful today."],
k:"Hope in Christ’s return shapes a life that is faithful, generous, honest, worshipful and hopeful."}
],
59:[
{h:"Reading your summary",
s:"Day 60 gathers your own words from the whole course, and there is a way to read them.",
t:["Your one-sentence summaries from each day are compiled below. Read them straight through once without stopping to edit. You are looking for patterns: which words keep appearing, such as Lamb, throne, overcome, witness, or the Old Testament sources.",
"Notice which days came easily and which felt hard. Hard days often point to areas worth revisiting, and easy days show where your understanding is strongest.",
"This summary is yours. It is a record of how your understanding grew over these weeks."],
k:"Read your summaries once for patterns: repeated words, easy days and hard days."},
{h:"The teach-it-back challenge",
s:"The best test of understanding is explaining something simply to someone else.",
t:["Teaching it back shows you gaps you did not know you had. Try this three-minute outline. First, what it is: Jesus’ unveiling, written to persecuted churches, in symbols drawn from the Old Testament. Second, the story: letters, the throne, the Lamb and the scroll, judgments as warnings, the conflict with the dragon and beasts, Babylon’s fall, Christ’s return, final judgment, and the new creation. Third, so what: worship, endure, stay faithful, and hope.",
"Then write about 200 words in the box at the bottom. Short is good. If you cannot say it simply, go back to the day that confuses you.",
"Use plain words. If a friend would be lost by a term, explain it or skip it."],
k:"Explain Revelation in three minutes: what it is, the story, and why it matters. Then write it in 200 words."},
{h:"Celebrate",
s:"You have walked a long road, and it is worth noticing.",
t:["You started with the risen Christ among the lampstands and finished at the tree of life. Along the way you met the throne, the Lamb, the seals, trumpets and bowls, the dragon and the beasts, Babylon, the return of the King and a new creation.",
"The book began with a blessing for anyone who reads, hears and keeps its words (1:3). By taking the time to learn it, you have done the first part.",
"This is a beginning, not a finish. Come back to the Bible’s own text, revisit Daniel and Ezekiel, and read Revelation straight through once more. It reads very differently now."],
k:"You have gone from the risen Christ to the tree of life. This is a solid beginning."}
],
60:[
{h:"From Lion to Lamb",
s:"The central surprise of the book deserves a closer look.",
t:["In Revelation 5:5–6, John is told to look at the Lion of Judah (Genesis 49:9–10) and turns to see a Lamb “as though it had been slain.” The word “conquered” in verse 5 is the same one used later of believers (3:21; 12:11). Jesus’ victory is won by sacrifice, and that is the pattern his people follow.",
"Both pictures are true. He is the Lion because he truly rules, and the Lamb because he won by giving himself. The cross does not interrupt his power. It is how it works.",
"This changes what strength means. In a world that admires domination, Revelation says the strongest one is the one who was slain."],
k:"The Lion conquers as the Lamb. The cross is how Jesus’ power works."},
{h:"The Lamb’s many roles",
s:"“Lamb” is Revelation’s favorite title for Jesus, and following it through the book tells a story.",
t:["The Greek word arnion appears about 28 times in Revelation for Jesus, far more than any other title. He is slain (5:6, 9, 12; 13:8), opens the scroll (5:7), shares worship with the Father (5:13), shepherds his people (7:17), marries the bride (19:7), and is the temple and lamp of the new city (21:22–23).",
"Sacrifice, sovereignty, care, love and light are all contained in one title. At every stage of the story, the Lamb is at the center.",
"If you ever lose your way in the book, ask where the Lamb is. He is almost always at the heart of the scene."],
k:"The Lamb is slain, reigns, shepherds, marries and lights the new city. Follow him through the book."},
{h:"Not a helpless victim",
s:"The Lamb’s strange appearance shows he is anything but weak.",
t:["The Lamb has seven horns and seven eyes (Revelation 5:6), which are “the seven spirits of God sent out into all the earth,” echoing Zechariah 4:10. Horns are power, eyes are knowledge, and seven means complete.",
"He is also “standing,” not lying on an altar. The picture is of one who was slain and is alive. He has all the power and awareness needed to carry out God’s plan.",
"The Lamb’s gentleness is chosen, not forced. He could have used power in other ways, and he chose to win by love."],
k:"The Lamb has complete power and knowledge, and he chose sacrifice."},
{h:"The Passover Lamb and the Servant",
s:"The Lamb’s Old Testament roots show what his death accomplished.",
t:["At the first Passover, a lamb’s blood on the doorposts meant death passed over the house (Exodus 12:1–13). Isaiah’s Servant was “led like a lamb to the slaughter” bearing others’ sins (Isaiah 53:7). Abraham had said, “God will provide for himself the lamb” (Genesis 22:8). John the Baptist said, “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” (John 1:29).",
"The Lamb is both a deliverer who protects from judgment and a substitute who bears sin. Revelation says those who belong to him have been ransomed (5:9).",
"All these threads come together in one person."],
k:"The Lamb is the Passover deliverer and the suffering Servant who bears sin."},
{h:"The wrath of the Lamb",
s:"The most surprising phrase in the book puts gentleness and anger together.",
t:["The kings of the earth hide from “the wrath of the Lamb” (Revelation 6:16). It is not contradictory. Mercy that is refused becomes judgment, and love that is real is angry at what destroys what it loves.",
"Notice how believers conquer. They conquer “by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony” and follow the Lamb “wherever he goes” (12:11; 14:4). They are never told to take up swords. Their victory is faithful witness, even to death.",
"The Lamb’s way is the way of the church: overcoming evil without becoming like it."],
k:"The Lamb’s wrath is love opposing what destroys. His people conquer by faithful witness, not force."},
{h:"Counterfeits",
s:"The dragon and the beasts imitate the Lamb at almost every point, and that tells us something.",
t:["The dragon has seven heads, where the Lamb has seven horns (12:3). The beast has a head that was wounded and lives, as the Lamb was slain yet lives (13:3; 5:6). The beast’s mark copies God’s seal (13:16; 7:3). The second beast has “two horns like a lamb” (13:11). The dragon, beast and false prophet form a false trinity (16:13).",
"Evil cannot create; it only imitates. One good way to tell the real from the counterfeit is to ask what each asks of you.",
"The Lamb gives himself for you. The beast demands that you give yourself to it."],
k:"The dragon and beasts imitate the Lamb. The Lamb gives himself, and the beast demands you."}
],
61:[
{h:"Four names",
s:"Revelation 12:9 gives the enemy four names, and each says something about how he works.",
t:["The great dragon: a monstrous, fearsome creature. The ancient serpent: recalling Genesis 3:1, the deceiver in Eden. The devil: from the Greek diabolos, “slanderer.” Satan: from the Hebrew for “adversary,” as in Job 1:6 and Zechariah 3.",
"Together the names say terror, deceit, slander and opposition. He is also called “the deceiver of the whole world.”",
"Knowing the enemy’s names takes away some of his mystery. Revelation does not want us fascinated by him, only informed."],
k:"The enemy’s four names describe his work: terrify, deceive, slander and oppose."},
{h:"The Accuser",
s:"Satan’s special role is to accuse God’s people before God, and the Bible shows how he is answered.",
t:["In Job 1–2, Satan stands before God to challenge Job. In Zechariah 3:1–5, he accuses the high priest Joshua, who stands in filthy clothes. God rebukes Satan and says, “Behold, I have taken your iniquity away from you.” Revelation 12:10 says he accuses “day and night.”",
"The answer is in 12:11: the blood of the Lamb. Paul asks, “Who shall bring any charge against God’s elect? It is God who justifies” (Romans 8:33–34), and John says we have “an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous” (1 John 2:1).",
"A believer’s confidence is not that they have no failures but that every charge has already been answered."],
k:"Satan accuses, but the Lamb’s blood answers every charge."},
{h:"A parody of the Lamb",
s:"The dragon’s appearance is an imitation of Jesus’ own.",
t:["Seven heads, ten horns and seven crowns (Revelation 12:3) mimic the Lamb’s seven horns and eyes. His red color points to violence and bloodshed. His tail sweeps a third of the stars to earth (12:4), language from Daniel 8:10 about pride and persecution.",
"Paul says “Satan disguises himself as an angel of light” (2 Corinthians 11:14). Evil often looks like a shortcut to what God offers, and gets it wrong.",
"The result is a counterfeit trinity in 16:13: the dragon, the beast and the false prophet."],
k:"The dragon imitates the Lamb with his own heads, horns and crowns. He copies, and he cannot create."},
{h:"Defeat, not stalemate",
s:"Revelation never presents the devil as God’s equal.",
t:["Follow his path: he is thrown down (Revelation 12:9), he “knows that his time is short” (12:12), he is bound (20:2–3), released briefly (20:7), and finally cast into the lake of fire (20:10). Every step is a step down.",
"This is not dualism, the idea that good and evil are two equal forces. One angel can bind him. Jesus said, “now will the ruler of this world be cast out” (John 12:31), and John writes, “The reason the Son of God appeared was to destroy the works of the devil” (1 John 3:8).",
"The war is real, but the outcome is not in doubt."],
k:"The devil’s path in Revelation goes only downward, from thrown down to bound to the lake of fire."},
{h:"Three weapons",
s:"The saints defeat Satan with three things, and none of them is a sword.",
t:["“They have conquered him by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony, for they loved not their lives even unto death” (Revelation 12:11). The blood of the Lamb answers every accusation. The word of their testimony is speaking truth about Jesus. Not loving their lives means staying loyal even if it costs everything.",
"Paul describes similar spiritual resources in Ephesians 6:10–18, such as truth, righteousness, faith, the word of God and prayer. James says, “Resist the devil, and he will flee from you” (James 4:7).",
"These weapons are quiet and costly. They work because they are the same way Jesus won."],
k:"The saints overcome by the Lamb’s blood, their testimony and loyalty even in death."},
{h:"Deceit is his method",
s:"The devil’s main tactic is lying, and Revelation keeps returning to it.",
t:["He “deceives the whole world” (Revelation 12:9; 20:3, 8). The same method appears in Eden, where he twisted God’s word with “Did God really say...?” (Genesis 3:1–5). Jesus called him “a liar and the father of lies” (John 8:44).",
"Violence usually serves deception, as with the beast’s image that kills those who will not worship it (13:15).",
"The best defense is knowing the truth. When Jesus was tempted in the wilderness, he answered each lie with Scripture (Matthew 4:1–11)."],
k:"The devil deceives, as he did in Eden. The defense is knowing and holding to the truth."}
],
62:[
{h:"The empires converge",
s:"The beast from the sea is built from Daniel’s four beasts, and the order matters.",
t:["In Daniel 7:3–7, four beasts rise from the sea: a lion, a bear, a leopard and a terrible fourth. In Revelation 13:2, the beast looks like a leopard, with feet like a bear and a mouth like a lion. Everything the earlier beasts did is gathered into one.",
"The sea suggests restless nations (Isaiah 57:20; Revelation 17:15). The beast is not simply one government but a recurring pattern: political power that makes itself an object of worship.",
"Each empire in history has some of these features, which is why many readers think the beast can apply to several powers while pointing to a final one."],
k:"The beast gathers the features of Daniel’s empires. It is a pattern that recurs in history."},
{h:"Seven heads and ten horns",
s:"Chapters 13 and 17 should be read together.",
t:["Revelation 13:1 gives the beast seven heads and ten horns, and 17:9–14 explains: the seven heads are seven hills and seven kings, and the ten horns are ten kings who receive power for “one hour” with the beast. Daniel 7:7 and 24 also describe ten horns.",
"In chapter 13, the beast is the political power. In chapter 17, it carries the prostitute, Babylon. They are the same beast seen from different angles.",
"The crowns are on the horns in chapter 13 and on the dragon’s heads in chapter 12, a small difference that may show the beast’s authority is received, not original."],
k:"Chapters 13 and 17 describe the same beast from different angles."},
{h:"Blasphemous names",
s:"The beast’s names reflect real claims made about Roman emperors.",
t:["“On its heads were blasphemous names” (Revelation 13:1). Augustus was styled “son of the divine,” “savior” and “lord.” An inscription from Priene in Asia Minor praises his birthday as the beginning of “good news” for the world. Domitian liked to be addressed as “lord and god.”",
"To John’s readers, these were not harmless honors. They were claims that belong to Jesus alone.",
"Revelation does not accuse Rome of ordinary politics. It accuses the empire of demanding worship."],
k:"The beast’s blasphemous names echo the divine titles claimed by Roman emperors."},
{h:"A mock resurrection",
s:"The beast copies the Lamb’s death and resurrection, and the crowd falls for it.",
t:["One head had “a mortal wound, but its mortal wound was healed” and the whole earth followed (Revelation 13:3). The crowd asks, “Who is like the beast?” (13:4), an echo of “Who is like you, O LORD?” (Exodus 15:11; Micah 7:18).",
"After Nero died in AD 68, rumors spread that he would return, and several pretenders appeared in the east. Some think John may be echoing this legend, though it is debated.",
"Whatever its origin, the point is that the beast fakes what only Jesus actually did."],
k:"The healed wound mocks the Lamb’s resurrection, and “Who is like the beast?” mocks “Who is like God?”"},
{h:"The register of the Lamb",
s:"The beast’s power is real, limited and countered by a different list.",
t:["He is given authority for forty-two months and makes war on the saints (Revelation 13:5–7). Again the language is “given.” Those who worship him are those “whose name has not been written before the foundation of the world in the book of life of the Lamb who was slain” (13:8).",
"The book of life appears at 3:5, 13:8, 17:8, 20:12–15 and 21:27. It is the Lamb’s register of citizens, the opposite of the beast’s followers.",
"The response is “the endurance and faith of the saints” (13:10), an echo of Jeremiah 15:2. Endurance here means loyalty, not violence."],
k:"The beast’s power is limited, and God’s people are enrolled in the Lamb’s book of life."},
{h:"The number 666",
s:"A closer look at the most famous number in the book.",
t:["In Hebrew, “Nero Caesar” is written nrwn qsr. The letter values are 50, 200, 6, 50, 100, 60 and 200, and they add up to 666. Without the final “n,” it is nrw qsr, which adds up to 616. Some ancient manuscripts, including Papyrus 115, read 616.",
"Using letters as numbers (gematria) was common in the ancient world. A graffito in Pompeii even says, “I love her whose number is 545.”",
"Other solutions have been proposed, and none can be proved. The text calls it “the number of a man,” which says the beast is human power, not something supernatural."],
k:"666 can be calculated from “Nero Caesar” in Hebrew letters, and 616 from a variant spelling. Other solutions are also proposed."}
],
63:[
{h:"A lamb’s look and a dragon’s voice",
s:"The second beast is an imposter, and Jesus warned about exactly this.",
t:["“It had two horns like a lamb and it spoke like a dragon” (Revelation 13:11). Jesus warned, “Beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep’s clothing but inwardly are ravenous wolves” (Matthew 7:15). Paul said false apostles “disguise themselves as apostles of Christ” (2 Corinthians 11:13–15).",
"He looks like Christ and speaks for Satan. The test is not appearance but message. John says to test the spirits by whether they confess that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh (1 John 4:1–3).",
"This beast serves the first beast’s authority. It is religion in service to political power."],
k:"The second beast looks gentle and speaks for the dragon. Test every message against Jesus."},
{h:"A false trinity",
s:"The dragon, the beast and the false prophet form a parody of the Father, Son and Spirit.",
t:["The dragon gives authority to the beast, as the Father gives it to the Son. The beast dies and appears to live. The false prophet directs worship to the beast, as the Spirit points to Christ (John 16:14). In 16:13, three unclean spirits come from the mouths of all three.",
"The label “counterfeit trinity” is an interpretive one, but the pattern is clear. Each member points to the next, and none points to God.",
"All three end together: the beast and false prophet in the lake of fire (19:20) and the dragon after them (20:10)."],
k:"The dragon, beast and false prophet imitate the Father, Son and Spirit, and end up together in the lake of fire."},
{h:"Signs prove nothing",
s:"The false prophet performs real-looking miracles, and the Bible says how to judge them.",
t:["He “performs great signs, even making fire come down from heaven” (Revelation 13:13), an imitation of Elijah on Mount Carmel (1 Kings 18:38). Jesus warned that false prophets “will show great signs and wonders, so as to lead astray, if possible, even the elect” (Matthew 24:24).",
"Deuteronomy 13:1–5 says that even if a prophet’s sign comes true, if he leads people away from the LORD, he is to be rejected. The test is loyalty to God, not power.",
"Miracles alone never prove a message is true. What the message says about Jesus does."],
k:"Signs alone never prove truth. The test is whether the message leads to Jesus."},
{h:"The image and the economy",
s:"The false prophet enforces worship through fear and economic pressure.",
t:["He makes an image of the beast that seems to speak and has people killed who do not worship it (Revelation 13:14–15). He also requires a mark so that “no one can buy or sell unless he has the mark” (13:17). In the Roman world, trade guilds and markets were tied to the worship of patron deities and the emperor, as in Thyatira.",
"Refusing could cost a person their income and their place in society. The pressure was social and economic, as well as political.",
"The pattern is not confined to one era. Wherever loyalty to something other than God is made the price of belonging, the same dynamic applies."],
k:"The false prophet enforces worship by threats and economic pressure."},
{h:"The mark and the seal",
s:"The mark on the hand and forehead is a deliberate copy of something God’s people already have.",
t:["God told Israel to bind his words on their hands and foreheads (Deuteronomy 6:8; 11:18). In Ezekiel 9:4, God marks those who grieve over sin. God’s servants are sealed on their foreheads in Revelation 7:3, and the Father’s name is on the 144,000 in 14:1 and on all his people in 22:4.",
"The beast’s mark copies that. The hand suggests actions and the forehead suggests thoughts, so it means total allegiance. Readers differ on whether it is a literal mark or a symbol.",
"Either way, there is no neutral ground. One belongs to the Lamb or to the beast."],
k:"The mark copies God’s seal. It means ownership and allegiance, and there is no neutral position."},
{h:"Named at last",
s:"This figure is not called the false prophet until later, and his fate is shared.",
t:["The second beast is named “the false prophet” in 16:13, 19:20 and 20:10. John often reveals the true identity of a figure only later in the story.",
"In 19:20 we learn that he “had done the signs by which he deceived those who had received the mark of the beast.” He is thrown alive into the lake of fire with the beast. In 20:10, the devil joins them.",
"Jesus said, “You will recognize them by their fruits” (Matthew 7:16). The false prophet’s fruit is deceit and the end is judgment."],
k:"The second beast is revealed as the false prophet and shares the beast’s fate."}
],
64:[
{h:"Sun, moon and twelve stars",
s:"The woman’s crown of stars points back to Joseph’s dream and Israel’s tribes.",
t:["“A woman clothed with the sun, with the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars” (Revelation 12:1). In Genesis 37:9–11, Joseph dreamed that the sun, moon and eleven stars bowed to him. They stood for his father, mother and brothers, the family of Israel.",
"Twelve stars suggest the twelve tribes, and possibly the twelve apostles as well, since 21:12–14 names both. Being clothed with the sun is a picture of glory.",
"She is a symbol of God’s people throughout history, glorious in his eyes even when she appears vulnerable."],
k:"The woman’s crown of twelve stars recalls Joseph’s dream and the twelve tribes."},
{h:"Labor and the child",
s:"Israel’s long labor produced the Messiah.",
t:["She cries out in labor (Revelation 12:2). Isaiah 66:7–9 describes Zion giving birth, and Micah 5:2–3 says, “until the time when she who is in labor has given birth.” The child will “rule all the nations with a rod of iron” (12:5; Psalm 2:9).",
"The pain suggests the long suffering and longing of Israel as she waited. Paul says that from Israel comes “the Christ, who is God over all” (Romans 9:5).",
"The scene shows a people in pain bringing forth hope for the world."],
k:"Israel’s long labor brought forth the Messiah, who will rule the nations."},
{h:"Caught up to God",
s:"One verse covers the whole story of Jesus, and the dragon fails.",
t:["“Her child was caught up to God and to his throne” (Revelation 12:5). The verse skips the ministry, the cross and the resurrection and jumps to the ascension and enthronement. Psalm 110:1 says, “Sit at my right hand.”",
"Behind earthly rulers, Satan had tried repeatedly to stop the child: Herod’s slaughter of the infants (Matthew 2), the temptations in the wilderness (Matthew 4) and the betrayal of Judas (Luke 22:3).",
"The dragon’s plan fails. The child reaches the throne."],
k:"The child is taken up to God’s throne. The dragon’s plan fails."},
{h:"The wilderness and the eagle’s wings",
s:"The woman’s flight shows how God protects his people when they suffer.",
t:["She flees to a place “prepared by God” where she is nourished for 1,260 days (Revelation 12:6) and is given “the two wings of the great eagle” (12:14). In Exodus 19:4, God says, “I bore you on eagles’ wings and brought you to myself.” Hosea 2:14 says God will lead his people into the wilderness and speak tenderly to them.",
"The wilderness is a place of danger and also a place of provision and intimacy. Readers see it as the time of the church’s whole history, the flight of Jerusalem’s Christians to Pella before AD 70, or a future period for Israel.",
"What is clear is that God’s people are hunted but not abandoned, and for a limited time."],
k:"God protects and feeds his people in the wilderness for a limited time."},
{h:"The rest of her offspring",
s:"The dragon’s final target tells you who the woman’s children are.",
t:["He goes to make war on “the rest of her offspring, those who keep the commandments of God and hold to the testimony of Jesus” (Revelation 12:17). That means the church.",
"The woman is both the people from whom the Messiah came and the mother of those who follow him. Paul says, “the Jerusalem above... is our mother” (Galatians 4:26).",
"Jesus told his followers that the world that hated him would hate them (John 15:18–20). The church and the Messiah share the same enemy and the same victory."],
k:"The woman’s other children are the church, who share Jesus’ enemy and his victory."},
{h:"Genesis 3:15 replayed",
s:"The whole chapter is the fulfillment of the first promise in the Bible.",
t:["In Genesis 3:15, God told the serpent, “I will put enmity between you and the woman, and between your offspring and her offspring; he shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise his heel.” Revelation 12 shows exactly that: the serpent against the woman and her offspring.",
"The “offspring” is both singular, Christ (Galatians 3:16), and plural, his people: “The God of peace will soon crush Satan under your feet” (Romans 16:20).",
"The story runs from Genesis 3 to Revelation 12 to Revelation 20. A promise made at the beginning is kept at the end."],
k:"Revelation 12 is Genesis 3:15 coming true: the serpent is defeated by the woman’s offspring."}
],
65:[
{h:"“Who is like God?”",
s:"Michael’s name is a question, and it is the question the beast’s followers mock.",
t:["Michael means “Who is like God?” in Hebrew. Jude 9 calls him the archangel, the only angel given that title by name. In Daniel he is “one of the chief princes” and “the great prince who has charge of your people” (Daniel 10:13, 21; 12:1).",
"In Revelation 13:4, the crowd asks, “Who is like the beast?” The beast’s followers ask the opposite of what Michael’s name asks.",
"The name is a quiet reminder that no creature can be compared to God."],
k:"Michael’s name asks “Who is like God?”, and the beast’s crowd asks the opposite."},
{h:"The war and the weapon",
s:"The great angelic battle is decided by something that happened on earth.",
t:["“Michael and his angels fighting against the dragon” (Revelation 12:7). But the verse that explains the victory is 12:11: “they have conquered him by the blood of the Lamb.” The angels fight, and the cross decides.",
"This shows that the battle’s real turning point was the death and resurrection of Jesus. Michael’s role is a part of the victory, not the cause.",
"It also protects against making too much of the angels. The center is always the Lamb."],
k:"Michael fights, but the Lamb’s blood decides the war."},
{h:"“The Lord rebuke you”",
s:"Jude gives a glimpse of Michael’s humility.",
t:["“When the archangel Michael, contending with the devil, was disputing about the body of Moses, he did not presume to pronounce a blasphemous judgment, but said, ‘The Lord rebuke you’” (Jude 9). The same words appear in Zechariah 3:2.",
"Even Michael did not rely on his own authority. His power is borrowed from God, and that is why he wins.",
"Jude uses this to warn against casual boasting about spiritual powers (Jude 8–10). Respect for the unseen realm is not fear, but humility."],
k:"Even Michael relied on the Lord’s authority. Spiritual power is always borrowed from God."},
{h:"Do not worship angels",
s:"Twice John bows to the wrong person.",
t:["In Revelation 19:10 and 22:8–9, John falls to worship the angel who shows him the visions, and each time the angel says, “I am a fellow servant... Worship God.” Paul also warned against worshiping angels (Colossians 2:18).",
"Hebrews 1:4–14 sets the Son far above the angels, who are “ministering spirits sent out to serve for the sake of those who are to inherit salvation” (1:14).",
"Angels are servants, like us. Impressive messengers still point beyond themselves."],
k:"Angels are fellow servants and are never to be worshiped."},
{h:"Angels everywhere",
s:"Revelation is full of angels doing God’s work.",
t:["There are angels for the seven churches (1:20), four angels holding the winds (7:1), seven angels with trumpets (8:2), an angel with the eternal gospel (14:6), seven angels with bowls (15:1), an angel with the chain (20:1), and an angel with the measuring rod (21:15).",
"Most are unnamed and appear only for a task. They are the instruments through which God governs events. Psalm 103:20 says they “do his word,” and Hebrews 12:22 speaks of “innumerable angels.”",
"The Bible shows them in service, not as objects of curiosity."],
k:"Angels in Revelation serve God’s purposes. Most are unnamed, because the focus is on God."},
{h:"The archangel’s voice",
s:"One verse about Jesus’ return mentions an archangel, but not by name.",
t:["“The Lord himself will descend from heaven with a cry of command, with the voice of an archangel, and with the sound of the trumpet of God” (1 Thessalonians 4:16). The archangel is not named, and it does not say it is Michael.",
"This is a reminder that the New Testament gives very little detail about angels. Building detailed theories about them goes beyond what Scripture says.",
"It is better to hold to what is clear: Jesus returns, the dead rise, and the church is with him forever."],
k:"The archangel in 1 Thessalonians 4:16 is unnamed. Scripture gives few details, so we should not invent them."}
],
66:[
{h:"Olive trees and lampstands",
s:"The witnesses’ title combines two Old Testament images.",
t:["“These are the two olive trees and the two lampstands that stand before the Lord of the earth” (Revelation 11:4). Zechariah 4 describes a lampstand fed by two olive trees, standing for Joshua the priest and Zerubbabel the king, the “two anointed ones” (4:14). The message was, “Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit” (4:6).",
"Revelation 1:20 says the lampstands are churches, and 1:6 and 5:10 call believers a kingdom and priests. So the witnesses may picture the church’s royal and priestly witness, powered by the Spirit. Others see them as two individuals.",
"Either way, the source of power is God’s Spirit, not human strength."],
k:"The witnesses are Spirit-powered, drawing on Zechariah’s olive trees and lampstands."},
{h:"Sackcloth and 1,260 days",
s:"The witnesses’ clothing and timing both carry meaning.",
t:["They prophesy for 1,260 days wearing sackcloth (Revelation 11:3). That is the same period as the 42 months when the nations trample the holy city (11:2) and the woman’s time in the wilderness (12:6).",
"Sackcloth is the clothing of mourning and repentance (Jonah 3:5–8; Matthew 11:21). The witnesses’ message is a call to repent, delivered in humility and not in triumph.",
"This means the church’s witness takes place in the same period as its suffering. It speaks while it is under pressure."],
k:"The witnesses speak in sackcloth, calling for repentance during the same period the nations trample the city."},
{h:"Elijah and Moses",
s:"The witnesses’ powers recall the two greatest figures of the Old Testament.",
t:["Elijah shut the sky for three and a half years (1 Kings 17:1; Luke 4:25; James 5:17) and called down fire (2 Kings 1:10). Moses turned water to blood and struck Egypt with plagues (Exodus 7–11). Malachi 4:4–6 closes the Old Testament by mentioning both. At the transfiguration, Moses and Elijah appear with Jesus (Matthew 17:1–4).",
"Together they stand for “the Law and the Prophets.” Some think the witnesses will be Moses and Elijah themselves. Others take the picture as the church carrying the same witness.",
"Either way, their testimony is rooted in the whole Old Testament."],
k:"The witnesses’ powers echo Elijah and Moses, the Law and the Prophets."},
{h:"Killed in the great city",
s:"The witnesses’ death shows what the world does with faithful testimony.",
t:["The beast from the abyss kills them (Revelation 11:7), and their bodies lie unburied in the street of the great city, “which is spiritually called Sodom and Egypt, where their Lord was crucified” (11:8). Isaiah 1:9–10 called Jerusalem’s leaders Sodom. Readers take the city as Jerusalem or as the whole world system that crucifies Christ in his people.",
"Leaving bodies unburied was a deep shame (Psalm 79:2–3). The world celebrates and sends gifts to one another because the prophets “had been a torment” to them (11:10; Esther 9:22).",
"Faithful witness often meets rejection, and the world’s joy is short."],
k:"The world celebrates the witnesses’ death, but its joy is short-lived."},
{h:"Raised and vindicated",
s:"After three and a half days the witnesses stand up, and the world’s reaction changes.",
t:["“But after the three and a half days a breath of life from God entered them, and they stood up on their feet, and great fear fell on those who saw them” (Revelation 11:11), echoing Ezekiel 37:5, 10. A voice says, “Come up here!”, as in 4:1, and they go up in a cloud (11:12).",
"A great earthquake follows, a tenth of the city falls and 7,000 die. “And the rest were terrified and gave glory to the God of heaven” (11:13). Earlier, people refused to give glory (9:20; 16:9). Here they do.",
"Readers disagree whether this is genuine repentance or terrified acknowledgment, but it contrasts with the stubbornness found elsewhere."],
k:"The witnesses are raised and taken up, and the survivors give glory to God."},
{h:"A courtroom scene",
s:"Two witnesses were needed for legal testimony, and Revelation is full of courtroom language.",
t:["Deuteronomy 19:15 says, “on the evidence of two witnesses or of three witnesses shall a charge be established.” The two witnesses mean their testimony stands.",
"The Greek word for witness, martys, gives us the word “martyr.” Revelation uses “testimony” repeatedly (1:2, 9; 6:9; 12:11, 17; 19:10; 20:4). The world is on trial, and the church gives evidence.",
"The scene also reassures: even if witnesses are killed, their testimony stands and will be vindicated at the final judgment (20:11–15)."],
k:"Two witnesses make testimony valid. Revelation treats the church’s witness as evidence in a cosmic trial."}
],
67:[
{h:"The four faces",
s:"The four living creatures were first seen by Ezekiel.",
t:["In Revelation 4:6–8, the creatures are like a lion, an ox, a man and an eagle, each full of eyes and with six wings. Ezekiel 1:10 describes faces of a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle. They never stop saying, “Holy, holy, holy” (Isaiah 6:3).",
"The lion is the noblest of wild animals, the ox of domestic ones, the eagle of birds and the man of all creatures. Some early church writers later linked them to the four Gospels, a tradition that goes beyond what the text says.",
"The creatures stand for the whole created world praising its Maker."],
k:"The creatures’ faces represent the noblest of creation, praising God without ceasing."},
{h:"Cherubim and seraphim combined",
s:"John merges two Old Testament visions into one.",
t:["Ezekiel 10:20 identifies Ezekiel’s creatures as cherubim. Isaiah 6 describes seraphim with six wings who cry “Holy.” Revelation’s creatures have Ezekiel’s eyes and four faces and Isaiah’s six wings and song.",
"Cherubim guarded Eden after the fall (Genesis 3:24) and were carved over the ark of the covenant (Exodus 25:18–22). They stand for God’s holiness and his closeness.",
"They show that Revelation’s throne room is the same throne room Isaiah and Ezekiel saw, now with the Lamb at its center."],
k:"The creatures combine Ezekiel’s cherubim and Isaiah’s seraphim: one throne room, seen again."},
{h:"They call the riders",
s:"The creatures take an active part in the events of the book.",
t:["Each of the first four seals is opened with a creature saying “Come!” (Revelation 6:1, 3, 5, 7). A creature also gives the seven bowls to the seven angels (15:7), and they say “Amen” to the worship of the Lamb (5:14).",
"Creation itself takes part in both judgment and worship. The creatures are close to the throne and respond when God acts.",
"They remind us that nature is not neutral. It is a servant of its Creator."],
k:"The living creatures call the riders and say “Amen.” Creation participates in God’s work."},
{h:"What the elders do",
s:"Every action of the elders has meaning.",
t:["They wear white (victory and purity), sit on thrones (rule), wear golden crowns (the victor’s wreath) and cast them before the throne (Revelation 4:4, 10). They hold harps and golden bowls of incense, “which are the prayers of the saints” (5:8). They fall down in worship repeatedly (5:8, 14; 7:11; 11:16; 19:4).",
"They are both royal and priestly, like the people of God in 1:6 and 5:10.",
"The crown-throwing is the most striking act. Whatever honor they have is given back to the one who gave it."],
k:"The elders reign, pray and worship, and they give their crowns back to God."},
{h:"Helpers for John",
s:"The elders are not silent props. They teach John.",
t:["One elder says, “Weep no more” in 5:5 and later asks, “Who are these, clothed in white robes, and from where have they come?” John replies, “Sir, you know,” and the elder explains (7:13–14).",
"They are real participants, interpreting the vision and comforting the visionary.",
"John models a good way to learn: he asks honest questions and admits what he doesn’t know. It is a good model for anyone studying Revelation."],
k:"The elders explain and comfort John. His humble questions are a good model for learners."},
{h:"Why twenty-four?",
s:"The number has more than one possible background, and they may all be intended.",
t:["First Chronicles 24:1–19 lists twenty-four priestly divisions in Israel, and chapter 25 lists twenty-four groups of singers. Revelation 21:12–14 names twelve tribes and twelve apostles, giving twenty-four again.",
"Some readers see the elders as heavenly beings, others as representing all of God’s people, old and new covenants together. The identity is debated.",
"The picture is clear even if the identity isn’t: priestly, royal worship surrounds the throne, and God’s people are part of it."],
k:"Twenty-four echoes priestly divisions and the twelve tribes plus twelve apostles. The elders picture worship and rule together."}
],
68:[
{h:"The pale horse",
s:"The fourth horse brings death, and Hades follows it.",
t:["“And behold, a pale horse! And its rider’s name was Death, and Hades followed him” (Revelation 6:8). The Greek word chloros means a sickly green, like the color of a corpse; it gives us “chlorine.” Death and Hades receive authority over a fourth of the earth, “to kill with sword and with famine and with pestilence and by wild beasts,” the same four judgments as in Ezekiel 14:21.",
"Death takes people, and Hades gathers them. Hosea 13:14 asks, “O Death, where are your plagues? O Sheol, where is your sting?”",
"The picture is grim, but notice the limit: a fourth, not all."],
k:"The pale horse’s rider is Death, followed by Hades. Their authority is limited."},
{h:"The keys on loan",
s:"Death and Hades have power, but Jesus holds the keys.",
t:["“I have the keys of Death and Hades” (Revelation 1:18). Their power is on loan and limited. Jesus said, “I am the resurrection and the life” (John 11:25).",
"Hebrews 2:14–15 says Jesus shared our humanity so that “through death he might destroy the one who has the power of death, that is, the devil, and deliver all those who through fear of death were subject to lifelong slavery.”",
"For believers, death has lost its terror because the one who has passed through it holds the keys."],
k:"Death and Hades have limited authority, because Jesus holds their keys."},
{h:"Hades is not the lake of fire",
s:"It is easy to confuse the two, and Revelation keeps them separate.",
t:["Hades is the realm of the dead, the Greek counterpart to Sheol. In Revelation 20:13, it “gave up the dead who were in it,” and then it is thrown into the lake of fire in 20:14. The lake of fire is the “second death.”",
"This means Hades is temporary: a holding place that is emptied at the final judgment. The lake of fire is final. In many English Bibles “hell” translates different words, so check which word is used.",
"See Day 50 for the intermediate state and different words for the dead."],
k:"Hades is the temporary realm of the dead. The lake of fire is the final judgment."},
{h:"Abaddon and Apollyon",
s:"The king of the locusts has a name in two languages.",
t:["“They have as king over them the angel of the bottomless pit. His name in Hebrew is Abaddon, and in Greek he is called Apollyon” (Revelation 9:11). Abaddon means “destruction” and appears in Job 26:6, 28:22 and Proverbs 15:11 alongside Death and Sheol. Apollyon means “destroyer.”",
"Some suggest the Greek name is a play on Apollo, whose symbol was the locust and whom Domitian associated himself with, though that is uncertain.",
"The name shows destruction personified. Yet the angel of the abyss serves under God’s limits: the key was “given” (9:1)."],
k:"Abaddon and Apollyon both mean “destroyer.” He rules the abyss only as far as God allows."},
{h:"The last enemy",
s:"Death is treated like a criminal: arrested, tried and executed.",
t:["Paul says, “The last enemy to be destroyed is death” (1 Corinthians 15:26). Isaiah 25:8 says God “will swallow up death forever.” Paul combines Isaiah and Hosea: “Death is swallowed up in victory. O death, where is your victory?” (1 Corinthians 15:54–55).",
"Revelation completes the thought. Death and Hades are thrown into the lake of fire (20:14), and “death shall be no more” (21:4).",
"For those who have lost loved ones, this is among the most personal promises in the book."],
k:"Death is the last enemy, and Revelation says it will be no more."},
{h:"Keys, defeat, removal",
s:"The book gives death’s story in three steps.",
t:["First, the keys: Jesus has authority over death now (Revelation 1:18). Second, the defeat: his resurrection broke death’s hold. Third, the removal: at the end, death itself is destroyed (20:14; 21:4).",
"We live between the second and third steps. Death is defeated but still present. That is the “already and not yet” of Day 5.",
"Paul put it this way: “For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain” (Philippians 1:21). He could say that because of the keys."],
k:"Death’s story is keys, defeat and removal. We live between the defeat and the removal."}
],
69:[
{h:"Who was John?",
s:"John names himself, and readers have asked who he was for centuries.",
t:["He names himself four times (Revelation 1:1, 4, 9; 22:8) and calls himself a “servant,” a “brother” and a partner in “the tribulation and the kingdom and the patient endurance” (1:9). He never uses the title “apostle.”",
"Justin Martyr and Irenaeus identified him as John the apostle. Others, from Dionysius of Alexandria onward, noted differences in style from the Gospel of John and suggested another John. Papias spoke of a “John the elder,” according to Eusebius.",
"Whoever he was, the book presents itself as the word of Jesus, given through a faithful servant."],
k:"John calls himself a servant and brother. Many identify him as the apostle, and some suggest another John."},
{h:"“Write”",
s:"John is a recorder, not an inventor.",
t:["He is on Patmos “on account of the word of God and the testimony of Jesus,” and is told to write: “Write what you see in a book” (Revelation 1:9–11, 19). The command returns in 14:13 and 21:5.",
"This matters because the book does not present itself as John’s own ideas. It is something shown and told to him, and he passes it on.",
"His situation, exiled for his faith, is the same as the readers who first needed to hear it."],
k:"John writes what he is shown and told. The message is Jesus’, delivered through a faithful messenger."},
{h:"A learner first",
s:"John’s honest reactions are one of the most reassuring parts of the book.",
t:["When no one can open the scroll, John weeps (Revelation 5:4). Twice he falls to worship an angel and is corrected (19:10; 22:8–9). When he first sees the risen Jesus, he falls “as though dead” (1:17), though he had once leaned against Jesus at the supper.",
"The book keeps the human reactions in. This shows that God uses ordinary people, with their mistakes, to pass on his message.",
"It also gives you permission to be confused, to ask questions and to get things wrong while learning."],
k:"John weeps, mistakes and falls. He was a learner before he was a teacher."},
{h:"Twelve names on the foundations",
s:"A small detail shows John’s humility.",
t:["The new city’s twelve foundations carry “the twelve names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb” (Revelation 21:14). John, if he was an apostle, does not draw attention to himself. Paul said the church is “built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus himself being the cornerstone” (Ephesians 2:20).",
"Some read this as modest distance from any claim of special status. Others simply see it as part of the city’s symbolism.",
"Either way, the focus is not on any messenger but on the Lamb and his people."],
k:"The apostles’ names are on the foundations, and the focus is on the Lamb and his people."},
{h:"The last words",
s:"The book ends with a conversation between Jesus and his church.",
t:["Jesus says, “Surely I am coming soon,” and the church answers, “Amen. Come, Lord Jesus!” (Revelation 22:20). It is the same as the Aramaic “Maranatha” of 1 Corinthians 16:22. The last verse is, “The grace of the Lord Jesus be with all” (22:21).",
"The book began with “the revelation of Jesus Christ” (1:1) and ends with his name. Everything in between is about him.",
"Your best next step is to read Revelation straight through once more. It will look different now, and you will notice things you couldn’t see before. Come, Lord Jesus."],
k:"The book ends with Jesus’ promise, the church’s prayer, and grace. Read it again, and it will read differently."}
]
});
